window.BIO_MODEL_ANSWERS = {

// ─── AQA 7402 June 2023 Paper 1 ───────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::01.1": "The three structural features of all virus particles are: genetic material (DNA or RNA), a capsid (protein coat), and attachment proteins (glycoproteins). The capsid protects the genetic material from enzymatic digestion and the environment.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::01.2": "Viruses are described as acellular because they are not made of cells — they have no cell-surface membrane, no cytoplasm, and no organelles. They are non-living because they have no metabolism; they cannot independently respire, excrete, or replicate without a host cell.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::01.3": "Antibiotics are not effective against viruses because viruses do not carry out metabolic processes (such as cell wall synthesis or protein synthesis on 70S ribosomes) that antibiotics can disrupt.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::02.1": "Both chitin and cellulose have alternate monomers rotated/flipped by 180°, both are joined by glycosidic bonds, and both form straight, unbranched chains.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::02.2": "The tracheole walls are very thin (one cell thick), providing a short diffusion pathway for rapid gas exchange. The tracheoles branch extensively and penetrate directly into muscle fibres, reducing the diffusion distance further and providing a large surface area for diffusion.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::02.3": "The xylem being kept open as a continuous tube allows an unbroken column of water from roots to leaves with no barrier to water movement. Cohesion between water molecules (due to hydrogen bonding) maintains the water column, and the tension created by transpiration at the leaf surface pulls water upwards through the continuous tube.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::03.1": "Any membrane-bound organelle, for example a mitochondrion, rough endoplasmic reticulum, nucleus (nuclear envelope), or vesicle/lysosome.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::03.2": "The Golgi apparatus modifies and packages proteins (forming glycoproteins) and lipids (forming glycolipids). It also produces vesicles and lysosomes, transporting substances to the cell surface membrane or for secretion.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::03.3": "India, Europe, East Asia, Central America, South America.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::03.4": "229 million × 6% (cases outside Africa) × 61% = 8.38 million cases worldwide caused by P. vivax each year.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::03.5": "A mutation originally produced the allele for resistance to P. vivax malaria. Individuals carrying this allele were less likely to die from malaria and therefore more likely to survive, reproduce, and pass the allele to their offspring. Over many generations the frequency of the resistance allele increased in the African population through natural selection, because non-resistant individuals were more likely to die before reproducing.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::04.1": "The loss of microvilli reduces the surface area of the ileum lining, meaning there are fewer carrier/channel proteins for co-transport and active transport of digestion products. This reduces the absorption of glucose and amino acids. Because fewer products of digestion are absorbed into the blood, the water potential of the lumen does not decrease as much, so less water moves out of the lumen into the blood by osmosis — resulting in diarrhoea.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::04.2": "The anti-toxin antibody would bind to and neutralise the C. difficile toxin (causing agglutination or preventing it from binding to ileum cells), so the toxin cannot damage the intestinal lining. From Figure 4, patients with diarrhoea have the lowest anti-toxin antibody concentrations, so the passive immunity should be offered to patients who develop C. difficile infection and have low antibody concentrations (i.e. those who already have diarrhoea or are at risk).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::04.3": "If taken orally, the antibody (a protein) would be digested in the gut. Endopeptidases would hydrolyse the internal peptide bonds, breaking the polypeptide into shorter fragments. Exopeptidases would then remove amino acids from the terminal ends, and membrane-bound dipeptidases would hydrolyse dipeptides into individual amino acids — destroying the antibody's tertiary structure and function.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::05.1": "Aseptic techniques include: washing hands with soap and disinfecting surfaces; using a sterile pipette/syringe to transfer the bacterial culture; flaming the neck of the bottle after removing the lid; lifting the lid of the agar plate at an angle (keeping the lid over the plate); working close to an upward-moving Bunsen flame; using a sterile spreader to spread the culture; and placing the pipette/spreader in disinfectant immediately after use.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::05.2": "Volume = πr²l → l = V ÷ (πr²) = 50 ÷ (3.14 × 3²) = 50 ÷ 28.26 = 1.77 mm.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::05.3": "Positive control: a known antimicrobial/antibiotic solution (added to the well to produce a clear zone, confirming the method can detect antimicrobial activity).\nNegative control: sterile water, or oil without cinnamon (added to the well to show that any clear zone is due to the cinnamon oil, not the oil carrier or water).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::05.4": "Cinnamon oil: median = 16 mm, mean = 16.6 mm (≈17 mm). Positive control: median = 12 mm, mean = 13.4 mm (≈13 mm).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::05.5": "Cinnamon oil: mean ± 2SD = 16.6 ± 4.8 = range 11.8 to 21.4 mm. Positive control: mean ± 2SD = 13.4 ± 4.4 = range 9.0 to 17.8 mm. Because these ranges overlap, the difference in means is likely to be due to chance — there is no significant difference between the antimicrobial effectiveness of cinnamon oil and the positive control.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::06.1": "Genome: the complete set of genes/all the DNA in a cell or organism. Proteome: the full range of proteins that a cell can produce, as coded for by its genome.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::06.2": "The early classification was limited in accuracy because the optical microscope has low resolution, making it difficult to distinguish fine structural differences between species. Flagella are fragile and easily broken or missing, so their arrangement could not be reliably observed; they are also difficult to stain, making them invisible in many preparations. Flagella may extend at any angle and not be visible in a given plane of focus, and not all bacteria have flagella, further reducing reliability as a classification criterion.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::06.3": "Several bacterial species have been renamed in recent years because DNA/mRNA base sequencing (genomic analysis) or amino acid sequencing has revealed that species previously grouped together based on morphology are genetically very different, or that apparently different species are actually closely related.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::06.4": "Magnification = image size ÷ actual size. Measuring the cell in Figure 6 gives approximately 45–47 mm (depending on the printed image). Using 46 mm: magnification = 46 000 µm ÷ 2.3 µm = 20 000×.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::07.1": "Exposure to CO reduces the maximum percentage saturation of haemoglobin (Hb) with oxygen. At high pO₂ (in the lungs), Hb can only reach 50% saturation when 50% of binding sites are occupied by CO, compared to ~100% normally — so less oxygen is loaded. At low pO₂ (in respiring tissues), the CO-exposed Hb has a higher affinity for oxygen and unloads less oxygen than normal — so less oxygen is delivered to tissues.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::07.2": "The scientists' data partially supports reducing WHO limits: the model predicts that children exposed to 9.6 mg m⁻³ CO for 8 hours would reach 2.5% COHb, which is below the current WHO limit of 10 mg m⁻³ — suggesting the current limit may not be safe for children. However, the model used adults' and children's data at 1 hour, where the COHb levels (31.2 and 40.2 mg m⁻³) actually exceed 2.5% at concentrations higher than the WHO limits, so the 1-hour data does not support reducing the 1-hour limit. The model is mathematical and was not tested in real people, so its accuracy is uncertain. It also only modelled people at rest, and exercise increases ventilation rate, which would raise COHb more quickly. Individual variation (age, sex, body size, respiratory conditions) means a single limit may not protect all people equally, and there was no statistical test to confirm significance of the differences.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::08.1": "The cells in drawing A (treated with MiTMAB) can be identified because they contain two nuclei — MiTMAB inhibits cytokinesis, so although nuclear division (mitosis) has occurred, the cytoplasm has not divided, leaving both nuclei in one large cell with no cell membrane forming between them.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::08.2": "MiTMAB is a non-competitive inhibitor, so it binds to dynamin at a site other than the active site (the allosteric/inhibitor site). This changes the tertiary structure of dynamin, altering the shape of its active site so that the substrate is no longer complementary to it. As a result, fewer enzyme–substrate complexes form and dynamin becomes inactive.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::08.3": "At the lowest concentrations of MiTMAB, dynamin is only slightly inhibited, so cytokinesis and cell division continue largely as normal — cancer cell numbers increase similarly to the control. As concentration increases, more dynamin is inhibited, reducing cytokinesis and increasing cell death, so cell numbers fall progressively below the control. At the highest concentrations, all dynamin is inhibited: cytokinesis is completely blocked and cell death is maximised, so fewer cells remain than were present at the start (negative proportion of control growth), as MiTMAB also inhibits the cell-survival role of dynamin.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::08.4": "From Figure 9, the concentration at which growth equals the control is approximately 30–70 nM, and the concentration giving 0.0 of control growth is approximately 2000 nM. Increase in concentration = 2000 − 30 = 1970 nM (accept 1930–1970).\nMass added = 1970 × 10⁻⁹ mol dm⁻³ × 0.01 dm³ = 1.97 × 10⁻⁸ mol. Assuming molecular mass of MiTMAB ≈ 1 000 g mol⁻¹ (as given from figure), the increase in mass ≈ 1970 nM × 0.01 dm³ = 19.7 µg (accept 19.3–19.7 µg).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::09.1": "At line T, there is an antigen specific to the dengue virus attached to the membrane. In a positive test, the patient's blood contains anti-dengue antibodies, which bind to the enzyme-labelled anti-human antibodies. This complex flows up the test strip and the dengue antigens at line T bind to the anti-dengue antibodies — enzyme converts the substrate into a coloured product, producing a visible line.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::09.2": "A line at C appears when anti-human antibody (with enzyme attached) has successfully moved through the strip and reached the control region — this shows the fluid has flowed correctly through the test, confirming the test has worked regardless of the result at T.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::09.3": "The fused hybridoma cells allow continuous production of monoclonal antibodies because the cancer cells (myeloma cells) divide rapidly and uncontrollably (providing immortality), while the B cells (plasma cells) produce the specific antibody — the fusion combines both properties, allowing indefinite antibody production.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::09.4": "An ethical argument for the process is that it produces monoclonal antibodies that can diagnose or treat disease, reducing human suffering. An argument against is that animals are used and may be harmed — mice are injected with the antigen and tumour cells, which may cause them distress or illness.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::09.5": "The data (Table 3) shows the new ELISA test detects more cases than the current clinic test at all time points, especially early (days 1–2: new test 6/14 vs current test 0/14). However, it detects fewer cases than the lab-based test early on (days 1–2: 6 vs 10). From days 5–7, the new and current tests perform equally (14/18 each), matching the lab test. Arguments for recommending the new test worldwide include that it is likely quicker and cheaper than the lab-based test, and doesn't require laboratory infrastructure — important in countries with limited healthcare facilities. However, the new test is less sensitive early in infection than the lab-based test, so some cases would be missed. Training may also be needed, and one study on one disease may not justify global implementation.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::09.6": "Blood proteins moving into the tissue fluid lower its water potential (increase solute concentration). This reduces the water potential gradient between the tissue fluid and the blood capillaries, so less water returns to the capillaries by osmosis — causing fluid to accumulate in the tissues (oedema).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::10.1": "Amino acids are joined by condensation reactions, forming peptide bonds between the amino group of one amino acid and the carboxyl group of another, producing a polypeptide chain (primary structure). Hydrogen bonding between amino acids in the chain produces secondary structures such as α-helices and β-pleated sheets. Interactions between R groups (including hydrogen bonds, disulfide bonds, ionic bonds, and hydrophobic interactions) cause the polypeptide to fold into its specific tertiary structure. Quaternary structure forms when two or more polypeptide subunits associate through interactions between their R groups, producing a functional multi-subunit protein.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::10.2": "DNA is a double-stranded polymer of nucleotides (polynucleotide), with each nucleotide consisting of deoxyribose, a phosphate group, and one of four organic bases (adenine, thymine, cytosine, or guanine). Adjacent nucleotides are joined by phosphodiester bonds between the sugar and phosphate groups. The two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs (adenine–thymine and cytosine–guanine), forming a double helix. DNA is associated with histone proteins around which it is wound. A chromosome consists of a highly coiled and condensed DNA-protein complex; when visible (during cell division), a chromosome appears as two chromatids joined at a centromere.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p1::10.3": "Other processes that increase genetic variation within a species include: independent segregation (assortment) of homologous chromosomes during meiosis I, meaning each gamete receives a random combination of maternal and paternal chromosomes; crossing over between homologous chromosomes during prophase I of meiosis, creating new combinations of alleles on each chromosome; and random fertilisation, where any male gamete can fuse with any female gamete, producing new combinations of alleles in the zygote.",

// ─── AQA 7402 June 2023 Paper 2 ───────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::01.1": "Draw a pencil line (origin) across the chromatography paper using a ruler. Use a pipette/dropper to apply a small spot of the chlorophyll solution to the origin. Lower the paper into a container of solvent A so that the solvent level is below the origin line. Allow the solvent to travel up the paper until it is near the top, then remove the paper and mark the solvent front. The pigments separate as they travel different distances due to their different solubilities in the solvent.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::01.2": "The sixth pigment was not separated using solvent A because two pigments had the same (or very similar) solubility in solvent A and travelled the same distance, appearing as one band. Solvent B has different solubilising properties, so these two pigments have different solubilities in it and different Rf values — they travel different distances and appear as separate bands.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::02.1": "Ziconotide blocks calcium ion channels at synapses in the pain pathway. Normally, when a nerve impulse arrives at the presynaptic knob, calcium ions enter through voltage-gated channels and cause synaptic vesicles to fuse with the presynaptic membrane, releasing glutamate. With ziconotide blocking these channels, no calcium ions enter, so no vesicles fuse and no glutamate is released into the synaptic cleft. No glutamate means no glutamate binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, so no sodium ions enter the postsynaptic neurone — no action potential is generated and no impulse is transmitted to the brain, reducing the sensation of pain.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::02.2": "If taken as a pill, ziconotide (a polypeptide) would be broken down by proteases in the digestive system — the peptide bonds would be hydrolysed and the drug would be destroyed before reaching the spinal cord. Injecting it directly into the cerebrospinal fluid allows it to reach the spinal cord synapses rapidly and directly, without being digested.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::02.3": "Mass = 3 ng kg⁻¹ h⁻¹ × 82 kg × 24 h × 8 days = 47 232 ng = 4.72 × 10⁻⁵ g.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::02.4": "Pain is subjective — its intensity varies between individuals due to differences in pain sensitivity, threshold, and tolerance. Using a statistically valid scale ensures that differences in recorded pain levels between patients are meaningful and not simply due to inconsistency in measurement, allowing valid comparisons to be made and enabling statistical testing to determine whether changes are significant.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::03.1": "Store 1 (marine sediments): 840 000 Tg × 1% = 8 400 Tg supplied to soil.\nStore 2 (rocks/soil): 200 000 Tg × 0.005% = 10 Tg supplied.\nDifference = 8 400 − 10 = 8 390 Tg ≈ 8 390 Tg (accept 12 Tg for an alternative reading of the stores).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::03.2": "The data shows that mycorrhizae significantly increase tomato production under water shortage conditions: treated plants (Q) produced more than untreated plants (P), with no overlap in ± 2 SD error bars, indicating a statistically significant difference. Under no water shortage, there was no significant difference between treated (S) and untreated (R) plants (error bars overlapped), suggesting mycorrhizae are not beneficial when water is abundant. However, the study only used one mycorrhizal species (G. intraradices) and one crop (tomatoes), in sterile soil in a greenhouse — results may not apply to other crops, soils, or field conditions. The large sample sizes increase reliability, but further investigation in real field conditions with different crops would be needed before recommending widespread use.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::03.3": "The soil was sterilised to kill any other mycorrhizal fungi or bacteria (including nitrogen-fixing, nitrifying, and saprobiotic bacteria) and any pathogens, pests, or weed seeds already present in the soil. This ensures that any effect on plant growth is due solely to the addition of G. intraradices, not to other soil organisms — keeping it as a controlled variable.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::03.4": "The recommended concentration of fertiliser was used so that nutrient availability (a controlled variable) was the same in all pots — ensuring it is not a confounding variable affecting plant growth. This also reflects realistic farming conditions, allowing the results to be applied practically. Fertiliser affects water potential of the soil, which could otherwise influence the effect of mycorrhizae on water uptake.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::04.1": "Epistasis.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::04.2": "Tortoiseshell female (the F allele is not present — genotype is ff — so pigment is produced; the codominant X^G and X^B alleles are both expressed, producing ginger and black patches).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::04.3": "Parental gametes: X^G F, X^G f (from X^G X^G Ff female) and X^B f, Y f (from X^B Y ff male).\nOffspring genotypes: X^G X^B Ff (white female), X^G X^B ff (tortoiseshell female), X^G Y Ff (white male), X^G Y ff (ginger male).\nRatio: 1 white female : 1 tortoiseshell female : 1 white male : 1 ginger male.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::04.4": "White fur = F_ = 36% of population.\nSo ff = q² = 1 − 0.36 = 0.64, therefore q (frequency of f allele) = √0.64 = 0.8.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::05.1": "Colour vision involves cone cells. Each type of cone contains a different photosensitive pigment that absorbs maximally at a particular range of wavelengths. At 600 nm (orange), the red-sensitive cones absorb more light than the green-sensitive cones. This produces more action potentials along the optic nerve from red-sensitive cones than from green-sensitive cones — the brain interprets this relative stimulation as the colour orange.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::05.2": "Several photoreceptors connecting to one neurone and spatial summation.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::05.3": "The two species are found at different altitudes (lewisii at 1600–3000 m, cardinalis up to 2000 m), which likely caused geographical/allopatric isolation initially. In their separate environments they experienced different selection pressures — different pollinators (bumblebees vs hummingbirds) favouring different flower colours through natural selection. Mutations provided variation in flower colour; individuals with flower colours that attracted their local pollinators were more likely to be pollinated and reproduce, passing on those alleles. Over time, allele frequencies changed in each population. Because the species overlap in the 1600–2000 m zone, some sympatric speciation driven by reproductive isolation via pollinator preference may also have occurred. Eventually, the two populations became reproductively isolated and could no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring — forming two distinct species.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::06.1": "Three features of proximal convoluted tubule cells that allow rapid reabsorption of glucose: (1) microvilli/folded membrane on the lumen surface — provides a very large surface area for co-transport proteins; (2) many mitochondria — produce ATP for the active transport of sodium ions out of the cell (maintaining the sodium gradient that drives co-transport of glucose); (3) many carrier/channel proteins in the membrane — facilitate the movement of glucose by co-transport and facilitated diffusion.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::06.2": "Collecting duct and distal convoluted tubule.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::06.3": "V receptors only bind with ADH because the receptor has a specific tertiary structure (shape) that is complementary only to the ADH molecule. No other molecule has the same shape as ADH and can therefore fit into and bind to the receptor.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::06.4": "The receptors that detect a decrease in blood pressure are found in the aorta and carotid artery/sinus (baroreceptors). When blood pressure falls, these receptors send signals to the hypothalamus/posterior pituitary, which releases more ADH. ADH travels in the blood to the collecting duct and distal convoluted tubule, where it increases the permeability of the walls to water. More water is reabsorbed from the filtrate by osmosis into the blood, increasing blood volume and therefore blood pressure back to normal.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::07.1": "(1) carrying / (2) niche / (3) abiotic / (4) community.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::07.2": "Rainforests should be conserved to maintain biodiversity — they contain a vast number of species and habitats/niches. They also play a critical role in reducing climate change by absorbing CO₂ and producing oxygen. Additionally, they are a source of medicines and drugs, and deforestation leads to soil erosion and loss of indigenous communities' ways of life.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::07.3": "Energy is lost between secondary and tertiary consumers because: (1) energy is lost as heat during cellular respiration in the secondary consumers; (2) not all of the secondary consumer is eaten — parts such as bones, fur, or scales are not consumed; (3) some energy is lost in excretion products such as urea in urine.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::08.1": "The KRAS gene normally codes for the K-Ras protein, which relays signals stimulating cell division. A mutation (change in the DNA base sequence) in the KRAS gene produces an altered K-Ras protein (oncogene product) that is permanently active, continuously sending signals for cell division even in the absence of growth factors. This causes rapid, uncontrolled cell division (mitosis), leading to the formation of a tumour.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::08.2": "The data shows that type II diabetes alone (Group A: 21.3 months) reduces survival time compared to patients without type II diabetes (32.2 months) — this difference is statistically significant (P = 0.007). Metformin treatment (Group B: 49.7 months) significantly increases survival beyond even the non-diabetic control (P = 0.022), suggesting metformin has an anti-cancer effect beyond just treating diabetes. The combination therapy (Group C: 38.3 months) improves survival compared to Group A, but the difference is not statistically significant vs the non-diabetic control (P = 0.636), suggesting the other drugs in the combination reduce the effectiveness of metformin. Overall, metformin alone appears to be the most beneficial drug treatment for CRC patients with type II diabetes.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::09.1": "STRs could be removed from DNA using restriction endonucleases (restriction enzymes), which cut DNA at specific recognition/palindromic base sequences on either side of the STR, releasing the STR fragment from the rest of the DNA.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::09.2": "Knowing the base sequences flanking the STR allows primers to be designed that are complementary to those sequences. The primers anneal to the flanking sequences on each strand of the denatured DNA, providing a starting point for DNA polymerase (Taq polymerase) — ensuring that only the specific STR region is amplified, not the whole genome.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::09.3": "After 50 cycles, there are 2⁵⁰ copies of the STR. Each STR is 12 base pairs.\nTotal base pairs = 12 × 2⁵⁰ = 12 × 1.13 × 10¹⁵ = 1.35 × 10¹⁶ base pairs.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::09.4": "STRs differ in the number of nucleotide repeats and therefore differ in length (number of base pairs) and mass. They also carry a negative charge (from the phosphate groups in the sugar-phosphate backbone), which allows them to migrate towards the positive electrode in gel electrophoresis. Shorter STRs migrate further; this difference in migration distance allows separation.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::10.1": "ATP is required in muscle contraction to: (1) break the actinomyosin cross-bridges (ATP binds to the myosin head, causing it to detach from actin); and (2) move/cock the myosin head back into position (ATP hydrolysis provides the energy for the power stroke, moving actin filaments inwards). ATP is also used for the active transport of calcium ions back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::10.2": "ATP concentration = 5 × 10⁻³ mmol g⁻¹ = 5 mmol kg⁻¹. Usage rate = 3.7 mmol kg⁻¹ s⁻¹. Time = 5 ÷ 3.7 = 1.35 seconds.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::10.3": "Fast-twitch muscle fibres are used during short-term, intense exercise (e.g. sprinting), while slow-twitch fibres are used during longer-term, endurance exercise. Creatine supplements help fast-twitch fibre performance: creatine forms phosphocreatine in the muscle, which rapidly donates a phosphate group to ADP to regenerate ATP without oxygen — useful during the first few seconds of intense exercise before aerobic or anaerobic respiration fully activates. Carbohydrate loading benefits slow-twitch fibre performance during endurance exercise: extra dietary carbohydrate is stored as glycogen in muscle via glycogenesis; during prolonged exercise, glycogen is hydrolysed to glucose (glycogenolysis), which is then used in aerobic respiration, delaying fatigue by maintaining ATP supply.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::10.4": "GLUT4 proteins in the cell-surface membrane allow more glucose to enter muscle cells by facilitated diffusion during exercise, providing more substrate for respiration and ATP production. CD36 in the mitochondrial membrane allows fatty acids to enter the mitochondria, where they can be used as substrate in the Krebs cycle for aerobic respiration — producing more ATP to sustain muscle contraction.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p2::10.5": "Increased muscle activity raises CO₂ production, which is detected by chemoreceptors (in the aorta, carotid bodies, and medulla). These chemoreceptors send more frequent impulses to the cardiac centre (cardiovascular centre) in the medulla oblongata. The cardiac centre increases the frequency of impulses along the sympathetic nerve to the sinoatrial node (SAN). This causes the SAN to generate action potentials more rapidly, increasing the frequency of cardiac contractions — raising the heart rate.",

// ─── AQA 7402 June 2023 Paper 3 ───────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::01.1": "A Pacinian corpuscle contains stretch-mediated sodium ion channels in the membrane of the sensory neurone. When pressure is applied, the lamellae of the corpuscle are deformed, stretching the membrane of the sensory neurone and opening the sodium ion channels. Sodium ions diffuse into the neurone down their concentration gradient, depolarising the membrane and generating a generator potential.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::01.2": "The all-or-nothing principle means that an action potential is only generated if the stimulus reaches the threshold membrane potential. From Figure 1, the low pressure stimulus did not reach the threshold, so no action potential was produced. The high pressure stimulus exceeded the threshold, producing a full-sized action potential — the size of the action potential is always the same once threshold is reached (all-or-nothing).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::01.3": "The refractory period.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::02.1": "Any two from: concentration/mass of yeast; concentration/volume of sugar solution; pH; temperature; volume of yeast; species of yeast; volume of sugar solution.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::02.2": "From Figure 3, the bubble column moved 37 mm in 25 minutes (1500 seconds).\nRate = 37 ÷ 1500 = 0.0247 mm s⁻¹ = 2.5 × 10⁻² mm s⁻¹.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::02.3": "Maltose is a disaccharide, so it must first be hydrolysed by maltase into two glucose monomers before the yeast can use it in respiration. This takes additional time compared to glucose (which can be used directly), resulting in a slower initial rate of CO₂ production.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::02.4": "The second student's method is less accurate because the colour change from blue to yellow is subjective — different observers may judge the endpoint differently. Accuracy could be improved by using a colorimeter to measure the absorbance or transmission of the solution at set time intervals, recording the objective absorbance value at which the colour change occurs as the endpoint.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::02.5": "A: pyruvate; B: CO₂; C: reduced NAD (NADH); D: acetyl coenzyme A (acetyl CoA).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::03.1": "B — statements 1, 2 and 4.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::03.2": "Mean colony count for novobiocin = (238 + 263 + 261) ÷ 3 = 254 colonies per 0.25 cm³ plate.\nBacteria per cm³ = 254 ÷ 0.25 = 1016 per cm³ of diluted sample.\nUndiluted = 1016 × 10⁵ ÷ 9 (to account for 9 cm³ original volume... wait, total = 1016 × 10⁵ per cm³ × 9 cm³ = 9.144 × 10⁸ bacteria total).\nAnswer: 9.14 × 10⁸ bacteria in the undiluted bottle.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::03.3": "Starting with 1 bacterium: 2ˣ ≥ 9.14 × 10⁸. Taking log₂: x = log(9.14 × 10⁸) ÷ log 2 = 8.961 ÷ 0.301 = 29.77. So 30 generations are required.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::03.4": "Two possible reasons: (1) the concentration of chloramphenicol used was too low/dilute to kill all the bacteria; (2) during incubation a mutation occurred that conferred resistance to chloramphenicol, allowing some bacteria to survive and grow.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::04.1": "The vesicle containing SCFR fuses with a lysosome. The lysosome contains hydrolytic enzymes (lysosomal proteases/lysozymes), which hydrolyse/digest the SCFR protein, destroying it.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::04.2": "Control group: after a heart attack, cardiomyocytes in the infarcted tissue die and cannot be replaced (no transplant), so the number of functional cardiomyocytes is reduced. However, not all cardiomyocytes become infarcted, so ventricular blood pressure is low but not zero. c-KIT− group: the transplanted stem cells lack a functioning c-KIT gene and therefore cannot produce SCFR, so SCF cannot bind and activate tyrosine kinase in the normal way. However, ventricular pressure is slightly higher than the control, suggesting some differentiation into cardiomyocytes occurred via an alternative pathway — c-KIT is not the only gene responsible for differentiation; TK may be activated by other means. The increase is less than in the c-KIT+ group because the alternative pathway is less efficient.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::04.3": "First subtract the control to find the increase due to transplantation: c-KIT+ increase ≈ (reading from Figure 6) vs control. Using the proportionality: if 68% of infarcted tissue became new cardiomyocytes in c-KIT+ giving a certain pressure increase, and c-KIT− gives half that increase, then c-KIT− percentage ≈ (c-KIT− rise ÷ c-KIT+ rise) × 68% = approximately 34%.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::04.4": "Connexin-43 is a channel protein that allows electrical impulses to pass between cardiomyocytes — the new cardiomyocytes can coordinate their contractions with surrounding heart muscle, improving ventricular contraction and raising blood pressure. GATA-4 stimulates expression of the genes for actin and myosin — more contractile proteins are produced in the new cardiomyocytes, allowing them to contract more forcefully and contribute to the increased ventricular blood pressure seen in the c-KIT+ group.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::05.1": "From Figure 8: d = N(N−1) ÷ Σn(n−1) = 624 × 623 ÷ 64 792 = 388 752 ÷ 64 792 ≈ 6.0. This value of d corresponds to the years 1982 and 2003 (reading from Figure 8).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::05.2": "In 1997 there were the most species (highest species richness = 48 species), but d was lowest. This is possible if one or a very few species were present in overwhelmingly large numbers while all other species were present in very small numbers — the dominance of one species reduces d even if species richness is high, because d accounts for the relative abundance (evenness) of each species.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::05.3": "The journalist's conclusion may not be valid for several reasons: species richness and the index of diversity are both lower after 29 years than at the start, suggesting a long-term negative effect of heated water on the community. The data is only from one measurement site, so it may not be representative of all fish communities in the river. Measurements were only taken in October, so seasonal variation in the fish community is unknown. The volume, temperature, and timing of heated water release may have varied over the 29 years, making it hard to attribute changes specifically to the power station. Fluctuations during the study suggest other factors (weather, prey availability, fishing pressure) also influenced the community. There is no data from before the power station opened or from a control river without heated water, making it impossible to be certain the power station is responsible.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::06.1": "Two types of cell that can stimulate an immune response: antigen-presenting cells (e.g. macrophages) and cells infected with a virus (or cancer cells/abnormal body cells).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::06.2": "Olive oil was applied to the left ear as a control, to show that any immune response (swelling) measured in the right ear is caused specifically by OXA and not by the olive oil vehicle/carrier itself.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::06.3": "A dual-bar chart should be drawn with: mean increase in ear thickness on the y-axis (with appropriate linear scale and units); groups of mice on the x-axis; equal-width non-touching bars for healthy and autoimmune mice in each sex; error bars (± 2 SD) plotted above and below the top of each bar. [Diagram question — no written answer required in the exam; in the mark scheme, marks are for correct axes, correctly plotted bars, and correct SD bars.]",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::06.4": "Cellular response: there is no significant difference in cellular response between healthy and autoimmune female mice (SD bars overlap). In male mice, autoimmune males show a significantly greater cellular response than healthy males (SD bars do not overlap). Humoral response: autoimmune female mice show a significantly greater humoral response than healthy female mice (SD bars do not overlap). There is no significant difference in humoral response between healthy and autoimmune male mice (SD bars overlap). Overall, autoimmune disease increases the cellular response in males but increases the humoral (antibody) response in females.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::06.5": "The student's first conclusion (SLE is caused by overproduction of antibodies) is supported by the data: oestrogen increases the humoral (antibody) response in autoimmune mice, and oestrogen accelerates SLE — suggesting more antibodies drive SLE progression. The second conclusion (RA is caused by overproduction of TC cells) is partially supported: oestrogen decreases the cellular response (which involves TC cells) in autoimmune mice, and oestrogen prevents RA progression — consistent with fewer TC cells reducing RA. However, both conclusions are weakened because: the investigation used mice, not humans; the mice may not have SLE or RA specifically; a decrease in the cellular response could mean fewer antigen-presenting cells, not just fewer TC cells; and the increase in humoral response could reflect faster antibody production rather than more antibodies overall.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::06.6": "The Hardy–Weinberg principle would not hold true, because one of its conditions is that all individuals have an equal chance of reproducing (no selection). Mice with the dominant allele for autoimmune disease are likely to have reduced reproductive success (they are less healthy/more likely to die early), so the allele is subject to natural selection. This means the frequency of the allele will change over generations, violating Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium. (Alternatively: if the disease does not affect reproductive success, HWE could hold — but this is unlikely given it is a harmful trait.)",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::07.1": "A high-band model essay on 'The importance of interactions between organisms and their environment' would integrate at least four specification topics:\n\nOrganisms interact with abiotic factors such as temperature, water availability, light intensity, and pH, which act as selection pressures driving adaptation and evolution. For example, temperature affects enzyme activity and metabolic rate — organisms in cold environments often have enzymes with different tertiary structures (adapted active sites) allowing catalysis at lower temperatures. Water availability affects transpiration rate in plants; stomata close in drought conditions, reducing the water potential gradient between the leaf and atmosphere.\n\nBiotic interactions — including predation, competition, mutualism, and parasitism — shape community structure and population dynamics. Predator–prey relationships drive cyclical changes in population size and act as selection pressures favouring camouflage, speed, and chemical defence in prey, and sensory acuity in predators. Competition for resources leads to resource partitioning and niche differentiation, reducing interspecific competition.\n\nNutrient cycling depends on interactions between decomposers and the abiotic environment: saprotrophic bacteria and fungi secrete extracellular enzymes to break down dead organic matter, releasing inorganic ions (ammonium, nitrate, phosphate) back into the soil for plant uptake. This links to the nitrogen cycle, where nitrifying and nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert atmospheric nitrogen into bioavailable forms through mutualistic associations (e.g. Rhizobium in root nodules).\n\nThe immune system exemplifies the interaction between organisms: pathogens (viruses, bacteria) evolve antigens that evade host immune responses; hosts counter with specific immune responses — phagocytosis, T-cell activation, and antibody production by B cells — creating a co-evolutionary arms race. Vaccination exploits immunological memory to pre-arm the host's immune system.\n\nAt the ecosystem level, all organisms interact through food webs and energy flow. Energy enters via photosynthesis, with light as the key abiotic factor; producers fix light energy into organic molecules that pass along trophic levels. Human activities (deforestation, eutrophication, climate change) disrupt these interactions, reducing biodiversity and ecosystem stability.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2023-p3::07.2": "A high-band model essay on 'The importance of membranes in the functioning of cells' would integrate at least four specification topics:\n\nThe cell-surface membrane is a phospholipid bilayer with embedded proteins that controls the movement of substances into and out of the cell. Its fluid-mosaic structure allows selective permeability: small, non-polar molecules (O₂, CO₂) diffuse freely across the lipid bilayer, while ions and polar molecules cross via specific channel and carrier proteins (facilitated diffusion) or are actively transported against concentration gradients using ATP. This selective transport maintains the internal environment of the cell, essential for enzyme-controlled metabolic reactions.\n\nMembranes are critical in cell signalling. Receptor proteins embedded in the cell-surface membrane are complementary in shape to specific signalling molecules (hormones, neurotransmitters). For example, ADH binds to V receptors on collecting duct cells, triggering the insertion of aquaporins and increasing water reabsorption — regulating blood water potential. At synapses, neurotransmitters are released by exocytosis (fusion of vesicle membrane with presynaptic membrane) and detected by complementary receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, propagating nerve impulses.\n\nWithin cells, membrane-bound organelles compartmentalise metabolic reactions. The inner mitochondrial membrane houses the electron transport chain: as electrons pass along carrier proteins, protons are pumped into the intermembrane space, generating a proton gradient used by ATP synthase to produce ATP from ADP and Pᵢ during oxidative phosphorylation. In chloroplasts, the thylakoid membrane contains photosystems I and II; light energy is captured and used to drive the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis, splitting water and generating ATP and reduced NADP.\n\nThe nuclear envelope (a double membrane with pores) controls communication between the nucleus and cytoplasm: mRNA passes through nuclear pores to ribosomes for translation, while transcription factors enter the nucleus to regulate gene expression. The endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi apparatus form a membrane system for protein modification and vesicle-mediated transport of secretory proteins.\n\nIn cell division, membrane breakdown and reformation are essential: during mitosis, the nuclear envelope breaks down in prometaphase to allow spindle fibres to attach to chromosomes, then reforms around each daughter nucleus in telophase. Cytokinesis involves the formation of a new cell-surface membrane between daughter cells. Cell recognition relies on glycoproteins and glycolipids on the membrane surface, allowing the immune system to distinguish 'self' from 'non-self' — the basis of the immune response and the principle of tissue typing for transplantation.",

// ─── AQA 7402 June 2022 Paper 1 ───────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::01.1": "The nucleus is surrounded by a nuclear envelope — a double membrane containing nuclear pores that allow the passage of mRNA and other molecules. Inside the nucleus, DNA is associated with histone proteins, forming chromatin (chromosomes). The nucleolus is the site of ribosomal RNA production. The nucleus stores the cell's genetic information and regulates gene expression; it is the site of DNA replication and transcription (mRNA production).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::01.2": "Plant cell wall: cellulose. Fungal cell wall: chitin.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::01.3": "Individual plant organisms of some species may be too small or too numerous to count accurately, making biomass a more practical measure of the contribution of each species to the community.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::01.4": "Σn(n−1) = (2×1) + (4×3) + (5×4) + (7×6) + (15×14) + (36×35) + (51×50) = 2 + 12 + 20 + 42 + 210 + 1260 + 2550 = 4096.\nN(N−1) = 120 × 119 = 14 280.\nd = 1 − (4096 ÷ 14 280) = 1 − 0.287 = 0.713 ≈ 0.71.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::01.5": "The plant species index of diversity would be lower on land previously used for crops compared to land never used for agriculture. Farming reduces the diversity of soil fungi (Figure 1 shows that fewer fungal species are associated with lower plant diversity), and monoculture farming reduces the variety of plant species by eliminating competing species. Therefore, even after the crops stop being grown, the reduced fungal diversity in the soil means that fewer plant species can establish and thrive.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::02.1": "Some bacteria in the population carried alleles/genes for antibiotic resistance due to random mutation. Antibiotic use in hospitals acts as a selection pressure — non-resistant bacteria are killed, but resistant bacteria survive and reproduce, passing on their resistance alleles. In hospitals, antibiotics are used more frequently and patients have weakened immune systems, so resistant bacteria spread more easily — over time the frequency of the resistance allele increases in the bacterial population.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::02.2": "Maltose.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::02.3": "Aseptic techniques: wash hands and disinfect the work surface. Use a sterile pipette/syringe to withdraw 0.3 cm³ of C. difficile from the bottle — remove the bottle lid and flame the neck of the bottle before and after. Lift the lid of the agar plate at an angle (keep the lid over the plate), transfer the culture quickly. Use a sterile spreader to distribute the culture evenly. Place the pipette and spreader into disinfectant immediately after use. Work near an upward Bunsen flame throughout.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::02.4": "The data supports the hypothesis that trehalose is a factor: resistant C. difficile grows faster when trehalose is added, while non-resistant bacteria show little effect (Figure 2). If resistant bacteria outgrow non-resistant bacteria in the presence of trehalose (from the diet), they would be more likely to dominate in patients. However, the investigation was done on agar plates in a laboratory, not in real patients — conditions in the human gut are different and other dietary factors may also affect bacterial growth. There was no statistical test shown to confirm the difference is significant. Additionally, non-resistant bacteria do not grow faster with trehalose, so trehalose specifically benefits resistant strains, which supports the link — but other carbohydrates in the diet might have similar effects.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::03.1": "Two features of all prokaryotic cells that are not features of eukaryotic cells: circular/loop DNA free in the cytoplasm (no nuclear envelope); and smaller (70S) ribosomes.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::03.2": "Hydrogen bonds maintain the helical secondary structure of the polypeptide.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::03.3": "The hydrophobic side of each AP helix is attracted to and positions itself within the hydrophobic fatty acid tails of the phospholipid bilayer, anchoring the AP in the membrane. The hydrophilic sides of the helices face inward, forming a water-filled channel through which ions can pass across the membrane.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::03.4": "From Figure 5, the diameter of the channel = 1.8 nm, so radius = 0.9 nm.\nArea = π × r² = 3.14 × (0.9)² = 3.14 × 0.81 = 2.5 nm².",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::03.5": "Cholesterol is present in eukaryotic cell membranes but absent in prokaryotic membranes. Cholesterol stabilises the eukaryotic membrane by restricting the movement of phospholipids (making it less fluid/more rigid). This prevents the APs from being able to insert themselves into and form channels in eukaryotic membranes, so the APs do not damage the eukaryotic cells of the organism producing them.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::03.6": "A monoclonal antibody with complementary tertiary structure to the AP binds specifically to the APs on the prokaryote surface. Gold particles are attached to the antibody, so wherever the AP is located, gold is present. In the transmission electron microscope, gold interacts with/scatters electrons, appearing as dense spots in the TEM image. The TEM has very high resolution (below the wavelength of visible light), allowing the precise position of the APs to be visualised.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::04.1": "During viral replication, attachment proteins (glycoproteins) on the virus surface bind to complementary receptor proteins on the host cell surface. The viral nucleic acid (RNA or DNA) enters the cell. The nucleic acid is replicated using the host cell's machinery; it is also transcribed/translated to produce viral proteins (capsid proteins, enzymes, attachment proteins). New virus particles are assembled and released from the cell (by lysis or budding).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::04.2": "Mitosis: ✓ Replication of linear DNA; ✓ Produces 2 daughter cells; ✓ Happens in eukaryotic cells.\nBinary fission: ✓ Replication of circular DNA; ✓ Produces 2 daughter cells; ✓ Happens in prokaryotic cells.\n(\"Produces 4 daughter cells\" has no tick in either column.)",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::04.3": "In the second meiotic division, non-disjunction of chromosome N means both chromatids of chromosome N pass to the same cell. Two of the four daughter cells: one receives both copies of chromosome N (plus one copy of all other chromosomes); its sister cell receives no copy of chromosome N (plus one copy of all other chromosomes). The other two daughter cells (from the non-affected cell) each receive one copy of all chromosomes including one copy of chromosome N, distributed normally.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::04.4": "From Figure 7, at age 37 the number of MM1 errors ≈ 197 and MM2 errors ≈ 83. Therefore there are more MM1 errors than MM2 errors at age 37, which is the opposite of what the student concluded. The student's conclusion is not valid.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::05.1": "Quaternary structure is the structure of a protein that contains more than one polypeptide chain/subunit, held together by interactions between the R groups of amino acids in each subunit.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::05.2": "Although enzymes P and Q have different overall amino acid sequences, the amino acids at their active sites have the same (or very similar) tertiary structure. Because both active sites are complementary in shape to the same substrate molecule, both enzymes can form enzyme–substrate complexes with the same substrate and catalyse the same reaction.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::05.3": "Control tubes should contain: (1) the same volume of buffer at each pH (8.4 and 7.5); (2) the same concentration of substrate; (3) the same volume of denatured enzyme (boiled/heat-treated enzyme with no active sites) instead of active enzyme. This ensures that any change in the reaction measured in the experimental tubes is due to enzyme activity, not the substrate spontaneously reacting or other factors.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::05.4": "Three conclusions from Figure 8: (1) Both enzyme P and enzyme Q are active at pH 8.4. (2) Enzyme P is equally active at both pH 8.4 and pH 7.5 (it is not affected by the change in pH), whereas enzyme Q is less active at pH 7.5 than at pH 8.4 — Q is more sensitive to pH change. (3) Enzyme Q appears to be denatured (inactive) at pH 7.5, while the reaction still occurs in the control (without enzyme), showing the reaction can proceed slowly without enzyme.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::06.1": "Between 5 am and midday, the rate of transpiration increases progressively. As daylight increases, light intensity increases — stomata open in response, widening the diffusion pathway for water vapour to leave the leaf. Temperature also rises throughout the morning, increasing the kinetic energy of water molecules and the rate of evaporation from mesophyll cell surfaces into the air spaces. The increasing temperature and light intensity also increase the water vapour pressure gradient between the leaf interior (saturated) and the external air, driving faster transpiration.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::06.2": "From Figure 9, rate at 1 pm ≈ 2.0 mmol m⁻² h⁻¹ and at 2 pm ≈ 2.8 mmol m⁻² h⁻¹ (read from graph).\nPercentage increase = (2.8 − 2.0) ÷ 2.0 × 100 = 40%.\n(Accept values consistent with the Figure 9 readings.)",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::06.3": "Cut a length of fresh mangrove root and divide it into equal segments. Place half the segments into sea water and half into distilled water (as a reference). After a set time, measure the mass of each segment before and after. If the root cells have a lower water potential than sea water, water would move from the sea water into the root cells by osmosis — the segments in sea water would gain mass. Compare the change in mass of segments in sea water vs distilled water to confirm the root has a lower water potential than sea water. Alternatively, use the root segments as an osmometer: if plasmolysis does NOT occur when the root cells are placed in sea water, the root cells have a lower water potential.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::07.1": "Three differences between DNA and tRNA:\n1. DNA is double-stranded; tRNA is single-stranded.\n2. DNA contains deoxyribose; tRNA contains ribose.\n3. DNA contains thymine; tRNA contains uracil instead.\n(Also accept: DNA is much longer/larger; DNA does not have a cloverleaf shape; DNA contains no anticodon loop.)",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::07.2": "The scientists would remove large organelles from the cell-content suspension by differential centrifugation: centrifuge at low speed first to pellet and remove the heaviest organelles (nuclei, cell debris). Then decant the supernatant for further use — this removes large organelles while leaving ribosomes in suspension.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::07.3": "From Figure 10, the detergent dissolves lipid membranes, releasing ribosomes from the endoplasmic reticulum and other membranes into solution. On ultracentrifugation, the 80S ribosomes (larger, heavier eukaryotic ribosomes) form a band lower in tube A (they sediment further as they are denser). The 70S ribosomes (if any mitochondrial ribosomes are present) are smaller and lighter, forming a band higher in the tube. In tube B (without detergent), ribosomes remain attached to membrane fragments and sediment at a different position — the band position in each tube reflects the density/sedimentation coefficient of the ribosome type released.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::08.1": "The first stain binds to DNA. Red blood cells in fish (unlike mammals) retain their nucleus, which contains DNA — so the first stain could bind to the nuclear DNA. However, the cytoplasm of red blood cells contains haemoglobin (a protein), which does not contain DNA. A second stain is needed to stain the red blood cells themselves — it could bind to haemoglobin (a protein) in the cytoplasm of the red blood cells, making them visible.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::08.2": "Healthy gill SA:V ratio = 7.4 × 10³ ÷ 2.3 × 10⁴ = 0.32:1 (to 2 s.f.).\nDamaged gill volume: SA ÷ ratio = (1.1 × 10⁴) ÷ 0.13 = 8.5 × 10⁴ µm³.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::08.3": "Difference: the uncontrolled cell division causes thickening of the tissue around the capillaries in the gill filaments (the cells proliferate, increasing the distance between the blood in the capillaries and the water), increasing the diffusion distance. Explanation: because the diffusion distance between the water and the blood is greater, the rate of diffusion of oxygen from water into the blood (and CO₂ in the opposite direction) is reduced — gas exchange is less efficient, as diffusion rate is inversely proportional to distance (Fick's law).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p1::08.4": "Two differences between fish and mammalian blood circulation:\n1. Fish have a single circulatory system (blood passes through the heart once per circuit: heart → gills → body → heart); mammals have a double circulatory system (blood passes through the heart twice per circuit: right side → lungs → left side → body).\n2. Blood pressure drops as blood passes through the gill capillaries in fish, so blood flows more slowly to the body; in mammals, the left ventricle re-pressurises the blood after oxygenation in the lungs, maintaining higher blood pressure to the body.",


// ─── AQA 7402 June 2022 Paper 2 ───────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::01.1": "(1) organic  (2) respiration  (3) carbon  (4) calorimetry.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::01.2": "In the light-independent reaction (Calvin cycle), CO₂ combines with ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) using the enzyme Rubisco, producing two molecules of glycerate-3-phosphate (GP). GP is then reduced to triose phosphate (TP) using reduced NADP and ATP (both from the light-dependent reaction). Triose phosphate is used to regenerate RuBP (using ATP) and to synthesise glucose and other organic molecules.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::02.1": "N = I − (F + R).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::02.2": "Beef cows as a percentage of all female cattle in December 2017 = 45% (beef share of breeding herd) × 48% (breeding herd as fraction of all females) = 21.6%.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::02.3": "Dairy cows December 2013 = 54% × 3.35 million = 1 809 000.\nDairy cows December 2017 = 55% × 3.45 million = 1 897 500.\nIncrease = 1 897 500 − 1 809 000 = 88 500 dairy cows.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::02.4": "Farming cattle is less efficient than farming crops because energy is lost at each trophic level. When humans eat cattle, energy has already been lost through the cattle's respiration (heat production), excretion (urea), movement, and in faeces. These losses mean that only a fraction of the energy originally fixed by plants passes to the cattle, and only a fraction of that reaches humans — whereas eating crops directly loses only one trophic level of energy.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::03.1": "Two controlled variables (other than temperature and pH): volume of bacterial culture; concentration/volume of oxygen (or nitrogen) in the culture vessel.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::03.2": "The data supports the conclusion: nitrogenase activity decreases as ammonium chloride concentration increases (negative correlation), and activity reaches zero when ammonium chloride concentration is ≥60 µg cm⁻³. At higher concentrations, ammonium chloride remains in the medium even after growth, consistent with the bacteria no longer needing to fix nitrogen. However, the investigation used ammonium chloride, not pure ammonia — the inhibition may be due to ammonium ions rather than ammonia, or possibly chloride ions. Only one species (A. chroococcum) was studied, so the conclusion may not apply to other nitrogen-fixing bacteria. No statistical test was mentioned to confirm significance.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::03.3": "When ammonia is available from the medium, the bacteria do not need to use nitrogenase. Nitrogen fixation requires 16 ATP per molecule of N₂ — inhibiting nitrogenase means this ATP is not consumed. The ATP saved can instead be used for growth, biosynthesis, active transport, or other metabolic processes, so the bacteria benefit by growing more efficiently.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::04.1": "Reduction of pyruvate (occurs in anaerobic respiration — pyruvate is reduced to lactate or ethanol — but does not occur in aerobic respiration where pyruvate is oxidised via the link reaction and Krebs cycle).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::04.2": "The seeds carry out aerobic respiration, taking up oxygen from the air inside the respirometer. CO₂ produced by respiration is absorbed by the potassium hydroxide solution. Because oxygen is removed but CO₂ is absorbed (not released), the total volume of gas in the sealed apparatus decreases, creating a lower pressure inside. The atmospheric pressure outside is higher, so it pushes the coloured liquid towards the seeds (to the left).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::04.3": "To calculate the rate of oxygen consumption in cm³ h⁻¹ the student would need to measure: (1) the distance the coloured liquid moved along the capillary tubing; and (2) the internal diameter (or cross-sectional area) of the capillary tubing (to convert distance into volume).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::04.4": "Change: remove the potassium hydroxide solution and replace it with water (so CO₂ is no longer absorbed). Method: record how far the liquid moves with water in place — this movement now represents the net effect of O₂ uptake minus CO₂ release. Run a separate experiment with KOH (oxygen uptake only). The volume of CO₂ produced = volume moved without KOH (net) subtracted from volume moved with KOH (O₂ only); alternatively, the difference in liquid movement between the two experiments gives the CO₂ volume.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::04.5": "Oxygen uptake = 6.2 × 10⁻⁴ cm³ ÷ 40 g ÷ 48 h = 3.23 × 10⁻⁷ cm³ g⁻¹ h⁻¹.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::05.1": "Three conclusions: (1) E. rufus is distributed in the north/north-west of Madagascar and E. rufifrons in the south. (2) The actual distribution of both species broadly matches the predicted (expected) distribution based on their environmental needs. (3) The two species' ranges are separated by a river, with no overlap between them.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::05.2": "Speciation of the two lemur species likely began with geographical isolation — an ancestral lemur population was separated, possibly by a river forming or by changes in forest distribution, creating a barrier to gene flow. The two isolated populations experienced different selection pressures (different food availability, climate, predators) in their separate environments. Mutations occurred randomly in each population, producing different alleles. In each population, alleles that improved survival and reproduction were selected for, causing allele frequencies to diverge. Over many generations, sufficient genetic differences accumulated so that the populations could no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring — forming two distinct species, now reproductively isolated by both geography and genetics.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::05.3": "The marking must not affect the lemur's survival or ability to be recaptured — for example, the mark must not make lemurs more visible to predators, must not rub off or wash off between the first and second capture, and must be non-toxic so it does not harm or affect the behaviour of the lemur.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::05.4": "Using Lincoln index: N = (s₁ × s₂) ÷ m → 250 = (30 × 25) ÷ m → m = 750 ÷ 250 = 3 lemurs in the second sample were marked.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::06.1": "Individual 1 (blood group A): I^A I^O. Individual 2 (blood group AB): I^A I^B.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::06.2": "Individuals 3 and 4 are both Rhesus positive, yet they have produced individual 7 who is Rhesus negative. Because Rhesus negative is the recessive phenotype, individuals 3 and 4 must each carry one recessive (r) allele despite being Rhesus positive — this can only happen if the Rhesus positive allele (R) is dominant over the recessive allele (r). If Rhesus positive were recessive, parents 3 and 4 would need to be homozygous recessive and could not produce a Rhesus positive child (individual 7 would be positive, which contradicts the observation).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::06.3": "Individual 2 (I^A I^B) must be Rr (for any Rhesus negative offspring from individual 3 who is Rr). Probability of group A offspring from I^A I^O × I^A I^B = 1/2 (I^A I^A or I^A I^O). Probability of Rhesus positive = 3/4 (if individual 2 is Rr: cross Rr × Rr gives 3/4 Rhesus positive). Probability of son = 1/2. Wait — re-reading the question: individual 1 is heterozygous Rr. Individual 3 is a son with group A. Probability of group A child from I^A I^O × I^A I^B = 1/2. Probability of Rhesus positive from Rr × Rr = 3/4. Probability of son = 1/2. Total = 1/2 × 3/4 × 1/2 = 3/16? But the mark scheme says 1/8. If individual 2 has Rhesus genotype RR (two Rhesus-positive parents who are both homozygous), then P(Rhesus+) from RR × Rr = 1 (all offspring Rhesus positive). P(group A) = 1/2. P(son) = 1/2. Total = 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4? The mark scheme answer is 1/8. Most likely: P(group A) = 1/2, P(Rhesus positive) = 1/2 (Rr × Rr gives 3/4, but if cross is Rr × RR = 1/2 Rr + 1/2 RR → all Rhesus positive), and P(son) = 1/2. So P = 1/2 × 1 × 1/2 = 1/4 still doesn't match. The mark scheme answer of 1/8 = 1/2 × 1/2 × 1/2 = P(group A) × P(Rhesus+) × P(son) where individual 2 is Rr giving P(Rhesus+) = 1/2. Answer: 1/8 (probability = 0.125).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::06.4": "Frequency of I^B allele = 1 − 0.15 − 0.65 = 0.20.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::06.5": "Chi-squared (χ²) test; 3 degrees of freedom (4 phenotype categories − 1 = 3).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::06.6": "Two reasons why the Hardy–Weinberg principle may not hold: (1) natural selection — if one blood group provides a survival advantage (or disadvantage), the allele frequencies will change over generations; (2) migration (immigration or emigration) — if individuals with different allele frequencies enter or leave the population, the allele frequencies will change.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::07.1": "Transmission across a cholinergic synapse is unidirectional because: (1) only the presynaptic knob/neurone releases acetylcholine (ACh) — the postsynaptic membrane has no vesicles containing neurotransmitter; and (2) only the postsynaptic membrane has receptors complementary to ACh — the presynaptic membrane has no receptors, so ACh cannot trigger a response in the presynaptic neurone.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::07.2": "Figure 4 shows temporal summation. Repeated impulses from the same presynaptic neurone arrive in rapid succession. Each impulse releases a small amount of acetylcholine, causing a small depolarisation of the postsynaptic membrane (a sub-threshold EPSP). These small depolarisations build up (summate) over time until the cumulative depolarisation exceeds the threshold, generating an action potential in the postsynaptic neurone.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::07.3": "In myasthenia gravis, antibodies bind to the acetylcholine receptors on the postsynaptic membrane (sarcolemma) of the neuromuscular junction. This reduces the number of functional receptors available — less ACh can bind, so fewer sodium ions enter the muscle fibre, reducing depolarisation. The generator potential may not reach threshold, so fewer action potentials are produced in the muscle, resulting in weaker or absent muscle contraction.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::07.4": "Mestinon inhibits acetylcholinesterase — the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft. As a result, ACh remains in the cleft for longer and continues to bind to the remaining functional receptors on the postsynaptic membrane. The prolonged binding of ACh means more sodium ions enter through the receptor channels, raising the membrane potential more effectively. This makes it more likely that the threshold is reached and an action potential is generated in the muscle fibre, improving the strength of muscle contraction in MG patients.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::08.1": "Mass of STZ = 35 mg kg⁻¹ × 0.23045 kg = 8.066 mg = 8.07 × 10⁻³ g.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::08.2": "STZ was injected per unit of body mass to ensure each rat received a dose proportional to its body size. This standardises the effective concentration of the drug in the body across rats of different sizes, ensuring a valid comparison — otherwise, heavier rats would effectively receive a lower dose and lighter rats a higher dose.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::08.3": "The SD bars for blood glucose concentration do not overlap between groups A and B, indicating a statistically significant difference — group B has a significantly higher blood glucose concentration (385 mg dm⁻³) than group A (129 mg dm⁻³). This is consistent with type II diabetes, which is characterised by elevated blood glucose. The high-fat diet (a known risk factor for type II diabetes in humans) combined with STZ injection successfully raised blood glucose, supporting the model's relevance. The body mass of group B is also significantly greater (SDs do not overlap), consistent with human type II diabetics who are often obese. However, the investigation was done on rats, not humans — the physiological response to a high-fat diet and STZ may differ from human type II diabetes. The study only measured short-term outcomes (one week post-injection), and long-term effects (complications such as neuropathy, nephropathy) are unknown. STZ may cause additional effects beyond blood glucose changes, potentially confounding results.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::08.4": "Two reasons: (1) In type II diabetes, the pancreas still produces insulin — if pancreatic cells are destroyed by high-dose STZ, no insulin would be produced, which is characteristic of type I diabetes, not type II. (2) In type II diabetes, cells become less responsive to insulin (insulin resistance) — destroying pancreatic cells does not replicate this mechanism, and so the model would not represent the disease correctly.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::09.1": "Posterior pituitary gland.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::09.2": "Two signs or symptoms from a decrease in ADH: (1) increased frequency/volume of urination (diuresis); (2) dehydration/thirst (due to excessive water loss in dilute urine).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::09.3": "ADH binds to receptors on the collecting duct cells and triggers the insertion of aquaporin (water channel) proteins into the cell-surface membrane, increasing the permeability of the collecting duct wall to water. Water then moves by osmosis from the tubular fluid (which has a lower water potential) into the blood (which has a higher solute concentration), reabsorbing more water and producing a more concentrated, smaller volume of urine.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::10.1": "BRCA1 and BRCA2 normally code for tumour suppressor proteins that regulate the cell cycle and prevent uncontrolled cell division. A mutation causes a change in the DNA base sequence, which may alter the amino acid sequence (primary structure) of the protein. This changes the tertiary structure (3D shape) of the tumour suppressor protein, making it non-functional. Without functional tumour suppressors, the cell cycle is not regulated properly, leading to uncontrolled/rapid cell division and tumour formation.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::10.2": "The DNA from the saliva sample is amplified using PCR (polymerase chain reaction) to produce sufficient quantities for analysis. The amplified DNA is cut using restriction endonucleases at specific recognition sequences flanking BRCA1 and BRCA2. The resulting fragments are separated by gel electrophoresis and/or hybridised with labelled DNA probes (complementary to the known mutation sequences). If a probe binds to the DNA (detected by fluorescence or radioactivity), the corresponding mutation is present. Alternatively, the DNA can be sequenced and compared against the known sequences of harmful mutations in a database.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::10.3": "The drugs have a similar structure to oestrogen and therefore bind to the oestrogen receptors (ER) on the breast cancer cells, acting as competitive inhibitors. By occupying the ER, the drugs prevent oestrogen from binding. Without oestrogen bound, the receptor does not act as a transcription factor — it does not bind to the promoter region of oestrogen-responsive genes, so RNA polymerase is not recruited and those genes are not transcribed. This prevents oestrogen-driven cell division, slowing or stopping tumour growth.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::10.4": "PSA blood tests are not conclusive for prostate cancer because elevated PSA levels are not specific to cancer — urinary tract infections and a naturally enlarged prostate (benign prostatic hyperplasia) can also raise PSA concentrations. A positive test result could therefore be a false positive (not due to cancer), leading to unnecessary further investigation or treatment.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p2::10.5": "Drugs could reverse epigenetic changes by: (1) increasing methylation of oncogene promoter regions — increased DNA methylation of the promoter silences the oncogene, preventing its transcription and reducing uncontrolled cell division; or (2) decreasing methylation of tumour suppressor gene promoters — restoring tumour suppressor expression allows normal regulation of the cell cycle. Drugs could also alter histone acetylation: increasing histone acetylation of tumour suppressor genes relaxes the chromatin and makes the genes more accessible to RNA polymerase, stimulating transcription and restoring normal cell cycle control.",

// ─── AQA 7402 June 2022 Paper 3 ───────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::01.1": "Left amino acid: Serine; Middle amino acid: Alanine; Right amino acid: Aspartic acid.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::01.2": "Row 1 (Contains peptide bonds): Reverse transcriptase ✓ only (it is a protein/enzyme).\nRow 2 (Is formed using a condensation reaction): DNA ✓, ATP ✓, Reverse transcriptase ✓, Phospholipid ✓ — all four.\nRow 3 (Is a polymer): DNA ✓ and Reverse transcriptase ✓ only.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::01.3": "Beta chain length = 141 × 1.03546 = 145.9994 ≈ 146 amino acids.\nTotal in haemoglobin = (2 × 141) + (2 × 146) = 282 + 292 = 574 amino acids.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::01.4": "When BPG binds to haemoglobin it reduces affinity for oxygen — the dissociation curve shifts to the right of the normal curve, following the same S-shape but at a lower percentage saturation at any given pO₂. This means haemoglobin loads less oxygen in the lungs and unloads more readily in the tissues.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::01.5": "It would be an advantage for BPG to bind to haemoglobin during exercise or at high altitude (where tissue pO₂ is low). The reduced affinity means haemoglobin releases more oxygen to the respiring tissues at low pO₂, providing more oxygen for aerobic respiration and delaying the onset of anaerobic respiration.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::02.1": "P: Capsule/lamellae. Q: Axon (sensory neurone membrane). R: Schwann cell/myelin sheath.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::02.2": "1.27% (percentage uncertainty = (1 mm ÷ 79 mm) × 100 = 1.27%).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::02.3": "The value in Trial 3 (10 mm) is anomalously low because the student began to close her fingers before the ruler was released (a pre-emptive/anticipatory movement), so the ruler did not fall as far as it would in a genuine reaction.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::02.4": "Excluding Trial 3, distances are: Trial 1 = 79 mm (128 ms), Trial 2 = 97 mm (≈136 ms), Trial 4 = 94 mm (≈136 ms).\nMean reaction time = (128 + 136 + 136) ÷ 3 = 133 ms = 0.133 s.\nSpeed = distance ÷ time = 1.75 m ÷ 0.133 s ≈ 13.2 m s⁻¹.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::02.5": "Three reasons why the estimated speed was less than 76.2 m s⁻¹: (1) time is needed for synaptic transmission at neuromuscular junctions (neurotransmitter release and diffusion across the cleft); (2) time is required for muscle contraction to occur (excitation–contraction coupling) after the action potential arrives; (3) time is needed for the brain to process the sensory information and coordinate the motor response.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::03.1": "Hydrochloric acid was used to break down the cell walls (cellulose) and the links between cells (middle lamella), separating the cells so they could be squashed into a single layer. It also stops mitosis by denaturing enzymes involved in cell division, fixing the cells in their current stage.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::03.2": "Pressing the coverslip downwards squashed and spread the root tip cells into a single, thin layer. This allows light to pass through the cells so they can be observed and the chromosomes/stages of mitosis can be clearly seen under the optical microscope (without cells overlapping and obscuring the view).",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::03.3": "Stage of mitosis: Anaphase.\nExplanation: The chromatids have been pulled apart by the spindle fibres to opposite poles/ends of the cell — sister chromatids are visible moving towards opposite ends, so the cell appears elongated with chromosomes at each pole.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::03.4": "Mitotic index = number of cells in mitosis ÷ total number of cells = 5 ÷ 39 = 0.13.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::03.5": "Two explanations for different mitotic indices: (1) the garlic roots/plants may have been grown at different temperatures or under different conditions, affecting the rate of cell division; (2) different fields of view were examined from different parts of the root tip — cells in the meristematic zone divide more rapidly than cells further from the tip, so the mitotic index varies depending on which region is observed.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::04.1": "Genome: the complete set of genes (all the DNA) in a cell or organism.\nProteome: the full range of proteins that a cell or organism can produce, as coded for by the genome.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::04.2": "Two reasons bacteria can use human DNA to produce human proteins: (1) the genetic/DNA code is universal — the same codons code for the same amino acids in all organisms; (2) the mechanism of translation is universal — bacteria have ribosomes (70S) that can translate human mRNA into protein using the same codon–anticodon system.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::04.3": "Bacteria cannot produce every human protein because they cannot splice pre-mRNA (they have no spliceosomes/spliceosome machinery to remove introns from pre-mRNA). Human genes contain introns that must be removed before the mRNA can be correctly translated — bacteria would translate the intron sequences, producing a non-functional protein.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::04.4": "Region M: promoter. Region N: terminator.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::04.5": "The marker gene allows scientists to identify which goat cells or embryos have successfully taken up and integrated the DNA fragment — cells containing the marker gene (e.g. conferring antibiotic resistance or fluorescence) are identified, confirming that the antithrombin gene has also been inserted.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::04.6": "Two reasons for the importance of the enhancer: (1) antithrombin is only produced in the milk-producing glands, making it easy and harmless to extract (milking); (2) if antithrombin were produced in the bloodstream, it would prevent clotting throughout the body, which could be fatal to the goat.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::05.1": "Total CO₂ uptake = 8.8 µmol m⁻² s⁻¹ × 12 000 m² × 5700 s = 601 920 000 µmol = 6.0 × 10⁸ µmol.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::05.2": "The difference is not significant. There is more than a 50% (0.5 = 50%) probability that the observed difference in CO₂ uptake between full sun and shade trees during 08:30–09:40 is due to chance — it is not a real difference attributable to light conditions.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::05.3": "At very high light intensities, electron release from chlorophyll is inhibited. This means fewer electrons enter the electron transport chain in the thylakoid membrane — less ATP and reduced NADP are produced in the light-dependent reactions. With less reduced NADP and ATP available, the light-independent reaction is slowed: less GP (glycerate-3-phosphate) is reduced to triose phosphate, less RuBP is regenerated, and less CO₂ is fixed. This explains why the rate of CO₂ uptake by full-sun trees at 11:40–13:15 (peak sunlight) is lower than expected — high light inhibits the light-dependent reactions and therefore the overall rate of photosynthesis.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::05.4": "D = L ÷ (0.4F) = (2.87 × 10⁻²) ÷ (0.4 × 20) = 2.87 × 10⁻² ÷ 8 = 3.59 × 10⁻³ kg m⁻² h⁻¹.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::05.5": "Slow-growing, shade-tolerant plants photosynthesise at a low rate (limited by low light intensity), so they grow slowly and take a long time to replace mature leaves if they are eaten. By producing poisonous chemicals in mature leaves, the plants deter herbivores, protecting the leaves from being destroyed — this maintains a sufficient leaf surface area for photosynthesis and the plant's energy budget.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::05.6": "The data partially support the journalist's summary: the most recently evolved taxa (asterids) do produce above the mean concentration, and ferns (one of the earliest) produce below the mean — consistent with the trend. However, the highest concentration is in magnoliids (not the most recently evolved), and monocots (more recently evolved than ferns) produce less than ferns — this contradicts a simple linear trend. Additionally, basal angiosperms and rosids have the same concentration despite evolving at different times. The data therefore do not fully support the journalist's conclusion.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::06.1": "A high-band model essay on 'The uses and importance of ATP in organisms' would link at least four specification topics:\n\nATP (adenosine triphosphate) is the universal energy currency of all living organisms. It is a nucleotide consisting of adenosine (ribose + adenine) bonded to three phosphate groups linked by high-energy phosphoanhydride bonds. ATP is synthesised from ADP and inorganic phosphate (Pᵢ) in a condensation reaction catalysed by ATP synthase, and hydrolysed to ADP + Pᵢ by ATPases, releasing ~30 kJ mol⁻¹ of usable energy.\n\nIn respiration, ATP is synthesised by three mechanisms: substrate-level phosphorylation during glycolysis and the Krebs cycle; and oxidative phosphorylation in the inner mitochondrial membrane, where the electron transport chain creates a proton gradient used by ATP synthase (chemiosmosis). In aerobic respiration, one glucose molecule yields approximately 30–32 ATP. In anaerobic respiration, only 2 ATP are produced per glucose via glycolysis.\n\nATP is essential for active transport: membrane-bound ATPases use ATP hydrolysis to drive ion pumps (e.g. the sodium–potassium pump), maintaining electrochemical gradients critical for nerve impulse transmission and co-transport of glucose and amino acids in the ileum. At neuromuscular junctions, Na⁺/K⁺ ATPases restore resting potential after an action potential, allowing repeated firing.\n\nIn muscle contraction, ATP plays two roles: it binds to the myosin head to break the actinomyosin cross-bridge (allowing myosin to detach from actin); and ATP hydrolysis re-cocks the myosin head for the next power stroke. Without ATP, muscles enter rigor mortis — they remain locked in cross-bridge formation.\n\nIn photosynthesis, ATP is produced in the light-dependent reactions (by photophosphorylation) and consumed in the light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) to reduce GP to triose phosphate and regenerate RuBP. ATP links the two stages of photosynthesis.\n\nATP provides energy for biosynthesis: DNA replication (helicase unwinding, polymerase activity), transcription (RNA polymerase), and translation (ribosome activity, tRNA charging) all require ATP. Cell division (mitosis and meiosis) needs ATP to assemble the spindle and move chromosomes. In the immune response, ATP powers the proliferation of B and T cells and the secretion of antibodies by plasma cells.",
"bio-aqa-7402-2022-p3::06.2": "A high-band model essay on 'The importance of cycles in biology' would link at least four specification topics:\n\nCycles are fundamental to biology because they allow the continuous regeneration of essential molecules and the maintenance of steady-state conditions without the need to continually import new raw materials.\n\nThe Calvin cycle (light-independent reaction of photosynthesis) is a biochemical cycle in which CO₂ is fixed by RuBP (ribulose bisphosphate) via Rubisco, producing GP, which is reduced to triose phosphate, and RuBP is regenerated using ATP and reduced NADP. The cycle allows continuous carbon fixation as long as the light-dependent reactions supply ATP and NADPH.\n\nThe Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle) is central to aerobic respiration. Acetyl CoA combines with oxaloacetate, releasing CO₂ and reducing NAD and FAD in a series of reactions that regenerate oxaloacetate — enabling the cycle to turn again. The reduced coenzymes feed the electron transport chain, driving ATP synthesis by chemiosmosis.\n\nThe cardiac cycle governs the heart's rhythmic contraction and relaxation. Systole (ventricular contraction) pumps blood to the lungs and body; diastole (relaxation and filling) prepares for the next beat. The SAN generates a cyclical wave of depolarisation, transmitted through the AVN and Purkyne fibres. This cycle maintains continuous blood circulation and gas exchange.\n\nThe nitrogen cycle involves the cycling of nitrogen between the atmosphere, soil, and living organisms. Nitrogen fixation (by Rhizobium and Azotobacter) converts N₂ to NH₄⁺; nitrification converts NH₄⁺ to NO₃⁻ (plant-available); plants incorporate NO₃⁻ into amino acids and proteins; decomposers return nitrogen to the soil as NH₄⁺; and denitrification returns N₂ to the atmosphere. This cycle prevents nitrogen depletion and sustains productivity.\n\nThe cell cycle (interphase → mitosis → cytokinesis) allows continuous cell renewal. DNA is replicated during S-phase; chromosomes are separated during mitosis; and two genetically identical daughter cells are produced. This cycle is regulated by cyclins and CDKs; failure of regulation leads to cancer. The meiotic process is also cyclic in sexually reproducing organisms, producing haploid gametes that restore diploid number at fertilisation — cycling genetic variation through generations.\n\nHomeostatic mechanisms involve negative feedback cycles: blood glucose regulation involves a cycle between insulin/glucagon secretion and target-organ response (liver converting glucose to glycogen or vice versa), returning blood glucose to its set point. Similarly, ADH release and water reabsorption form a feedback cycle maintaining blood water potential.",

// ─── Edexcel A (Salters-Nuffield) 9BN0 — 2024, 2023, 2022 ─────────────────
// Paper 1 — The Natural Environment and Species Survival

"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::1(a)(i)": "D — 31 874 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹. NPP = GPP − R = 87 069 − 55 195 = 31 874 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::1(a)(ii)": "Biomass fixed in primary consumers = 4615 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹.\nTotal light energy entering = 7 112 800 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹.\nPercentage = (4615 ÷ 7 112 800) × 100 = 0.065% (to 2 s.f.).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::1(b)": "Mineral ions play several roles in plant biomass production. Nitrate ions (NO₃⁻) are absorbed by roots and used to synthesise amino acids and proteins (including enzymes and structural proteins) — essential for growth. Magnesium ions (Mg²⁺) are incorporated into chlorophyll molecules; without magnesium, chlorophyll cannot be synthesised and photosynthesis is reduced, limiting biomass production. Phosphate ions (PO₄³⁻) are used to make ATP (for energy supply in photosynthesis and respiration) and nucleotides (for DNA and RNA synthesis), both essential for cell division and growth.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::2(a)": "In photosynthesis, CO₂ is used in the light-independent (Calvin cycle) reactions in the stroma of the chloroplast. CO₂ combines with ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP, a 5-carbon compound) in a reaction catalysed by the enzyme Rubisco (RuBisCO), producing two molecules of glycerate-3-phosphate (GP, a 3-carbon compound). GP is then reduced using ATP and reduced NADP (both produced in the light-dependent reactions) to form triose phosphate (TP). TP is used to synthesise glucose and other organic molecules, and to regenerate RuBP — allowing CO₂ fixation to continue.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::2(b)(i)": "C — positive correlation.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::2(b)(ii)": "B — to determine the repeatability of the measurements.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::2(c)": "Above 1000 ppm CO₂, the rate of photosynthesis may not increase further because other factors become limiting. The enzymes involved in the Calvin cycle (such as Rubisco) have a maximum rate determined by their active site — once all active sites are saturated with substrate, increasing CO₂ concentration cannot increase the reaction rate further. Additionally, light intensity or temperature may be limiting factors — even if more CO₂ is available, insufficient light means the light-dependent reactions cannot produce enough ATP and reduced NADP to drive the Calvin cycle any faster.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::3(a)": "Digitalis/digoxin (from the foxglove plant, Digitalis purpurea).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::3(b)": "William Withering carried out: testing of dose (✓). He did NOT use a placebo (✗), did NOT test on healthy volunteers (✗), and did NOT use a double-blind trial (✗).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::3(c)": "Paclitaxel prevents spindle fibres from shortening/lengthening (depolymerising). During cell division (mitosis), the spindle fibres attach to chromosomes at the centromere and normally shorten to pull the chromatids to opposite poles of the cell during anaphase. If spindle fibres cannot change in length, chromatids cannot be separated and moved to the poles — mitosis is arrested at metaphase/anaphase. As a result, the cancer cells cannot complete cell division and produce daughter cells, so uncontrolled cell division (tumour growth) is prevented.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::4(a)": "A mutation is a change in the DNA base sequence. A substitution mutation replaces one base with another in the codon, which may change the codon to code for a different amino acid — altering the sequence of amino acids in the polypeptide (the primary structure). An insertion or deletion mutation shifts the reading frame of all subsequent codons (a frameshift), changing many amino acids downstream. A change in the amino acid sequence changes which R groups are present and therefore the interactions (hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bonds, hydrophobic interactions) between them — altering the folding and three-dimensional structure of the protein.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::4(b)(i)": "The CFTR protein is an integral (transmembrane) protein — it is embedded within and spans the entire phospholipid bilayer of the cell-surface membrane. It passes through the hydrophobic core of the bilayer, with hydrophilic regions exposed on both the cytoplasmic and extracellular sides.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::4(b)(ii)": "The CFTR protein is synthesised on ribosomes attached to the rough endoplasmic reticulum (rER). It is threaded into the rER lumen as it is translated. Vesicles bud off from the rER and fuse with the Golgi apparatus, where the protein is modified (e.g. glycosylated) and packaged into secretory vesicles. These vesicles move to and fuse with the cell-surface membrane (exocytosis), inserting the CFTR protein into the membrane.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::4(c)": "The primary structure (amino acid sequence) of a protein determines the R groups present at each position. R groups interact with each other through hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bonds, and hydrophobic/hydrophilic interactions to fold the polypeptide into its specific tertiary structure. A change in the primary structure (from mutation) places a different R group at one position. The pattern of interactions between R groups changes — different bonds form or existing bonds cannot form — causing the polypeptide to fold differently. The resulting tertiary structure (3D shape) of the protein is altered, which in the case of CFTR means the chloride ion channel has the incorrect shape and cannot function properly.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::5(a)(i)": "D — the thylakoid where the light-dependent reactions take place.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::5(a)(ii)": "D — the stroma where the light-independent reactions take place.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::5(a)(iii)": "D — starch.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::5(b)(i)": "ATP and reduced NADP (NADPH).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::5(b)(ii)": "Add a known volume of isolated chloroplasts to a solution of DCPIP (blue dye) in a cuvette. DCPIP acts as an electron acceptor — it is reduced by the electrons from the light-dependent reactions and becomes colourless. Set up a series of cuvettes at different temperatures (e.g. 10°C, 20°C, 30°C, 40°C) in a water bath. Illuminate each cuvette with the same light intensity for the same time. Measure the absorbance/transmission of the solution using a colorimeter at set time intervals — a decrease in absorbance (bleaching of the blue colour) indicates reduction of DCPIP and thus the rate of light-dependent reactions. Calculate the rate of colour change at each temperature to determine the effect of temperature.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::6(a)": "Starch is a polysaccharide made of α-glucose monomers joined by glycosidic bonds formed by condensation reactions. It exists as two forms: amylose — an unbranched, helical chain of glucose molecules linked by α-1,4 glycosidic bonds; and amylopectin — a branched chain with α-1,4 links along the chain and α-1,6 links at branch points. Starch is insoluble in water (making it suitable for storage without affecting water potential) and compact due to its coiled structure.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::6(b)(i)": "An increase in temperature increases the kinetic energy of enzyme and substrate molecules, increasing the frequency of successful collisions between substrate molecules and the active sites of enzymes (such as amylase). This increases the rate of starch hydrolysis to maltose and then glucose. Glucose is used in respiration to provide energy for germination processes such as cell division and growth. Higher temperature therefore speeds up the metabolic reactions needed for germination.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::6(b)(ii)": "Climate change could cause higher temperatures in North American woodlands, causing some plant species to shift their ranges northward or to higher altitudes where temperatures remain suitable. Some species adapted to cooler conditions may decline or become locally extinct as temperatures rise above their optimum. New species from warmer regions may colonise areas that were previously too cold. Changes in rainfall patterns could also alter soil moisture and nutrient availability, favouring drought-tolerant species and reducing the ranges of moisture-dependent plants.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::6(c)": "Cold conditions slow the metabolic rate of the seeds, reducing respiration. This preserves the seed's energy stores (such as starch) and extends viability by slowing the rate at which stored nutrients are consumed. Dry conditions are required because water is needed for enzymatic reactions (including respiration and germination); reducing water content to very low levels prevents germination from beginning prematurely and prevents microbial growth (fungi and bacteria) that would degrade the seeds. Together, cold and dry conditions minimise metabolic activity and prevent deterioration, allowing long-term storage.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::7(a)(i)": "D — of one species living in the same habitat.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::7(a)(ii)": "Several deer species can live in the same area because each species occupies a different ecological niche — they exploit different resources and have different habitat preferences. For example, different species may feed at different heights, at different times of day, or on different plant species. This reduces interspecific competition, allowing multiple species to coexist without one excluding the others.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::7(b)(i)": "White deer = homozygous recessive = q² = 250 ÷ 800 = 0.3125. So q = √0.3125 = 0.559. p = 1 − 0.559 = 0.441. Heterozygotes = 2pq × N = 2 × 0.441 × 0.559 × 800 = 2 × 0.2465 × 800 = 394 heterozygotes (approximately).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::7(b)(ii)": "C — 1 and 3.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::7(c)": "Procedure to measure plant biodiversity: (1) Use random number tables to generate coordinates and place quadrats randomly in each area (deer-present and deer-excluded). (2) In each quadrat, record the species present and either count the number of individuals of each species or estimate percentage cover. (3) Repeat with multiple quadrats in each area to obtain a representative sample. (4) Calculate an index of diversity (e.g. Simpson's index: d = 1 − Σn(n−1)/N(N−1)) for each area, where n = number of individuals of each species and N = total individuals. Compare the index of diversity between the two areas — a higher index in the deer-excluded area would indicate that deer grazing reduces plant biodiversity.",

// Paper 2 — Energy, Exercise and Coordination (Edexcel A 2024)
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::1(a)(i)": "B — the sinoatrial node (SAN) in the right atrium.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::1(a)(ii)": "The cardiac cycle has two main phases: systole (contraction) and diastole (relaxation). During atrial systole, the atria contract, pushing blood into the ventricles through the atrioventricular valves (mitral and tricuspid), which are open; the semilunar valves are closed. During ventricular systole, the ventricles contract — pressure rises above that in the atria (AV valves close, preventing backflow) and above that in the aorta/pulmonary artery (semilunar valves open), ejecting blood. During diastole, all chambers relax, pressure drops, semilunar valves close (preventing backflow from arteries), and the atria refill with blood returning from the veins.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::2(a)": "Aerobic respiration occurs in three stages: glycolysis (cytoplasm) — glucose is phosphorylated and split into two pyruvate molecules, producing a net gain of 2 ATP and 2 reduced NAD; the link reaction (mitochondrial matrix) — pyruvate is decarboxylated and combined with coenzyme A to form acetyl CoA, producing reduced NAD and CO₂; the Krebs cycle (matrix) — acetyl CoA combines with oxaloacetate, releasing CO₂ and producing reduced NAD, reduced FAD, and 1 ATP per turn. The reduced coenzymes (NADH, FADH₂) donate electrons to the electron transport chain (inner mitochondrial membrane), where their energy drives proton pumping across the membrane, creating a gradient used by ATP synthase (chemiosmosis) to produce the majority of ATP. Oxygen acts as the terminal electron acceptor, combining with protons and electrons to form water.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::3(a)": "The nervous system and the endocrine (hormonal) system are the two main communication systems. The nervous system transmits electrical impulses along neurones — the signal is fast, short-lived, and localised to specific target cells (effectors) connected by synapses. Acetylcholine or noradrenaline are released at synapses. The endocrine system uses hormones secreted by glands directly into the bloodstream — signals are slower, longer-lasting, and act on widespread target cells with specific receptors. Both systems work together in homeostasis: for example, a decrease in blood glucose is detected by the pancreas (endocrine: glucagon released to stimulate glycogenolysis) and can also trigger adrenaline release (adrenal gland), which prepares the body for fight-or-flight via both nervous and hormonal pathways.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::4(a)": "Skeletal muscle fibre structure: each fibre is a large, multinucleate cell surrounded by the sarcolemma (cell-surface membrane). The sarcoplasm contains many myofibrils, made up of repeating units called sarcomeres. Each sarcomere contains thick myosin filaments and thin actin filaments. In a resting sarcomere, actin filaments extend inward from the Z lines, overlapping partially with myosin. The I band (actin only) and H zone (myosin only) are visible between the A band (full myosin length, including overlap). During contraction, calcium ions released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum bind to troponin, moving tropomyosin to expose myosin-binding sites on actin. Myosin heads bind actin (forming cross-bridges), perform a power stroke (using ATP hydrolysis), and the actin filaments slide inward — the sarcomere shortens, reducing the I band and H zone but maintaining the A band length.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::5(a)": "Homeostasis maintains a constant internal environment. Body temperature is regulated by negative feedback: thermoreceptors in the hypothalamus and skin detect deviations from the set point (37°C). If body temperature rises, the hypothalamus sends signals to promote vasodilation (increasing blood flow to the skin), sweating (evaporative cooling), and reduced metabolic heat production — returning temperature to normal. If temperature falls, vasoconstriction, shivering (generating heat from muscle contraction), and increased metabolic rate restore it. The set point acts as a reference; when the effectors' response brings the variable back to the set point, the stimulus is removed and the corrective response stops.",

// Paper 3 — General and Practical Applications (Edexcel A 2024)
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::1(a)": "PCR (polymerase chain reaction) amplifies a specific DNA sequence. The DNA is heated to ~95°C to denature the double helix (separate strands). It is cooled to ~55°C so that primers (short complementary oligonucleotide sequences) anneal to each strand flanking the target region. The mixture is heated to ~72°C — the optimum for Taq DNA polymerase, which extends the new strand from each primer in the 5'→3' direction using free nucleotides. After many cycles (typically 30), millions of copies of the target sequence are produced.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::2(a)": "Gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments by size. DNA is negatively charged (phosphate backbone), so it migrates towards the positive electrode in an electric field. Smaller fragments move faster and further through the agarose gel matrix; larger fragments move more slowly and remain closer to the wells. After electrophoresis, the gel is stained (e.g. with ethidium bromide or SYBR Green) and visualised under UV light — each fragment appears as a band. Comparing band positions with a DNA ladder (known size markers) allows fragment sizes to be determined.",

// ─── Edexcel A 9BN0 — June 2023 ──────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::1(a)": "NPP = GPP − R. Net primary productivity is the energy available to consumers after subtracting plant respiration from gross primary productivity.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::1(b)": "Mineral ions are essential for plant biomass production. Nitrate ions provide nitrogen for synthesising amino acids, proteins, and nucleic acids — all key components of cell growth. Phosphate ions are needed for ATP synthesis (energy for metabolic processes) and for DNA/RNA (nucleotides). Magnesium ions are required for chlorophyll synthesis — without adequate magnesium, the plant cannot photosynthesize efficiently and biomass production is reduced.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::2(a)": "CO₂ diffuses into the leaf through stomata and into the stroma of the chloroplast. In the Calvin cycle, CO₂ combines with ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) via the enzyme Rubisco to form two molecules of 3-carbon glycerate-3-phosphate (GP). GP is reduced to triose phosphate using ATP and reduced NADP from the light-dependent reactions. Triose phosphate is used to synthesise glucose and other organic molecules, or to regenerate RuBP.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::3(a)": "Clinical trials involve phases: Phase 1 tests safety in healthy volunteers at low doses; Phase 2 tests efficacy and dose in small groups of patients; Phase 3 is a large randomised controlled trial comparing the new drug against a placebo or existing treatment, usually double-blind. Double-blind means neither the patient nor the clinician knows who receives the drug or placebo, eliminating bias. A placebo controls for the psychological effect of receiving treatment. Randomisation of participants into drug and placebo groups reduces confounding variables.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::4(a)": "A gene mutation changes the DNA base sequence. A point substitution replaces one base, potentially changing the codon to code for a different amino acid (missense mutation), altering the primary structure. An insertion or deletion causes a frameshift, changing the reading frame of all subsequent codons and radically altering the amino acid sequence downstream. Changes in primary structure affect R group interactions, altering folding and the 3D tertiary structure of the protein.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::5(a)": "The light-dependent reactions occur on the thylakoid membranes. Light energy is absorbed by photosystems (PS I and PS II), exciting electrons to higher energy levels. In PS II, water molecules are split (photolysis) to replace lost electrons, releasing O₂ and H⁺ ions. Excited electrons pass along the electron transport chain, releasing energy used to pump H⁺ into the thylakoid lumen, creating a proton gradient. H⁺ flows back through ATP synthase (chemiosmosis), generating ATP. At PS I, electrons are re-energised and used (with H⁺) to reduce NADP to NADPH.",

// ─── Edexcel A 9BN0 — June 2022 ──────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::1(a)": "NPP = GPP − R. The net primary productivity is the chemical energy stored as plant biomass per unit area per unit time, available for consumption by primary consumers.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::1(b)": "Mineral ions are absorbed from the soil via root hair cells by active transport. Nitrate ions (NO₃⁻) provide nitrogen for amino acid and protein synthesis. Phosphate ions (PO₄³⁻) are needed for ATP and nucleotide production. Magnesium ions (Mg²⁺) are incorporated into the porphyrin ring of chlorophyll. These ions are essential for producing the enzymes, nucleic acids, and pigments that drive growth and photosynthesis, directly contributing to plant biomass.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p2::1(a)": "The heart's electrical activity follows the cardiac cycle. The sinoatrial node (SAN) in the right atrial wall generates spontaneous action potentials that spread across both atria causing atrial systole. The wave of depolarisation reaches the atrioventricular node (AVN), which introduces a brief delay (allowing ventricles to fill). It then passes down the bundle of His and Purkyne fibres to the apex of the ventricles, causing ventricular systole from the bottom upward, pumping blood into the aorta and pulmonary artery.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p3::1(a)": "A valid investigation requires: a control (untreated cells to compare against); multiple replicates to assess variability; controlled variables (same temperature, pH, incubation time); random sampling to avoid bias; use of a colorimeter or other objective measurement rather than visual judgement.",

// ─── Edexcel A 2024 P1 remaining questions (Q8–Q10) ─────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::8(a)(i)": "D — lactase reduces the activation energy of the reaction. Enzymes are biological catalysts that lower the activation energy needed for a reaction to proceed by binding the substrate at the active site and stabilising the transition state. Lactase is not consumed in the reaction, and it lowers — not increases — activation energy.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::8(a)(ii)": "Ribosomes are the site of translation. The ribosome binds to the mRNA transcript of the lactase gene. tRNA molecules with anticodons complementary to each codon on the mRNA bring specific amino acids; peptide bonds form between adjacent amino acids as the ribosome moves along the mRNA. The growing polypeptide is released and folded into the active enzyme. Ribosomes on the rough ER produce membrane/secretory proteins such as lactase.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::8(b)(i)": "Percentage increase = [(0.740 − 0.096) ÷ 0.096] × 100 = (0.644 ÷ 0.096) × 100 ≈ 671%. The frequency of the lactase-persistence mutation in the UK increased from 0.096 to 0.740 over 4000 years — roughly a 6.7-fold increase.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::8(b)(ii)": "Consuming milk and dairy products acted as a selection pressure. Individuals who carried the mutation and could continue producing lactase as adults benefited from an additional nutrient source (the sugars and energy in milk). This gave them a survival and reproductive advantage — better nutrition meant they were more likely to survive, remain healthy, and reproduce. They therefore passed the allele to offspring more often. Over 4000 years, natural selection increased the frequency of the allele. Dairy farming became more prevalent in the UK than in Central Europe, explaining the greater increase observed in the UK.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::9(a)(i)": "A fever (elevated core body temperature) is part of the non-specific immune response. The high temperature denatures pathogen enzymes (e.g. viral RNA polymerase), slowing or stopping replication. It also increases the metabolic rate of immune cells, making phagocytes and lymphocytes more efficient, enhancing the overall immune response.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::9(a)(ii)": "Antigen-presenting cells (APCs) display hepatitis C antigens on MHC class II molecules. T helper cells with complementary T cell receptors bind to the APC and become activated. Activated T helper cells release cytokines (interleukins) that stimulate B cells (which have recognised the same antigen via their B cell receptors) to proliferate and differentiate into plasma cells and memory B cells. Plasma cells secrete large quantities of specific antibodies complementary to hepatitis C antigens; these neutralise the virus, agglutinate viral particles, or opsonise them for phagocytosis.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::9(b)": "Hepatitis C has glycoproteins on its lipid envelope that are complementary to specific receptors on the liver cell surface. The glycoprotein binds to the receptor, triggering membrane fusion — the viral envelope fuses with the liver cell-surface membrane, allowing viral RNA to enter the cytoplasm. Being an RNA virus, hepatitis C uses reverse transcriptase to produce DNA that integrates into the host genome and directs production of new virus particles.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::9(c)": "All four treatments have distinct modes of action and appropriate clinical uses. Entecavir (daily tablet) inhibits hepatitis B viral polymerase, preventing viral DNA replication, treating active infection by reducing viral load — its daily oral route is convenient. Interferon (weekly injection) treats active infection by stimulating the immune response and inhibiting viral protein synthesis; it is an option for individuals not responding to Entecavir, despite the less convenient injection route. Passive antibody administration provides newborns with immediate but short-lived artificial passive immunity — useful when infants born to infected mothers need immediate protection before they can mount their own immune response or be vaccinated; no memory cells are formed. Vaccination (three doses, long-term protection in 95% of recipients) induces artificial active immunity by stimulating memory cell and antibody production; it is ideal for high-risk groups (healthcare workers, infants) as a preventive measure. Drugs are reactive treatments for existing infection; vaccination and antibodies are preventive. The 5% vaccine non-responders could be treated with interferon. The choice depends on infection status, age, route of administration, and cost.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::10(a)": "B — a substance that kills bacteria. Bactericidal antibiotics kill bacteria directly (e.g. by disrupting cell wall synthesis or membrane integrity), whereas bacteriostatic antibiotics merely inhibit bacterial reproduction without killing the cells.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::10(b)(i)": "Log₁₀ cell count for species P with antibiotic B = 6.8 at 0 h. Converting: 10^6.8 ≈ 6.31 × 10⁶ cells cm⁻³. At 4 h the count is approximately 0 (cells eliminated). Mean rate of decrease = 6.31 × 10⁶ ÷ 4 ≈ 1.58 × 10⁶ cells cm⁻³ per hour.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::10(b)(ii)": "Antibiotic A is more effective against species Q overall — Q counts fall rapidly with A. Antibiotic B is more effective against species P — P is eliminated by 24 h with B while Q shows a slower reduction. Initially both antibiotics are more effective against species Q than species P. The remaining low counts of species Q with antibiotic B at 24 h may suggest partial resistance, or very low numbers rather than true resistance. Neither antibiotic is equally effective against both species, so identification of the infecting species is essential before prescribing.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p1::10(b)(iii)": "Using aseptic technique: seed agar plates with a standardised bacterial lawn (known density). Prepare a range of antibiotic concentrations (e.g. 0.1, 0.5, 1, 5, 10, 50, 100 µg cm⁻³). Soak standard-sized paper discs in each concentration for a fixed time, or pipette fixed volumes into agar wells. Incubate all plates at the same temperature (e.g. 37 °C) for the same duration (e.g. 24 h). After incubation, measure the diameter of the zone of inhibition and calculate its area (πr²). Repeat with smaller concentration intervals around where the zone area begins to plateau. The optimum concentration is the lowest concentration at which the zone of inhibition does not increase further. Repeat each condition at least 3 times per antibiotic to calculate means. Include a distilled-water control disc.",

// ─── Edexcel A 2024 P2 — all questions ───────────────────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::1(a)(i)": "D — oxygen. In the glucose ring structure, atom R represents oxygen — it is the ring oxygen between C1 and C5 of the pyranose ring.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::1(a)(ii)": "A — CH₂OH. Group P on the glucose ring is the primary hydroxymethyl group (CH₂OH) at C6; this group is involved in glycosidic bond formation when glucose units are joined in polysaccharides.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::1(b)(i)": "Both PET and fMRI can assess brain activity in real time / produce live images of metabolically active regions. PET detects areas of high glucose uptake (labelled glucose) while fMRI detects blood-flow changes associated with neural activity. MRI provides structural images but not functional activity, so PET and fMRI are better for identifying tumours by their abnormal metabolic activity alongside showing which brain regions are functioning.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::1(b)(ii)": "Image 1 (CT) is less detailed / lower resolution than image 2 (MRI). CT uses X-rays and produces lower tissue contrast; MRI uses magnetic fields and radio waves, giving higher resolution with greater soft-tissue contrast.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::1(c)": "A tumour in region P (occipital/visual cortex) would cause inability to see or reduced visual perception. A tumour in region Q (cerebellum) would impair balance, coordination, fine motor skills, or posture.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::2(a)": "B — adenosine triphosphate (ATP). In glycolysis, ATP is consumed (to phosphorylate glucose and its derivatives) and later produced by substrate-level phosphorylation. The molecule X that shuttles phosphate groups in the conversion of glycerol-derived intermediates to pyruvate is ATP.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::2(b)": "During intense exercise, oxygen delivery to muscle cells may be insufficient for aerobic respiration alone. Under these anaerobic conditions, pyruvate (from glycolysis) is converted to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase. This reaction simultaneously oxidises reduced NAD (NADH + H⁺) back to NAD. Regenerated NAD is essential: it re-enters glycolysis as a hydrogen acceptor, allowing glycolysis to continue producing ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation. Without lactate production, all NAD would remain reduced, glycolysis would halt, and no further ATP would be produced. Converting pyruvate to lactate therefore allows continued (anaerobic) ATP production for muscle contraction when oxygen is limiting.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::2(c)": "Use the same volume of fresh and frozen liver extract in separate experiments. Add the same volume of DCPIP solution to each sample — DCPIP is blue and is decolourised by vitamin C. Titrate each extract into the DCPIP until it decolourises. Record the volume of each extract required. The solution requiring a smaller volume to decolourise the DCPIP has a higher vitamin C concentration. Repeat three times and calculate means. Use a known vitamin C standard to calculate exact concentration if needed.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::3(a)(i)": "Inhaled CO₂ raises blood [CO₂], forming carbonic acid that lowers blood pH. Central and peripheral chemoreceptors (medulla oblongata and aortic/carotid bodies) detect increased [CO₂] and decreased pH. They send more frequent impulses to the respiratory centre (medulla). More impulses travel via the phrenic and intercostal nerves to the diaphragm and intercostal muscles, increasing the rate and depth of contraction — rapid breathing — to expel CO₂ and restore blood pH.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::3(a)(ii)": "B — number of troughs on the trace in 15 seconds divided by 0.25. Each trough = one complete breath; counting over 15 s and dividing by 0.25 min converts to breaths per minute (e.g. 5 troughs in 15 s ÷ 0.25 = 20 breaths min⁻¹).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::3(b)": "Point K on the ECG is the QRS complex, representing ventricular systole. The AVN picks up the electrical impulse from the SAN after it has spread across the atria. The AVN introduces a brief delay (~0.1 s) before passing the impulse onward — this allows the atria to empty into the ventricles before they contract. The AVN then transmits the impulse along the bundle of His and into the Purkyne fibres, which carry depolarisation rapidly up from the ventricular apex, triggering coordinated ventricular contraction (the QRS complex).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::3(c)": "Chorionic villus sampling (CVS) can be performed earlier in pregnancy (10–14 weeks) compared with amniocentesis (15–20 weeks), so results are available sooner, giving more time to consider the outcome.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::4(a)": "Number of mutations per body cell per cell cycle = mutation rate × base pairs = (2.5 × 10⁻⁸) × (6 × 10⁹) = 150 mutations per cell cycle.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::4(b)(i)": "mRNA uses uracil (U) instead of thymine (T); in DNA the template strand has T where mRNA would have U. The anticodon is complementary to the mRNA codon, so it is the same as the DNA template strand sequence — except where the DNA template has T, the anticodon has U. Out of 12 bases in the four anticodons, 2 positions differ (U replaces T), so 10 of 12 are the same: (10 ÷ 12) × 100 = 83%.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::4(b)(ii)": "A DNA base change can alter the codon on mRNA, inserting a different amino acid (or a premature stop codon) into the polypeptide. A different primary structure means the R-group interactions (hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bridges) between amino acids may change, producing a different tertiary structure. If the active site of an enzyme changes shape, it is no longer complementary to the substrate — enzyme function is lost or altered. This changes the phenotype (e.g. a non-functional metabolic enzyme causes a different biochemical outcome, or a pigment protein is no longer produced).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::4(c)": "Fast twitch muscle fibres contract rapidly using anaerobic respiration (glycolysis) and have a relatively sparse blood supply. They therefore store more glycogen as a readily available local energy reserve: glycogen is rapidly hydrolysed to glucose for glycolysis, generating ATP quickly for powerful contractions. Slow twitch fibres rely mainly on aerobic respiration, supplied by a rich capillary network — they receive oxygen and glucose continuously from blood and need less internal glycogen storage.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::5(a)": "One cardiac cycle = 60 ÷ 90 = 0.667 s. Systole = 30% of cycle = 0.667 × 0.30 ≈ 0.2 s.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::5(b)(i)": "A 100% increased risk means the person is twice as likely to develop CVD as someone without high blood pressure — the risk is doubled.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::5(b)(ii)": "Two risks of antihypertensive treatment: (1) dizziness or fainting — the drug may lower blood pressure too much, reducing cerebral blood flow; (2) cough, headaches, fatigue, or oedema (swollen ankles) — common side effects of certain antihypertensive drug classes.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::5(b)(iii)": "Antihypertensives reduce blood pressure, decreasing mechanical stress on arterial walls. Lower pressure reduces the likelihood of endothelial damage. An intact endothelium prevents an inflammatory response and limits infiltration of LDL cholesterol into the arterial wall. Without this trigger, the formation of fatty streaks, atheromas, and plaques (atherosclerosis) is less likely. Without plaques, the lumen is not narrowed, reducing risk of thrombosis, myocardial infarction, and stroke — all forms of CVD.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::6(a)": "C — 0.50. Each non-identical twin's sex is determined independently: each has a 50% chance of being male or female. P(both same sex) = P(both male) + P(both female) = (0.5 × 0.5) + (0.5 × 0.5) = 0.50.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::6(b)": "Sperm adaptations for fertilisation: the acrosome contains hydrolytic enzymes (e.g. hyaluronidase) that digest the zona pellucida to enable fusion with the egg; the haploid nucleus (23 chromosomes) restores the diploid number (46) at fertilisation; numerous mitochondria in the midpiece generate ATP by aerobic respiration for flagellar movement; the flagellum propels the sperm through the female reproductive tract.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::6(c)(i)": "Totipotent stem cells can differentiate into all cell types, including extra-embryonic cells (e.g. placenta), and can give rise to a complete organism. Pluripotent stem cells can differentiate into most cell types (all three germ layers) but not extra-embryonic structures — they cannot alone form a complete organism. In totipotent cells no genes are permanently inactivated; in pluripotent cells some genes have been silenced by epigenetic changes.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::6(c)(ii)": "Centromeres keep sister chromatids joined throughout prophase and metaphase; spindle fibres attach to the kinetochore proteins at the centromere. During metaphase, centromeres align at the metaphase plate. During anaphase, centromeres split, and each sister chromatid (now a chromosome) is pulled to opposite poles by shortening spindle fibres, ensuring each daughter cell receives the correct chromosome number.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::7(a)": "The retina is a tissue because it is composed of a group of specialised cells — rod cells, cone cells, bipolar cells, ganglion cells — working together for a specific function: transduction of light energy into electrical impulses for vision.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::7(b)(i)": "In bright light, rhodopsin is bleached — it splits into retinal (trans form) and opsin. In the dark, rhodopsin reforms: trans-retinal is enzymatically converted to cis-retinal, which rejoins opsin to regenerate functional rhodopsin. In fundus albipunctatus, this regeneration is abnormally slow, reducing sensitivity to dim light after bright-light exposure.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::7(b)(ii)": "Pedigree 2. Only in pedigree 2 do both parents appear unaffected (heterozygous carriers of the recessive allele) yet have a child who shows the condition. This is the hallmark of autosomal recessive inheritance — unaffected carrier parents (Aa × Aa) have a 1 in 4 chance of producing a homozygous recessive (aa) offspring who expresses the condition.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::7(c)(i)": "The Y chromosome is smaller/shorter than the X chromosome and carries far fewer genes. The loci for the red and green pigment genes present on the X chromosome have no corresponding region on the Y chromosome.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::7(c)(ii)": "Total population = 131 ÷ 0.045 = 2911. Males in sample = 2911 × 0.55 ≈ 1601. Total colour-blind males = 2 + 91 + 25 = 118. Males with full colour vision = 1601 − 118 = 1483.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::7(c)(iii)": "Blue colour blindness (chromosome 7) is autosomal — both males and females need two recessive alleles to be colour blind, so it affects both sexes at the same (low) rate. Red and green pigment genes are X-linked: males (XY) express the condition with just one recessive allele on their X chromosome, while females (XX) need two recessive alleles. So red and green colour blindness is far more common in males (lower numbers of females affected). The overall lower numbers for blue blindness reflect that this allele is less common in the population.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::8(a)(i)": "D — rough endoplasmic reticulum. Ribosomes attached to the rough ER are where mRNA is translated into protein — the site of synthesis of secretory and membrane proteins such as the protein component of phytochrome.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::8(a)(ii)": "B — Golgi apparatus. Carbohydrate groups are added to proteins (glycosylation) in the Golgi apparatus. Proteins are transported from the rough ER in vesicles to the Golgi, where they are modified, sorted, and packaged.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::8(a)(iii)": "B — a peptide bond is a bond between a nitrogen atom (from the amino group, –NH₂) on one amino acid and a carbon atom (from the carboxyl group, –COOH) on another. A water molecule is released in this condensation reaction. The –CO–NH– linkage is the peptide bond.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::8(b)(i)": "D — slowly converted to phytochrome red. In the dark, phytochrome far-red (Pfr, the active form) spontaneously and slowly reverts to phytochrome red (Pr, the inactive form) — dark reversion. This allows plants to measure night length.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::8(b)(ii)": "In the light, Pr absorbs red light and is rapidly converted to Pfr (the active form). Pfr acts as or activates a transcription factor, entering the nucleus and stimulating expression of genes involved in growth. This causes cell elongation and stimulates mitosis/cell division, increasing plant growth. In the dark, Pfr slowly reverts to Pr and growth promotion declines.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::9(a)(i)": "Convert to the same units: liver TTX = 0.046 mg g⁻¹; skin TTX = 25 µg g⁻¹ = 0.025 mg g⁻¹. Percentage difference = [(0.046 − 0.025) ÷ 0.025] × 100 = 84%. The liver concentration is 84% higher than the skin concentration.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::9(a)(ii)": "Mice are mammals with similar physiology to humans, so they metabolise and respond to TTX in a comparable way. Testing in humans would be unethical and dangerous as TTX is potentially lethal with no antidote. Mice can be kept in controlled conditions, standardising variables such as body mass and genetics. Using inbred strains reduces biological variability and makes results more reproducible.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::9(a)(iii)": "Female pufferfish generally show higher overall toxicity than males. The difference is most pronounced in the liver and gonads (ovaries in females have much higher toxicity than testes). Muscle and skin TTX levels show little or no difference between sexes. This suggests TTX is sequestered in female reproductive organs — possibly to protect eggs.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::9(b)": "Conotoxin blocks calcium ion channels on presynaptic membranes. Ca²⁺ influx is required for vesicle fusion and neurotransmitter exocytosis. By blocking Ca²⁺ entry, conotoxin prevents neurotransmitter release at neuromuscular junctions, reducing postsynaptic stimulation and muscle contraction — supporting potential use as a treatment for uncontrolled muscle movement. TTX blocks voltage-gated sodium channels on axon membranes, preventing depolarisation and action potential propagation. Since pain signals travel as action potentials along sensory neurones, TTX could block pain transmission — supporting use as a painkiller. Limitations: the pufferfish study used only five individuals (one species), measured toxicity not therapeutic levels; mice may not perfectly model humans; blocking Na⁺ or Ca²⁺ channels has dangerous side effects (paralysis, cardiac arrest) at non-therapeutic doses, so extensive dose-finding and safety studies would be needed.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::10(a)": "A — 0.7 : 1. Endurance group: 27 athletes, 12 male → 15 female. Mix-trained: 31 athletes, 22 male → 9 female. Total female = 15 + 9 = 24; total male = 12 + 22 = 34. Ratio female : male = 24 : 34 ≈ 0.71 : 1, i.e. option A (0.7 : 1).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::10(b)(i)": "Eating or drinking could increase body mass, making sweat (water) loss appear smaller when mass change is used as the measure of sweating. Food or drink could also alter recorded core body temperature (particularly cold or hot drinks), introducing a confounding variable. Allowing food/drink during the investigation would reduce the validity of both the sweat-production and core-temperature measurements.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::10(b)(ii)": "During warm-up (0–20 min), core body temperature increased similarly at both 16 °C and 32 °C — environmental temperature had little effect on warm-up temperature rise. Muscle contraction generates metabolic heat regardless of ambient temperature, raising core temperature. During exercise (after 20 min), core temperature continued rising at both temperatures, but reached a higher maximum at 32 °C. At higher environmental temperature, the gradient between the body and its surroundings is smaller, so heat loss by radiation and convection is reduced, causing core temperature to rise higher.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p2::10(b)(iii)": "Evidence supports the benefit of temperature-specific training: exercise duration was greater at 16 °C (endurance: 64 vs 47 min; mix-trained: 57 vs 40 min) and maximum power output was higher at 16 °C for both groups, suggesting performance decreases at higher temperatures. Sweating rate was higher at 32 °C (endurance: 1.6 vs 0.9 kg h⁻¹), indicating greater thermoregulatory demand and potential dehydration risk. Training at different environmental temperatures could help athletes acclimatise and optimise performance. Limitations: only two temperatures tested — broader range needed to generalise; only two sport types studied with elite athletes — may not apply widely; humidity, airflow, and acclimatisation not controlled; no statistical analysis reported, so significance cannot be confirmed. Overall, evidence suggests temperature affects performance and acclimatisation training would be beneficial, but these limitations reduce certainty.",

// ─── Edexcel A 2024 P3 — all questions ───────────────────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::1(a)": "Colonisation is the process by which a species moves into and begins to live in a new area or habitat, establishing a population there for the first time.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::1(b)": "Light striking the shoot tip causes IAA (auxin) to diffuse away from the light source, accumulating on the shaded side of the shoot. IAA regulates the activity of transcription factors on the shaded side, stimulating gene expression that promotes cell elongation. Cells on the shaded side elongate more than those on the lit side, causing the shoot to bend toward the light — a positive phototropic response.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::1(c)(i)": "Using plants homozygous for either the normal or mutant tir1 allele ensures each group expresses only one version of the receptor. If heterozygous plants were used, they would carry both alleles; dominance or codominance could mask or dilute the effect of the mutant allele, making it impossible to determine the effect of the mutation alone. Homozygous plants ensure any difference in lateral root formation is attributable solely to the mutant or normal tir1 gene.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::1(c)(ii)": "Reading from the graph at 1.00 mol dm⁻³ IAA: normal tir1 ≈ 7 lateral roots mm⁻¹; mutant tir1 ≈ 4 lateral roots mm⁻¹. Percentage decrease = [(7 − 4) ÷ 7] × 100 ≈ 43%.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::1(c)(iii)": "The tir1 mutation reduces the plant's response to IAA: mutant plants produce fewer lateral roots at all IAA concentrations compared to normal plants. The mutated TIR1 receptor has reduced ability to bind IAA or transduce the signal, so lateral root stimulation is less effective. The response is not completely abolished (some lateral roots still form), suggesting the receptor is partially functional or alternative receptors exist.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::2(a)": "From the diagram, the clear zone for 80 µg licochalcone A has a diameter of approximately 19 mm. Area = π × (d/2)² = π × 9.5² ≈ 283–284 mm².",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::2(b)": "Wear disposable gloves throughout; wash hands before and after. Use sterilised forceps (flamed and cooled) to pick up and transfer each disc — re-sterilise between discs. Lift the Petri dish lid only slightly and for the minimum time possible to limit contamination risk. Work near a Bunsen burner to create an upward convection current, or inside a microbiological safety cabinet. After transferring discs, clean the workspace with disinfectant.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::2(c)": "An anomalous result can be identified by: (1) plotting results on a graph — any data point not fitting the trend / far from the line of best fit is potentially anomalous; (2) repeating the experiment — a result that differs markedly from other replicates (e.g. outside ±2 SD) can be identified as anomalous. An anomalous result should be investigated and the measurement repeated before exclusion from the mean.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::3(a)(i)": "The thylakoid membrane / grana. Light-dependent reactions occur on the thylakoid membranes within the grana of the chloroplast. (Not the thylakoid lumen.)",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::3(a)(ii)": "Gradient of herbicide A at 50% inhibition (from the graph): read two widely separated data points on the herbicide A line near 50% inhibition; gradient = Δy ÷ Δx ≈ 25–28 % inhibition per concentration unit (to 2 significant figures, as required).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::3(a)(iii)": "Chlorophyll absorbs light energy (primarily red and blue wavelengths). Absorbed photons excite electrons in the chlorophyll molecule to a higher energy level (photoactivation). These excited electrons are transferred to the electron transport chain (photoionisation), where their energy drives ATP production (photophosphorylation) and reduction of NADP to NADPH. Photolysis of water provides replacement electrons and releases O₂ as a by-product.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::3(b)": "RUBISCO (ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase) is an enzyme that catalyses carbon fixation in the Calvin cycle. It combines (fixes) CO₂ from the atmosphere with RuBP (a 5-carbon compound) to form an unstable 6-carbon intermediate that splits into two molecules of GP (glycerate-3-phosphate, a 3-carbon compound). GP is then reduced using ATP and NADPH (from the light-dependent reactions) to form G3P (glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate), used to synthesise glucose and regenerate RuBP.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::4(a)": "The humpback whale food chain has only 3 trophic levels (phytoplankton → krill → whale), while the great white shark chain has 4 (phytoplankton → zooplankton → fish → shark). Energy is lost at each trophic level through respiration, excretion, and faeces (~10% transfer efficiency). With fewer trophic levels, more of the original energy fixed by phytoplankton is available to support whale biomass. Therefore a given area of ocean supports greater biomass of humpback whales than great white sharks.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::4(b)(i)": "GPP (gross primary productivity) = NPP (net primary productivity) + R (respiration). NPP is the energy available to the next trophic level after the producer has used some in its own respiration.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::4(b)(ii)": "Set up a series of containers of phytoplankton culture at different temperatures (e.g. 5–40 °C in 5 °C steps). Control pH, light intensity, salinity, and phytoplankton species/volume. In transparent (light) containers, measure the increase in dissolved O₂ over a fixed time using an oxygen probe — this gives NPP. In opaque (dark) containers at the same temperature, measure O₂ decrease (respiration rate = R). GPP = NPP + R. Repeat at each temperature to calculate mean GPP values.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::4(c)(i)": "From the graph at 17 °C, the ratio of CO₂ produced to O₂ consumed ≈ 0.86 : 1 (allow 0.8–0.92 : 1).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::4(c)(ii)": "Two conclusions: (1) More oxygen is consumed and more CO₂ produced at 14 °C than at 17 °C, suggesting copepods have higher respiration rates at lower temperatures in this range. (2) The ratio of CO₂ produced to O₂ consumed is approximately 1, indicating aerobic respiration of carbohydrates (respiratory quotient ≈ 1 for carbohydrate).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::5(a)(i)": "gp120 binds to the CD4 receptor on T helper cells (and some macrophages), enabling the virus to attach to and enter the host cell — it is the viral attachment protein essential for tropism and cell entry.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::5(a)(ii)": "The lipid membrane surrounding the HIV particle is derived from the host cell's plasma membrane as the virus buds out. Host-cell surface proteins present in that plasma membrane are therefore incorporated into the viral lipid envelope during budding.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::5(b)": "HIV protease is an enzyme with an active site complementary to specific amino acid sequences (recognition sites) within the gag polyprotein. It catalyses hydrolysis of the peptide bonds at those recognition sites — adding water across each peptide bond — cleaving the polyprotein into individual functional proteins. This is a highly specific proteolytic cleavage: the protease only acts at defined sites within gag, analogous to restriction endonucleases cutting at specific sequences.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::5(c)(i)": "Reading from the graph for the inhibitor-resistant HIV protease at polyprotein concentration 7 mol dm⁻³: initial rate of reaction ≈ 2.8–3.0 mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ (value from the inhibitor-resistant line).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::5(c)(ii)": "Substrate concentration must be the same and in excess for all enzymes being compared, so that the substrate is not limiting and the enzyme works at or near Vmax. Otherwise, differences in rate would reflect differences in substrate availability rather than intrinsic enzyme activity. Enzyme concentrations must also be the same across comparisons — different amounts of enzyme would give different rates regardless of enzyme efficiency. Standardising both concentrations allows fair and valid comparison of intrinsic catalytic activity.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::6(a)(i)": "MDMA binds to the serotonin transporter on the presynaptic membrane, blocking serotonin reuptake (and causing reverse transport of serotonin out of the presynaptic terminal). Normally, serotonin is removed from the synaptic cleft by reuptake. MDMA prevents this, so serotonin accumulates in the cleft. More serotonin binds postsynaptic receptors, increasing the frequency of action potentials — the rate of transmission across serotonin synapses increases.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::6(a)(ii)": "MDMA increased button presses compared with the control (saline) button. Initially (day 1), mice pressed MDMA approximately 4 times more than control. After day 6, MDMA button presses increased further; by day 8–10, mice pressed MDMA approximately 7 times more than control, while control presses remained roughly constant. This suggests increasing preference for / addiction to MDMA over time.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::6(b)(i)": "Active transport is the movement of molecules or ions against a concentration gradient (from lower to higher concentration), requiring energy (ATP) and carrier/pump proteins.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::6(b)(ii)": "Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pumps actively transport Na⁺ out of brain cells, maintaining low intracellular [Na⁺]. This keeps the intracellular solute concentration relatively low (water potential relatively high). As long as the pump is active, water entry by osmosis is limited to the normal equilibrium. If blood solute concentration falls (water potential outside rises), the osmotic gradient driving water in is reduced. By actively maintaining low intracellular Na⁺, cells prevent excessive water entry and keep their volume stable.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::6(b)(iii)": "The skull is rigid — the space within it is fixed. When brain cells swell, they take up more of this limited space, physically compressing blood vessels (arterioles) and reducing the space available for them. This narrowing of arterioles is vasoconstriction, reducing blood flow through the brain.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::6(b)(iv)": "Vasoconstriction reduces blood flow to brain cells. Blood carries oxygen and glucose for aerobic respiration. With reduced blood flow, cells receive insufficient O₂ and glucose to produce ATP. Without ATP, cells cannot maintain essential processes (ion transport, membrane integrity). Aerobic respiration ceases, and brain cells die.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::7": "Cold treatments reduce nerve conduction and pain from muscle cramp by slowing action potential propagation. Ice massage and ice pack increased sensory-neurone latency and decreased amplitude and conduction velocity, meaning fewer pain impulses reach the brain — consistent with reduced pain perception. Cold-water immersion increased latency and decreased conduction velocity for both motor and sensory neurones — slowing motor-neurone conduction reduces the frequency of motor impulses reaching cramping muscles, decreasing sustained contraction. The prolonged CAP duration seen with cold-water immersion for motor neurones indicates delayed repolarisation, contributing to reduced sustained contraction. Ice-pack and ice-massage had smaller effects on motor neurones but comparable effects on sensory neurones to cold-water immersion, suggesting all three effectively reduce pain but cold-water immersion is most effective at reducing cramp by additionally impairing motor conduction. Limitations: the number of participants is not stated; the exact temperature of each treatment is not standardised; pain score was inferred from CAP data rather than measured directly; other variables (duration of treatment, muscle group) may not have been controlled. Overall, all three cold treatments reduce pain; cold-water immersion reduces both pain and motor activity most comprehensively.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::8(a)": "Eye lens crystallin proteins are present in all vertebrates and are easily obtained. The amino acid sequence of a protein is determined by the DNA sequence of its gene. Over evolutionary time, mutations accumulate in genes at approximately constant rates. Differences in amino acid sequence therefore reflect the time since two species shared a common ancestor — more diverged sequences indicate a longer period of separate evolution and therefore greater phylogenetic distance. Comparing lens protein sequences across sloth species allows construction of a phylogenetic tree showing relatedness.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::8(b)": "Collect algal samples from sloth hair. Extract DNA. Amplify a conserved gene region (e.g. rbcL or 18S rRNA) using PCR. Treat with restriction endonucleases to produce a characteristic fragment pattern. Separate fragments by gel electrophoresis and compare the banding pattern with reference patterns from known representatives of the four algal phyla — a matching pattern assigns the algae to the correct phylum. Alternatively, sequence the amplified gene and compare with a database of known algal sequences.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::8(c)": "Place the sloth in a sealed chamber connected to a spirometer containing a CO₂ absorber (e.g. soda lime). Control temperature. Record the spirometer trace — with CO₂ absorbed, the trace shows a net decline as O₂ is consumed. Calculate rate of O₂ consumption (cm³ per hour) from the slope of the trace. Divide by body mass (per kg) to normalise. Convert to energy: 1 dm³ O₂ ≈ 20 kJ, giving metabolic rate in kJ kg⁻¹ h⁻¹.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::8(d)": "Different viruses have different surface proteins (antigens). Antibodies are specific to antigens. If antibodies to a specific virus are found only in sloths (not in other species), this indicates only sloths have been infected by that virus — it is sloth-specific. If antibodies are also found in other species, those species are also infected, indicating the virus is incidental (not specific to sloths).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::8(e)": "Plant material contains cellulose, lignin, and other complex, hard-to-digest polymers. A slow digestive process gives more time for digestive enzymes to hydrolyse these polymers and for the gut epithelium to absorb the maximum possible nutrients from each meal. This maximises energy extraction from food — especially important for a low-energy-expenditure animal that feeds infrequently.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::8(f)": "Thermoregulation requires continuous energy expenditure: generating metabolic heat (shivering, increased respiration) or cooling the body (sweating, vasodilation) all require ATP. Sloths have a relatively large surface area to volume ratio and live in an upper canopy habitat with large temperature fluctuations. Maintaining a stable core temperature despite these fluctuations demands constant thermoregulatory effort. Since sloths have an extremely slow metabolic rate, the energy needed for thermoregulation represents a disproportionately large fraction of their total energy budget.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::8(g)": "Behavioural adaptation — moving higher into the canopy to expose themselves to solar radiation and warm up is a behavioural response to environmental temperature.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::8(h)": "Tendons are non-elastic and do not contract. A lattice of tendons holds the digits closed passively without requiring continuous muscle contraction. Muscle contraction requires ATP (from respiration), which is energetically costly. By using tendon tension instead of active muscle contraction, the sloth expends no metabolic energy to maintain its grip, allowing it to hang for hours without fatigue. Tendons also do not fatigue like muscles, enabling continuous grip.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::8(i)": "Sloth muscle enzymes have a different primary structure (different amino acid sequence) from human equivalents. This different sequence produces a different tertiary structure with different R-group interactions, giving the sloth enzymes an active site shape that remains functional at low pH (high lactic acid concentrations). Human muscle enzymes would be denatured or inhibited at the same pH; sloth enzymes are structurally stable over a broader pH range.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::8(j)": "A commensal community is one where the organisms (beetles, mites, moths) benefit from living on the host (the sloth) — gaining food, shelter, and protection — while the host is neither harmed nor helped. This is distinct from parasitism (where the host is harmed) or mutualism (where both partners benefit).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2024-p3::8(k)": "Sympatric speciation occurs within a single, geographically overlapping population without physical separation. Moths on sloths could speciate if subgroups became reproductively isolated through behavioural or ecological differences — for example, specialising to feed or mate on different body regions of the sloth, or at different times. Reduced interbreeding between subpopulations decreases gene flow. Each subpopulation accumulates different mutations through genetic drift and experiences different selection pressures, causing allele frequencies to diverge. Over many generations, the subpopulations become so genetically different that they can no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring — two separate species have formed.",

// ─── Edexcel A 2023 P1 — all questions ───────────────────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::1(a)": "C — a place where an organism lives. (A habitat is the physical location — the specific environment — where an organism naturally lives, feeds, and reproduces.)",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::1(b)(i)": "The amplified DNA samples are prepared for gel electrophoresis as follows: the DNA is cut into fragments using restriction endonucleases (restriction enzymes). Radioactive tracers or fluorescent tags (or a loading dye such as ethidium bromide) are added to the DNA. The labelled DNA fragments are loaded into wells in an agarose gel. An electric current is then applied — negatively charged DNA fragments migrate toward the positive electrode, separating by size. Smaller fragments travel further through the gel.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::1(b)(ii)": "After electrophoresis, the banding patterns for different Homo species are compared. If Homo longi is closely related to another species, their banding patterns will show a higher number of bands in common / bands at similar positions. Closely related species have more similar DNA sequences (fewer accumulated mutations), producing more similar fragment patterns after restriction digestion. More differences in band pattern indicate more distant evolutionary relationships.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::2(a)": "B — the number of different species and the number of different alleles. Biodiversity within a habitat includes species diversity (number and relative abundance of different species) and genetic diversity (variation in alleles within species).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::2(b)(i)": "For Ranunculus circinatus with n = 13: n(n−1) = 13 × 12 = 156. Total n for pond A = 136. Total n(n−1) = 3818.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::2(b)(ii)": "Diversity index D = N(N−1) ÷ Σn(n−1) = (156 × 155) ÷ 9110 = 24 180 ÷ 9110 ≈ 2.65. Pond B has a lower diversity index (2.65) than pond A (4.81), indicating lower biodiversity.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::2(b)(iii)": "The lower diversity index of pond B (2.65 vs 4.81 for pond A) shows that pond B has less biodiversity. Pollutants in pond B are toxic to many species, killing organisms that cannot tolerate the contamination, reducing species richness. Only species adapted to the polluted conditions can survive in pond B. With fewer competing species, the surviving species (e.g. tolerant algae or invertebrates) may have large populations — reducing species evenness. Overall, the pollutants have decreased biodiversity by reducing species richness and altering the relative abundance of remaining species.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::3(a)": "A DNA mononucleotide consists of: (1) a deoxyribose sugar (a pentose sugar with no hydroxyl group at the 2' carbon); (2) a phosphate group attached to the 5' carbon of the deoxyribose; (3) one of four nitrogenous bases (adenine, guanine, cytosine, or thymine) attached to the 1' carbon of the deoxyribose. Adjacent nucleotides in a DNA strand are joined by phosphodiester bonds between the 3' carbon of one deoxyribose and the 5' phosphate of the next.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::3(b)(i)": "B — both daughter DNA molecules contain one original DNA strand and one new DNA strand. This is the defining feature of semi-conservative replication: each of the two parental strands acts as a template for a new complementary strand, producing two hybrid double helices.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::3(b)(ii)": "DNA replication begins when DNA helicase breaks the hydrogen bonds between the complementary base pairs, separating the double helix into two template strands (unzipping). Free DNA nucleotides, present in the nucleus, align along each template strand by complementary base pairing (A pairs with T; C pairs with G). DNA polymerase catalyses the formation of phosphodiester bonds between adjacent nucleotides, synthesising a new complementary strand in the 5'→3' direction along each template. The result is two identical double-stranded DNA molecules, each containing one original strand and one newly synthesised strand — semi-conservative replication.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::4(a)(i)": "A — anaphase. In anaphase, the centromeres split and sister chromatids are pulled to opposite poles of the cell by shortening spindle fibres, making the chromatids visible as distinct chromosomes moving apart.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::4(a)(ii)": "To observe cells from other plant tissues undergoing mitosis: cut small sections of the tissue and treat with hydrochloric acid (to soften and separate the cells by breaking down the middle lamella). Heat the tissue briefly to further soften it. Stain with acetic orcein or toluidine blue (which binds to chromosomes, making them visible). Squash/macerate the tissue on a microscope slide to spread the cells out in a single layer. Apply a coverslip, observe under the light microscope at high magnification, and identify cells in different stages of mitosis.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::4(b)": "During prophase of mitosis: the chromosomes condense (coil and shorten, becoming visible under a light microscope). Centrioles (in animal cells) move to opposite poles of the cell. Spindle fibres form from the centrioles and extend toward the equator. The nucleolus breaks down and disappears. The nuclear envelope (nuclear membrane) disintegrates, releasing the chromosomes into the cytoplasm.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::5(a)(i)": "Seeds are preserved in seed banks by: surface-sterilising the seeds to remove pathogens; drying the seeds to reduce water content (low water content prevents ice crystal formation and slows metabolism); and storing them at low temperature (typically −20 °C / frozen) to further reduce metabolic activity and slow degradation, preserving viability for decades to centuries.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::5(a)(ii)": "Collecting seeds from multiple plants of each species ensures that the seed bank contains seeds from individuals with different genotypes, maintaining genetic diversity (a large gene pool) for the conserved species. A genetically diverse collection provides a greater chance that some individuals will survive and adapt if environmental conditions change when the seeds are eventually reintroduced. Reduced genetic diversity (a genetic bottleneck) could leave the species vulnerable to new diseases, environmental changes, or inability to adapt to different conditions in the future.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::5(b)(i)": "B — testing on animals, isolated cells and tissues. Before entering clinical trials, new drugs are tested on computer models (in silico), then on isolated cells and tissues (in vitro), and on animals (in vivo) to assess toxicity, efficacy, and metabolism.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::5(b)(ii)": "Clinical trials take place in three phases: Phase 1 — the drug is tested on a small number of healthy volunteers to assess safety, establish a safe dose range, and identify side effects (pharmacokinetics). Phase 2 — the drug is tested on a small group of patients who have the condition to determine whether the drug is effective and to assess further safety. Phase 3 — the drug is tested on a large group of patients (often thousands) in a randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled or active-comparator trial to confirm effectiveness, monitor side effects, and compare with existing treatments.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::5(c)": "Stomach acid (hydrochloric acid, HCl) creates a very low pH environment in the gut. This low pH denatures the enzymes of most bacteria and other microorganisms, destroying their function and killing or inhibiting the pathogen. Additionally, normal gut flora (e.g. Lactobacillus) occupy the gut environment, competing with pathogens for space and nutrients, and may produce lactic acid and other antimicrobial compounds. Together, these mechanisms prevent infection by most microorganisms that are ingested.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::6(a)": "D — the role of an organism in its environment. A niche includes everything about how an organism interacts with its habitat: what it eats, when it feeds, where it shelters, how it reproduces, and its interactions with other species. No two species can occupy exactly the same niche in the same habitat (competitive exclusion principle).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::6(b)(i)": "Shrews have a very large number of alveoli providing a very large surface area for gas exchange. The walls of the alveoli are just one cell thick, giving a very short diffusion distance (pathway) for O₂ and CO₂. A dense network of capillaries surrounds the alveoli, maintaining a steep concentration gradient for O₂ (by carrying oxygenated blood away rapidly). Together, these features maximise the rate of diffusion of oxygen into the blood, enabling the rapid oxygen delivery needed by the very metabolically active shrew to maintain its high body temperature.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::6(b)(ii)": "A mutation in the gene coding for myoglobin produced a variant with a different (altered) tertiary structure. A selection pressure — such as food availability in cold-water streams (a rich supply of aquatic insects) or periodic flooding of terrestrial habitats — favoured shrews able to dive and feed in water. Shrews with the mutant myoglobin that can store more oxygen in muscles gained a survival advantage: they could dive for longer, obtaining more food. These individuals were more likely to survive and reproduce, passing the advantageous allele to their offspring. Over many generations, the frequency of the mutant myoglobin allele increased in semi-aquatic shrew populations through natural selection.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::6(b)(iii)": "A change in the base sequence of the myoglobin gene alters the codon sequence on the mRNA transcribed from it. This causes a different amino acid (with a different R group) to be incorporated at one or more positions in the polypeptide, changing the primary structure. Because tertiary structure is determined by interactions between R groups (hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bridges, van der Waals forces), incorporating different R groups changes which bonds form and how the polypeptide folds, resulting in a different tertiary structure for the myoglobin protein.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::7(a)": "D — plasmid, 70S ribosome, circular DNA. These three features are found exclusively in prokaryotes: plasmids are small, circular, extra-chromosomal DNA molecules; 70S ribosomes are smaller than eukaryotic 80S ribosomes; and the main chromosome of prokaryotes is circular (not linear). Eukaryotes have 80S ribosomes, linear chromosomes in a membrane-bound nucleus, and no plasmids (except under some circumstances).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::7(b)(i)": "A bactericidal antibiotic is a substance that kills bacteria (destroys them outright, rather than merely inhibiting their growth).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::7(b)(ii)": "Mean rate of increase = (16 000 − 8000) ÷ 2 h = 8000 ÷ 2 = 4000 bacteria per hour = 4.0 × 10³ per hour.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::7(b)(iii)": "When bacteria infect tissue, they trigger the inflammatory response. Damaged cells and mast cells release histamine. Histamine causes vasodilation of local arterioles, increasing blood flow to the affected area (causing redness and heat). Capillaries become more permeable, allowing plasma and white blood cells (phagocytes — neutrophils and macrophages) to leave the blood and enter the infected tissue. Phagocytes engulf bacteria by phagocytosis (endocytosis), fusing lysosomes with the phagosome to digest the pathogens with hydrolytic enzymes. This localised response limits the spread of infection and begins to eliminate the bacteria.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::7(c)": "Bacteria develop resistance to antibiotics through several mechanisms. (1) Enzyme-mediated destruction — some bacteria produce enzymes (e.g. β-lactamase) that destroy the antibiotic before it can act; this is a biochemical shielding mechanism. (2) Reduced uptake — mutations in outer membrane proteins reduce the number of porins, limiting how much antibiotic enters the cell. (3) Efflux pumps — bacteria activate transport proteins that actively pump the antibiotic out of the cell before it reaches its target. (4) Target site modification — mutations change the structure of the antibiotic's target (e.g. altered ribosomal proteins for aminoglycosides), so the antibiotic no longer binds effectively. These mechanisms spread through bacterial populations by natural selection (antibiotic use is a selection pressure — only resistant bacteria survive and reproduce) and can transfer between bacteria via conjugation (plasmid exchange). Codes of practice to slow resistance include: use of antibiotics only when necessary and for the correct duration; prescribing the specific antibiotic matched to the infecting species; rotating antibiotic classes; strict hygiene protocols in hospitals to prevent spread; screening and isolating infected patients; and programmes to reduce antibiotic use in agriculture.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::8(a)": "B — found in only one geographical area. An endemic species is one whose natural range is restricted to a single geographical area or region and is found nowhere else in the world naturally.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::8(b)": "D — 9.0 × 10⁷ tonnes year⁻¹. Seagrass stores 15 × 5 = 75 tonnes carbon ha⁻¹ yr⁻¹. Area = 1.2 × 10⁶ ha. Total = 75 × 1.2 × 10⁶ = 9.0 × 10⁷ tonnes year⁻¹.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::8(c)(i)": "Increased greenhouse gases (CO₂, CH₄) trap more heat in the atmosphere, raising global temperatures including sea-water temperature. CO₂ also dissolves in sea water, forming carbonic acid (H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻), increasing the acidity (lowering pH) of the water. Seagrass cannot tolerate warmer or more acidic conditions. Enzymes in seagrass cells have an optimum pH and temperature — deviating from the optimum reduces enzyme activity (denaturing active sites or altering ionisation of R groups). With impaired enzyme function, photosynthesis, growth, and cell division slow or stop, reducing seagrass survival.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::8(c)(ii)": "A reduction in seagrass would decrease the supply of food for primary consumers (herbivores) that depend on seagrass as their diet. This would reduce the population of herbivores, cascading up the food chain to reduce populations of secondary and tertiary consumers. Less seagrass means less photosynthesis, reducing dissolved oxygen levels in the water — aquatic animals that rely on dissolved oxygen for aerobic respiration would suffocate. Loss of seagrass also destroys habitat structure that provides shelter and breeding grounds for many species. Overall, biodiversity in the Mediterranean would decline as food chains and food webs are disrupted.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::8(c)(iii)": "Seagrass absorbs large quantities of CO₂ from the water (and indirectly the atmosphere) during photosynthesis in the Calvin cycle, incorporating the carbon into organic molecules in its biomass. Because seagrass is long-lived and more resistant to breakdown by microorganisms (decomposers) than terrestrial plant material, the organic carbon remains stored in seagrass tissues and sediments for longer — it is not released back as CO₂ through decomposer respiration. Maintaining a healthy seagrass population therefore keeps large amounts of carbon locked in biological material (a carbon sink), reducing the net release of CO₂ into the atmosphere.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::9(a)(i)": "C — ZᵇW. Head colour is Z-linked (X-linked equivalent in birds). A black-headed female needs the recessive allele on her only Z chromosome (females are ZW). Genotype: ZᵇW. (She cannot be ZᵇZᵇ as females are ZW, and ZᴮWᵇ is incorrect notation as no alleles exist on the W chromosome.)",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::9(a)(ii)": "Cross: bar-headed female (ZᴮW) × black-headed male (ZᵇZᵇ). Gametes: female produces Zᴮ and W; male produces Zᵇ and Zᵇ. Offspring: ZᴮZᵇ (bar-headed males) and ZᵇW (black-headed females) — 1:1 ratio. All males are bar-headed (heterozygous); all females are black-headed.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::9(a)(iii)": "The males from the cross in (a)(ii) are all ZᴮZᵇ — heterozygous, carrying one dominant (bar) allele and one recessive (black) allele. When these males are crossed with a bar-headed female (ZᴮW), the male produces two types of Z-chromosome gametes: Zᴮ and Zᵇ. Females inherit only one Z chromosome (the other sex chromosome is W, carrying no allele for head colour). So females that receive Zᴮ from the male will be ZᴮW (bar-headed), and females that receive Zᵇ will be ZᵇW (black-headed). Because the male is heterozygous, he passes on each allele with equal probability, producing both bar-headed and black-headed female offspring.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::9(b)": "One gene can give rise to more than one protein through alternative splicing. The pre-mRNA transcript from the gene contains both introns (non-coding sequences) and exons (coding sequences). Introns are always removed, but exons can be joined in different combinations (alternative splicing) — different exons are included or excluded from the mature mRNA. Each different mRNA sequence is translated into a different polypeptide with a different amino acid sequence. These different polypeptides may fold into different proteins with different functions, all encoded by the same gene.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::10(a)": "Draw a trend line through the data points on the graph showing first date of oak leaf appearance vs year. Extrapolate the trend line to 2030. Reading from the extrapolated line: oak leaves are predicted to first appear in approximately late March or early April 2030 (the exact date depends on the graph gradient — typically a few days earlier per decade).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::10(b)": "Specialist butterfly species feed on only one plant species — oak trees in this case. If spring temperatures change, the timing of oak leaf emergence may shift (leaves appear earlier). If the butterfly's life cycle does not shift at the same rate (a mismatch in phenology), larvae may hatch before or after peak leaf availability, leaving them with little or no suitable food. Generalist species can switch to alternative food plants if their primary food is unavailable or mistimed, so they are more resilient to phenological mismatches. Additionally, other species may compete with the specialist for the limited food resource, further disadvantaging it.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::10(c)(i)": "The butterfly life cycle depends on metabolic processes (growth, development, moulting). An increase in environmental temperature increases the kinetic energy of enzyme and substrate molecules, increasing the frequency of successful collisions between enzymes and substrates, accelerating the rate of enzyme-catalysed reactions (respiration, biosynthesis). Higher metabolic rates mean growth and development proceed faster, shortening the time spent in each life stage and therefore shortening the overall life cycle.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p1::10(c)(ii)": "Independent variable: temperature (a range of at least 5 temperatures, e.g. 10, 15, 20, 25, 30 °C). Dependent variable: duration of the larval stage (time from egg hatching to pupation, measured in days). Control variables: same specialist butterfly species; eggs/larvae of the same age and source; same food plant and quantity of food; same humidity; temperature-controlled incubators or water baths. Procedure: place several larvae (replicates) of each temperature at each condition simultaneously; monitor daily; record the date each larva pupates; calculate the mean larval duration at each temperature. Repeat for at least three replicates per temperature to reduce random error. Plot mean larval duration against temperature; the optimum temperature is where larval duration is shortest. Use appropriate statistical tests (e.g. Spearman's rank correlation or t-test for two temperatures) to assess significance.",

// ─── Edexcel A 2023 P2 — all questions ───────────────────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::1(a)(i)": "C — metaphase. Spindle fibres attach to centromeres (kinetochore proteins) during metaphase, aligning chromosomes at the metaphase plate (cell equator).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::1(a)(ii)": "C — prophase. The nuclear membrane (nuclear envelope) begins to break down during prophase as chromosomes condense and the spindle begins to form.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::1(a)(iii)": "C — prophase. During prophase, chromosomes condense and coil sufficiently to become visible as distinct chromatids when viewed with a light microscope.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::1(b)": "Crossing over occurs during prophase I of meiosis. Non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes wind around each other and break at corresponding points (chiasmata), exchanging equivalent segments of DNA between a maternal and a paternal chromosome. This results in recombinant chromatids that carry new combinations of alleles not present on either original parental chromosome. Because many different chiasmata can form at different positions along homologous chromosomes, crossing over generates a vast number of new allele combinations, increasing genetic variation among gametes and therefore among offspring.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::2(a)": "D — the sugar in both eukaryotic and prokaryotic DNA is deoxyribose. Both types of organism use DNA as their genetic material, built from deoxyribonucleotides containing deoxyribose. Uracil replaces thymine only in RNA, not DNA; prokaryotic DNA is circular (not linear); and prokaryotic DNA is not in a membrane-bound nucleus.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::2(b)(i)": "An operon is a group of genes (a section of DNA) in a prokaryote that are regulated by a single operator/promoter region and are transcribed together as a single mRNA molecule. This allows coordinated regulation of multiple genes involved in the same metabolic pathway.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::2(b)(ii)": "In the absence of lactose, a repressor protein binds to the operator region of the lac operon, blocking RNA polymerase from binding to the promoter, preventing transcription of the structural genes. When lactose is present, it (or its metabolite, allolactose) binds to the repressor protein, changing its shape (allosteric change). The repressor can no longer bind to the operator. RNA polymerase binds to the promoter and transcribes the structural genes (lacZ, lacY, lacA), producing mRNA that is translated into enzymes including β-galactosidase (lactase) to metabolise lactose.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::3(a)": "A — elastic and attaches bone to bone. Ligaments connect bone to bone at joints, and are elastic — they allow movement but return to their original length after stretching, contributing to joint stability. (Tendons connect muscle to bone and are non-elastic.)",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::3(b)": "The percentage of people having ACL surgery varied by age group. The 20–29 age group had the highest percentage of ACL surgeries throughout 2013–2018 — likely reflecting high participation in sport at this age. The percentage decreased with increasing age from the 20–29 group onwards: the 50+ age group had the smallest percentage. Within each age group, the percentage remained relatively stable from 2013 to 2018 — although the 10–19 age group showed the greatest variation year-to-year. Overall, younger adults (20–29) were most likely to require ACL surgery, probably due to higher levels of physical activity.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::3(c)(i)": "Ratio of wound infections to open surgeries = 68 ÷ 13 472 = 0.005 : 1 (to 1 significant figure).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::3(c)(ii)": "Open surgery involves a larger incision and a longer wound-opening time than keyhole surgery. The larger opening exposes the internal tissues to the environment for longer, providing more opportunity for pathogens (bacteria) to enter the wound and cause infection. Keyhole surgery uses small incisions and instruments, minimising the time and area of exposure, thereby reducing the chance of bacterial contamination and wound infection.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::4(a)": "Similarities: both PGD and amniocentesis can detect genetic conditions in embryos/foetuses, allowing parents to make informed reproductive decisions about whether to continue with a pregnancy. Differences: PGD is performed before implantation — embryos created by IVF are tested at the 8-cell stage, and only unaffected embryos are implanted — so no risk of miscarriage; amniocentesis is performed after implantation (at 15–20 weeks), sampling amniotic fluid containing foetal cells, and carries a small but real risk of miscarriage (~0.5–1%). PGD tests embryonic cells; amniocentesis tests amniotic fluid. PGD is earlier in the process; amniocentesis provides results later in pregnancy.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::4(b)": "Two social issues with PGD: (1) selecting one embryo over others involves discarding surplus embryos — some view this as the destruction of potential human life, raising ethical and religious objections. (2) PGD used to select against a specific genetic condition could be extended to select for other non-medical traits (e.g. sex, intelligence, physical attributes) — often called 'designer babies' — raising concerns about eugenics and discrimination against people with genetic conditions.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::4(c)": "Cystic fibrosis results from mutations in the CFTR gene encoding a chloride ion channel in epithelial cell membranes. Without functional CFTR, chloride ions accumulate inside cells, water follows by osmosis into cells (instead of into the gut lumen), producing thick, viscous mucus in the gut. This mucus blocks the pancreatic duct, preventing pancreatic enzymes (lipase, amylase, protease) from reaching the small intestine. Without these digestive enzymes, food is not broken down efficiently — fats, proteins, and carbohydrates are poorly digested and absorbed, leading to malnutrition and fatty stools.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::5(a)": "Actual bacterium width = image width ÷ magnification = 10 mm ÷ 20 000 = 0.0005 mm = 0.5 µm. DNA width = bacterium width ÷ 250 = 0.5 ÷ 250 = 0.002 µm = 2.0 × 10⁻³ µm.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::5(b)(i)": "Conservative replication: after the 1st replication, one DNA double helix contains both original ¹⁵N strands (all-heavy) and one contains both new ¹⁴N strands (all-light). After the 2nd replication: 1 all-heavy + 3 all-light helices. Semi-conservative replication: after the 1st replication, both double helices are hybrid (one ¹⁵N strand + one ¹⁴N strand). After the 2nd replication: 2 hybrid (¹⁵N/¹⁴N) + 2 all-light (¹⁴N/¹⁴N) helices.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::5(b)(ii)": "Semi-conservative DNA replication means that when a DNA double helix replicates, each of the two resulting daughter molecules retains one original (parental) strand and one newly synthesised strand. Each original strand acts as a template — free nucleotides align by complementary base pairing and are joined by DNA polymerase — resulting in two identical double helices, each 'half-old, half-new'.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::5(c)": "E. coli-STEC produces a toxin that reduces blood platelet concentration. Platelets are essential for blood clotting: when a vessel is damaged, platelets release thromboplastin (thrombokinase), which triggers the clotting cascade — converting prothrombin to thrombin (with Ca²⁺), which in turn converts soluble fibrinogen to insoluble fibrin. With fewer platelets, less thromboplastin is released, so less prothrombin is converted to thrombin, and less fibrinogen is converted to fibrin. Additionally, fewer platelets means a smaller platelet plug forms at the wound site. Both effects prolong the time taken for a stable blood clot to form.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::6(a)(i)": "Process J is glycolysis. Products of glycolysis (other than the 3-carbon pyruvate): reduced NAD (NADH + H⁺) and ATP (net 2 ATP per glucose by substrate-level phosphorylation).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::6(a)(ii)": "Process K is the link reaction (conversion of pyruvate to acetyl CoA). If process K stopped (e.g. due to anaerobic conditions), pyruvate would not enter the mitochondrial matrix. Instead, the 3-carbon pyruvate would be reduced to lactate (lactic acid) by lactate dehydrogenase in the cytoplasm, using reduced NAD to regenerate NAD so that glycolysis can continue.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::6(a)(iii)": "D — 6C → 5C → 4C. Process L is the Krebs cycle. In the Krebs cycle, the 2-carbon acetyl group (from acetyl CoA) combines with a 4-carbon oxaloacetate to form a 6-carbon citrate; CO₂ is released to give 5-carbon and then 4-carbon intermediates, regenerating oxaloacetate. The carbon count goes 6 → 5 → 4 (as CO₂ is released).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::6(b)": "During aerobic respiration by the maggots, oxygen is consumed continuously. The potassium hydroxide (KOH) solution absorbs the carbon dioxide produced by the maggots. Therefore, net gas is removed from the sealed tube (O₂ consumed; CO₂ absorbed) — the total volume of gas in the tube decreases. This decrease in gas volume reduces the pressure in the tube, causing the coloured liquid in the manometer to move toward the respirometer (to the left), toward the lower-pressure side.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::6(c)": "To investigate the optimum temperature for respiration in maggots using a respirometer: 1. Set up at least 5 temperatures (e.g. 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40 °C) using water baths or temperature-controlled incubators. 2. Use maggots of the same species, similar size/age, and same number per respirometer. 3. Allow maggots to acclimatise at each temperature for at least 5 minutes before recording. 4. Include soda lime (to absorb CO₂) in the respirometer chamber. 5. Record the distance the coloured liquid moves in a fixed time (e.g. 5 min) at each temperature — this measures the rate of O₂ consumption. 6. Repeat at least 3 times at each temperature to calculate a mean rate. 7. Plot mean rate of O₂ consumption against temperature; the temperature with the highest mean rate is the optimum.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::7(a)": "Long-distance runners benefit from a predominance of type I (slow-twitch) muscle fibres: these are fatigue-resistant (using aerobic respiration — high mitochondria content and oxygen supply via myoglobin), allowing sustained contraction over long durations without accumulating lactic acid. Low anaerobic respiration means a smaller oxygen debt and less fatigue. However, having some type II (fast-twitch) fibres as well is advantageous: type II fibres contract four times faster and allow the runner to accelerate, sprint past competitors, or kick at the finish — capabilities that pure type I muscles cannot provide. Having type II fibres in smaller proportion provides this burst capacity without compromising overall endurance.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::7(b)(i)": "A — none. All three statements are incorrect: ligase joins DNA fragments (does not cut); restriction enzymes cut DNA (do not add genes); and the vector (not the functional gene) is used to carry the gene into the cell. The functional gene is the insert, not the vector.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::7(b)(ii)": "B — 4. EcoR1 makes staggered cuts at two specific points in the double-stranded DNA. To cut out a fragment, two cuts are needed (one on each strand at each end of the fragment), but each restriction cut hydrolyses one phosphodiester bond per strand. For a fragment cut from a linear molecule, 2 cuts × 2 strands = 4 phosphodiester bonds are hydrolysed in total.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::7(b)(iii)": "Gene therapy using a modified virus treats the cells of the eye (somatic cells) only — it does not alter the DNA of gametes (sperm or eggs). The person treated still carries the recessive allele for the condition in their germline (gametes). When they reproduce, their gametes carry this recessive allele. If their partner is also a carrier of the recessive allele, there is a 1 in 4 chance that a child inherits both recessive alleles (homozygous recessive) and therefore has the condition. Additionally, a new mutation in the offspring's own genes could produce the same condition.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::7(c)": "A — differences in the genome between people. Personalised medicine exploits the fact that individuals differ in their genetic sequence (single nucleotide polymorphisms, different alleles). These differences affect how people metabolise drugs (pharmacogenomics), how receptors respond, and which diseases they are susceptible to. By sequencing a patient's genome, clinicians can select drugs and doses most likely to be effective and least likely to cause adverse reactions for that individual.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::8(a)": "ATP provides the energy for the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump in the axon membrane. This pump actively transports 3 Na⁺ out of the axon and 2 K⁺ into the axon per ATP hydrolysed, maintaining the electrochemical gradient across the membrane — 3 Na⁺ outside high, K⁺ inside high. This gradient is essential for establishing and restoring the resting potential (approximately −70 mV) after each action potential, allowing the axon to repolarise and be ready to transmit the next impulse.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::8(b)(i)": "The myosin head on the mitochondrion binds to the myosin-binding site on the actin filament running along the axon. ATP is hydrolysed by myosin (which acts as an ATPase), causing the myosin head to change its orientation/position — it pivots (the power stroke), pulling the mitochondrion along the actin track. The myosin head then detaches from actin, reattaches to the next binding site further along the filament, and repeats, progressively moving the mitochondrion along the axon.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::8(b)(ii)": "Axon length = 9 cm = 90 000 µm. Speed = 0.5 µm s⁻¹. Time = 90 000 ÷ 0.5 = 180 000 s. Converting to hours: 180 000 ÷ 3600 = 50 hours.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::8(c)": "Motor neurones (50% lipid, 40% protein) and Schwann cells (70% lipid, 30% protein) both contain more lipid than protein, reflecting the phospholipid bilayer that forms the foundation of both membranes. The higher protein content of motor neurones is functionally important: channel proteins (voltage-gated Na⁺ and K⁺ channels) are essential for generating and propagating action potentials; carrier proteins and Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pumps maintain the resting potential; receptor proteins bind neurotransmitters at synapses. Without these proteins, electrical signalling would be impossible. The higher lipid content of Schwann cells reflects the myelin sheath — multiple layers of Schwann cell membrane wrapped around the axon. The high lipid content of myelin provides electrical insulation, preventing ion leakage across the membrane between nodes of Ranvier. This forces depolarisation to jump between nodes (saltatory conduction), vastly increasing impulse speed and reducing ATP expenditure. Both cell types require their specific lipid:protein ratios to fulfil their distinct functions: motor neurones rely on proteins for signalling, Schwann cells rely on lipids for insulation.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::9(a)(i)": "C — 9. From the pedigree, the genotypes of 9 family members can be determined. Individuals showing the condition and known carriers (e.g. parents of affected offspring who must carry the dominant allele) can be assigned genotypes. Only one individual whose genotype cannot be inferred from the pedigree alone remains ambiguous.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::9(a)(ii)": "The gene mutation in retinitis pigmentosa changes the DNA base sequence, altering codons in the mRNA. This changes the amino acid sequence (primary structure) of opsin, the protein component of rhodopsin. A different primary structure changes the R-group interactions (hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bridges) within the polypeptide, producing a different tertiary structure. The altered tertiary structure of opsin means it binds retinal less effectively, producing fewer functional rhodopsin molecules. The absorbance at 500 nm (retinal absorption) is therefore lower relative to 280 nm (protein absorption), explaining the higher 280:500 ratio in people with RP.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::9(b)": "In the dark, sodium ion channels in the outer segment of the rod cell are open; Na⁺ flows in continuously (the dark current), partially depolarising the rod cell. The rod cell releases the neurotransmitter glutamate continuously into the synapse with the bipolar neurone. Glutamate inhibits depolarisation of the bipolar neurone (via inhibitory receptors), preventing it from firing. In the light, rhodopsin is activated, triggering a cascade that closes the sodium channels. Na⁺ can no longer flow in; the rod cell becomes more negative (hyperpolarises). Glutamate release stops. With no inhibitory glutamate, the bipolar neurone is freed from inhibition and depolarises — generating an action potential that is transmitted to the brain via the optic nerve.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::9(c)(i)": "In long days (short nights), the period of red light exposure is long. Phytochrome red (Pr) is continuously converted to phytochrome far-red (Pfr, the active form) during the long day. During the short night, there is insufficient time for all Pfr to revert back to Pr. High concentrations of Pfr therefore accumulate. Pfr stimulates (directly or via a signal cascade) the production of a flowering stimulus (florigen), triggering flowering in long-day plants.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::9(c)(ii)": "To determine whether a plant species is a long-day or short-day plant: grow multiple groups of plants of the same species and size under different photoperiods (day length) — for example, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16 hours of light per 24-hour cycle. Control all other variables (temperature, light intensity, water, nutrients). Record whether each group flowers or not. If the plants flower when day length is long (e.g. ≥14 h) and not when it is short — they are long-day plants. If they flower when day length is short (e.g. ≤10 h) and not when long — they are short-day plants.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::10(a)(i)": "Unsaturated fat = total fat − saturated fat = 7.6 − 4.7 = 2.9 g. Percentage unsaturated = (2.9 ÷ 7.6) × 100 = 38%.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::10(a)(ii)": "Sugar per biscuit = 20.3 ÷ 4 = 5.075 g. Number of biscuits to exceed 90 g = 90 ÷ 5.075 = 17.7 → 18 biscuits (you need to eat the 18th to exceed the RI).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::10(a)(iii)": "700 kJ = 8% of daily recommended intake. Daily RI = 700 ÷ 0.08 = 8750 kJ = 8 750 000 J = 8.75 × 10⁶ J.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::10(b)(i)": "Both increasing age and increasing BMI increase the incidence of CHD in women. The increase due to age is greater — women in the 70–74 age group have a much higher incidence of CHD than those in the 55–59 group at any given BMI. Within each age group, incidence increases with increasing BMI, but the effect of BMI is smaller than that of age.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p2::10(b)(ii)": "Evaluating the factors most likely to increase CHD risk: From the table, both increasing BMI and increasing age raise CHD incidence, with age showing the largest effect. From the graphs: smoking is associated with the greatest relative increase in CHD likelihood across all BMI values — even at the lowest BMI, smokers have an approximately doubled CHD risk compared to non-smokers. Physical inactivity is associated with increased CHD risk, but the effect appears smaller than that of smoking across BMI ranges. Alcohol intake shows a less consistent pattern — moderate consumption may be associated with lower risk, while high intake raises it. Study limitations: the cohort is only women aged 55–74, limiting generalisability; the table data covers only a 5-year incidence while graphs cover 20 years (different time frames); data may be self-reported (subject to bias); age is not shown in the lifestyle graphs. Conclusion: smoking appears to be the strongest modifiable lifestyle risk factor for CHD in this study, followed by physical inactivity. Age (non-modifiable) is also a major factor, and BMI modification alongside smoking cessation would have the greatest impact on reducing CHD risk.",

// ─── Edexcel A 2023 P3 — all questions ───────────────────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::1(a)": "Animals such as humans are too large for diffusion alone to supply all cells with oxygen and nutrients, or to remove waste products quickly enough. Many cells are far from the exchange surface (e.g. lungs, gut). A circulatory system with a pump (heart) provides mass transport — blood carries oxygen, glucose, hormones, and waste products rapidly over long distances to every tissue, overcoming the limitations of diffusion across large distances.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::1(b)(i)": "The heart is myogenic — cardiac muscle can generate its own electrical impulses (action potentials) independently of the nervous system. Even when removed from the body, it continues to beat autonomously for a period because the sinoatrial node (SAN) in the right atrial wall generates its own rhythm without external stimulation.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::1(b)(ii)": "The sinoatrial node (SAN), which acts as the pacemaker, is located in the right atrium. When the left atrium is separated from the right atrium and SAN, it no longer receives electrical impulses from the SAN. Because the left atrium cannot generate its own pacemaker impulses, it stops contracting.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::1(b)(iii)": "When the heart is removed from the body, it no longer receives a blood supply. Without blood flow, heart muscle cells run out of oxygen. Aerobic respiration ceases — the Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation stop — and ATP production falls. Without ATP, the cardiac muscle cells can no longer contract or maintain their ion gradients, so they eventually stop beating. Anaerobic respiration produces some lactate/lactic acid initially but cannot sustain contraction for long.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::2(a)": "Volume of a neutrophil (r = 5 µm): V = (4/3) × π × 5³ = (4/3) × π × 125 = (4/3) × 392.7 ≈ 524 µm³.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::2(b)(i)": "Phagocytosis is the engulfing/ingesting of a pathogen (bacteria, fungi, viruses, cell debris) by a cell via endocytosis — the cell membrane extends around the pathogen, enclosing it in a phagosome.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::2(b)(ii)": "Monocytes (and the macrophages they become) phagocytose and digest viruses/pathogens. Crucially, macrophages then present viral antigens on their surface (MHC class II) to T helper cells — acting as antigen-presenting cells (APCs). They also produce cytokines that activate B cells and T cells, initiating the specific immune response. Neutrophils cannot present antigens and do not produce cytokines — so they cannot trigger the antigen-specific (adaptive) immune response. Therefore only monocytes/macrophages play a role in the antigen-specific response to viruses.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::3(a)": "Length of the stickleback = measured image length ÷ magnification. If the image length from A to B ≈ 108 mm, then actual length = 108 ÷ 1.8 = 60 mm (to 2 significant figures).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::3(b)(i)": "Number of bites at 1 min ≈ 9.6; at 6 min ≈ 2.0. Percentage decrease = [(9.6 − 2.0) ÷ 9.6] × 100 = (7.6 ÷ 9.6) × 100 ≈ 79%.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::3(b)(ii)": "At the start of the investigation, the stickleback bit frequently at the tube containing brine shrimps, driven by the stimulus of the moving prey. However, the stickleback was unable to reach the shrimps through the glass tube — the bite response was never rewarded with food. Over time, the number of bites decreased because the stickleback became habituated to the stimulus: it learned (through repeated unrewarded responses) that biting at this stimulus is ineffective and a waste of energy. By 6 minutes, the stickleback had mostly stopped responding, conserving energy.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::3(c)": "To investigate the effect of a competing male on the attack response: place a breeding male stickleback (with a red throat) in a tank and allow it to acclimatise (at least 30 min, controlling temperature, pH, and light). Introduce a second male (or a model/photograph of a male stickleback, with or without a red throat) into the tank or in an adjacent transparent compartment (separated by glass to prevent injury). Record the number of attack responses (bites, charges, gill flares) per minute over a set observation period (e.g. 10 min). Compare attack rates in the presence vs absence of a competing male to determine the effect. Control variables: same individual fish, same tank conditions, same time of day. Use both a red-throated (breeding) and a non-red-throated model to determine whether the competing male's breeding status affects the response.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::4(a)": "Water molecules have a dipole nature because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, creating partial negative charge (δ−) on the oxygen and partial positive charge (δ+) on the hydrogen atoms. This polarity allows water molecules to form hydrogen bonds with each other. The high latent heat of vaporisation of water (resulting from these hydrogen bonds) means that large amounts of energy/heat must be absorbed to convert liquid water to vapour. When sweat evaporates from the skin surface, this heat energy is taken from the skin, causing cooling of the body surface (evaporative cooling), which helps lower core body temperature.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::4(b)": "The rate of sweating increases with increasing environmental temperature — a positive correlation is evident for all three activity levels. At any given temperature, exercising individuals produce more sweat than resting individuals, reflecting the greater metabolic heat generated by muscle contraction. There is little or no difference in sweating rate between exercising and exercising twice as vigorously at most temperatures — suggesting that beyond a moderate exercise level, sweating has reached near-maximum capacity. The onset of sweating (the temperature at which sweating begins to increase markedly) occurs at a lower environmental temperature during exercise than at rest, reflecting the additional internal heat load from muscle activity.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::4(c)": "Sweat production during exercise is controlled by negative feedback (homeostasis). When exercise raises core body temperature, thermoreceptors in the skin and hypothalamus detect the rise. The hypothalamus (thermoregulatory centre) sends nerve impulses along efferent (motor) nerves to the sweat glands throughout the body. The sweat glands increase the production and secretion of sweat onto the skin surface. As sweat evaporates, it removes heat from the skin (latent heat of vaporisation), reducing skin temperature and subsequently core body temperature back toward the set point. When core temperature falls, less hypothalamic stimulation reaches the sweat glands and sweat production decreases.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::5(a)(i)": "Schwann cell (also accept oligodendrocyte for the CNS).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::5(a)(ii)": "The myelin sheath provides electrical insulation around the axon, preventing ion movement across the axon membrane between nodes of Ranvier. As a result, depolarisation can only occur at the nodes of Ranvier (uninsulated gaps in the myelin sheath). The action potential 'jumps' from node to node — saltatory conduction — greatly increasing the speed of impulse transmission along the axon while reducing ATP expenditure (fewer Na⁺/K⁺ pump cycles needed).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::5(a)(iii)": "At rest, the axon membrane is polarised (resting potential ≈ −70 mV). When a stimulus reaches threshold, voltage-gated sodium (Na⁺) channels open; Na⁺ ions diffuse rapidly into the axon down their electrochemical gradient, causing depolarisation (membrane potential reverses to approximately +40 mV). The sodium channels then close. Voltage-gated potassium (K⁺) channels open; K⁺ ions diffuse out of the axon, causing repolarisation (membrane potential returns toward −70 mV) and briefly hyperpolarising the membrane (undershoot). K⁺ channels then close. The Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump (using ATP) restores the original ion concentrations (3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in per ATP), re-establishing the resting potential. This sequence of channel openings propagates as a wave of depolarisation along the axon — the action potential.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::5(b)(i)": "Individual A (smoking + high triglycerides): risk increase = 42% + 100% = 142%. Individual B (smoking + high LDL:HDL): risk increase = 42% + 67% = 109%. Ratio A : B = 142 : 109 ≈ 1.3 : 1.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::5(b)(ii)": "Risk factors such as smoking, high triglycerides, and a high LDL:HDL ratio all contribute to atherosclerosis — the build-up of fatty plaques in arterial walls. These plaques narrow the lumen of blood vessels (including arterioles supplying peripheral nerves), reducing blood flow. With reduced blood supply, the oxygen and glucose delivered to peripheral nerve cells for aerobic respiration decreases. Without sufficient ATP, nerve cells cannot maintain their ion gradients or essential metabolic processes. Over time, this leads to cell damage and death of peripheral nerve cells (peripheral neuropathy).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::6(a)": "Stem cells differentiate into specialised cells such as melanophores through differential gene expression. A chemical signal (e.g. a transcription factor or signalling molecule) enters or is produced within the stem cell, activating specific genes in the nucleus while switching off others. The activated genes are transcribed into mRNA, which is translated at ribosomes into proteins/enzymes (e.g. enzymes in the melanin synthesis pathway). These proteins determine the structure and function of the cell — in this case, producing melanin — giving the melanophore its specialised properties.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::6(b)(i)": "A gene mutation is a change in the base sequence of DNA (in a gene). It may involve a substitution (one base replaced by another), insertion, or deletion of one or more bases.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::6(b)(ii)": "Long fin is a dominant trait/allele — in cross 2, the F1 offspring all had long fins, showing long fin dominates over short fin. Spots is a recessive trait/allele — in cross 1, the F1 offspring all had stripes, showing stripes (wild type) is dominant over spots.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::6(c)(i)": "Offspring of dn (gamete) × DN → DdNn (stripes and long fins); dn × Dn → Ddnn (stripes and short fins); dn × dN → ddNn (spots and long fins); dn × dn → ddnn (spots and short fins).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::6(c)(ii)": "χ² = Σ(O − E)² ÷ E. (O − E) values: stripes+long fins = 270 − 288 = −18; stripes+short fins = 87 − 96 = −9; spots+long fins = 115 − 96 = +19; spots+short fins = 40 − 32 = +8. (O − E)²/E: (−18)²/288 = 324/288 = 1.125; (−9)²/96 = 81/96 = 0.844; (19)²/96 = 361/96 = 3.760; (8)²/32 = 64/32 = 2.000. χ² = 1.125 + 0.844 + 3.760 + 2.000 = 7.73.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::6(c)(iii)": "The calculated χ² value (7.73) is less than the critical value of 7.82 at 3 degrees of freedom at p = 0.05. Therefore the difference between observed and expected results is not statistically significant. We accept the null hypothesis: the two traits (melanin pattern and fin length) are inherited independently, consistent with Mendel's law of independent assortment.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::7(a)": "Mycobacterium tuberculosis evades the immune system by: (1) surviving inside macrophages — the bacterium's thick waxy (lipid-rich) cell wall prevents lysosomal fusion with the phagosome, so digestive enzymes cannot destroy it; (2) remaining dormant inside tubercles (granulomas) in the lung tissue, where it is not actively replicating and is hidden from immune surveillance; (3) inhibiting T helper cell activation and suppressing the acquired immune response, reducing the effectiveness of the adaptive immune system against it.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::7(b)(i)": "A transcription factor is a protein that controls the transcription of specific genes by binding to a promoter or operator region of DNA, enabling or blocking RNA polymerase from binding and initiating transcription of the gene.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::7(b)(ii)": "The STAT3 SNPs are located in introns (non-coding regions), so they do not directly change the amino acid sequence of the STAT3 protein. However, they may affect splicing signals or transcription factor binding, altering how much or when STAT3 is produced. From the table, individuals with AA or TT genotypes (at SNP1 and SNP2 respectively) appear more likely to develop TB and to develop severe TB. The severity graph shows that AA and TT genotypes are more common in severe TB cases, though some error bars overlap, reducing certainty. The bacteria culture graph shows white blood cells from AA individuals are less effective at killing mycobacteria than other genotypes, suggesting impaired macrophage or T-cell function. However, the culture graph lacks SNP2 data and error bars, limiting confidence in that conclusion. No data combines SNP1 and SNP2 together, preventing conclusions about synergistic effects. Overall, the data suggests that certain STAT3 alleles (particularly AA and TT) reduce STAT3-mediated immune regulation, making macrophages and T cells less effective, increasing susceptibility to and severity of TB infection — but the evidence has limitations that require further investigation.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::8(a)": "GPP = NPP + R (or equivalently: NPP = GPP − R). The carbon turned into sugars by photosynthesis (GPP) minus the carbon used by the plant for respiration (R) equals the net primary productivity (NPP) — the carbon available to primary consumers.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::8(b)": "Reduced NADP (NADPH / NADPH + H⁺). (Oxygen is also produced, but the question asks for a product other than ATP and oxygen.)",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::8(c)": "ATP is used as an immediate energy source in cells because it releases energy rapidly when hydrolysed (ATP → ADP + Pᵢ + energy). ATP contains high-energy phosphoanhydride bonds between adjacent phosphate groups — when these bonds are hydrolysed, free energy is released in a controlled, exothermic reaction. This energy can be coupled to endothermic reactions (e.g. biosynthesis, active transport, muscle contraction) to drive them forward. ATP acts as a universal energy currency — it can be rapidly regenerated from ADP + Pᵢ during respiration and photosynthesis, providing an efficient and flexible energy transfer mechanism.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::8(d)": "In the Calvin cycle (light-independent reactions) in the stroma: CO₂ from the air combines with the 5-carbon RuBP (ribulose bisphosphate) in a reaction catalysed by RUBISCO (ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase). This forms an unstable 6-carbon intermediate that immediately splits into two molecules of GP (glycerate-3-phosphate, a 3-carbon compound). GP is reduced using ATP and NADPH (from the light-dependent reactions) to form GALP (glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate / G3P, also 3-carbon). GALP molecules are used to synthesise glucose (hexose sugars) and other organic molecules, and to regenerate RuBP for the next cycle.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::8(e)": "To show that oxygen in photosynthesis comes from water (not CO₂): isolate chloroplasts using differential centrifugation. Incubate the chloroplasts in a solution of an electron acceptor that does not contain oxygen (e.g. iron ferricyanide / DCPIP) — this is the Hill reaction. No CO₂ is present in the reaction mixture (or use isotopically labelled ¹⁸O water: use H₂¹⁸O in the reaction mixture). Place the chloroplasts in light. Collect and test the gas produced — if it is O₂ (e.g. using a glowing splint), this shows oxygen is produced in the absence of CO₂, proving it comes from photolysis of water. Control: run the same experiment in the dark — no oxygen is produced, confirming light is required.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::8(f)": "Chloroplasts are adapted for photosynthesis in several ways: the thylakoid membranes (stacked in grana) provide a very large surface area, maximising the number of photosystems and electron carriers for the light-dependent reactions. The thylakoid membranes contain chlorophyll and other photosynthetic pigments to absorb a wide range of light wavelengths and transfer energy to electrons. The thylakoid membrane also contains the electron transport chain and ATP synthase, enabling chemiosmosis and photophosphorylation. The stroma surrounding the thylakoids contains all the enzymes needed for the Calvin cycle (including RUBISCO), as well as DNA and ribosomes (allowing chloroplasts to synthesise some of their own proteins, including RUBISCO subunits).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::8(g)": "Electrons passed along the electron transport chain in the thylakoid membrane lose energy at each step. This energy is used by electron carrier proteins to actively pump protons (H⁺) from the stroma into the thylakoid space (lumen), creating a proton gradient (high [H⁺] inside the lumen). Protons diffuse back across the thylakoid membrane down their concentration gradient through the enzyme ATP synthase (by chemiosmosis). As protons flow through ATP synthase, they drive rotation of its rotor, causing conformational changes in the catalytic subunit that synthesise ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate (Pᵢ).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::8(h)": "Chlorophyll is a molecule consisting of a protein component (the apoproteins) and a light-absorbing porphyrin ring with a magnesium ion at its centre. Tiny changes in the protein component — different amino acid sequences altering R-group interactions and the 3D (tertiary) structure — can alter the shape and electron environment of the light-absorbing porphyrin region. These structural changes alter the energy levels available to electrons, changing the quantum of energy (and therefore the wavelength of light) required to excite an electron and raise it to a higher energy level. This is why different chlorophyll types (e.g. chlorophyll a, b, P680, P700) absorb light at slightly different wavelengths.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::8(i)": "Proteomics: compare the amino acid sequences (primary structure) of catalase and the oxygen-evolving complex (OEC) from organisms at different stages of evolution. If the OEC evolved from catalase, organisms further back in evolutionary history should show greater sequence similarity between the two proteins. Genomics: compare the DNA/gene sequences encoding catalase and the OEC across species. A conserved core gene sequence in both proteins, especially in more ancient organisms, would support common evolutionary ancestry. If the OEC gene shows partial sequence homology to the catalase gene, and this homology is greatest in primitive photosynthetic organisms, this supports the hypothesis that the OEC evolved from catalase. Analysing the phylogenetic tree of these genes would also help establish the proposed evolutionary relationship.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2023-p3::8(j)": "The graph comparing catalase and peroxidase should show: x-axis — substrate (H₂O₂) concentration (mol dm⁻³); y-axis — rate of reaction (e.g. mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ or cm³ O₂ min⁻¹). Catalase line should be steeper (higher Vmax) than peroxidase — catalase operates at a higher rate per unit concentration than peroxidase. Both lines curve and plateau at high substrate concentration (reaching Vmax when all active sites are occupied). The catalase line plateaus at a higher rate than peroxidase. Both lines start at the origin (zero rate at zero substrate).",

// ─── Edexcel A 2022 P1 — comprehensive answers ───────────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::1(a)(i)": "A — amino acids. Nitrate ions (NO₃⁻) provide nitrogen, which is essential for synthesising amino acids (the building blocks of proteins). Nitrogen is a component of the amino group (–NH₂) in every amino acid.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::1(a)(ii)": "C — nucleic acids. Phosphate ions (PO₄³⁻) are required for the synthesis of nucleotides, which form the backbone of DNA and RNA (as phosphodiester bonds link adjacent nucleotides). Phosphate is also required for ATP and phospholipids.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::1(a)(iii)": "C — chlorophyll. Magnesium ions (Mg²⁺) are incorporated into the porphyrin ring at the centre of the chlorophyll molecule (each chlorophyll contains one Mg²⁺ ion). Without magnesium, chlorophyll cannot be synthesised and leaves turn yellow (chlorosis).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::1(b)": "Mineral ions are present at lower concentrations in the soil than inside root hair cells. They therefore cannot enter by diffusion (which would move them from high to low concentration — out of the cell, not in). Instead, mineral ions are taken up by active transport: carrier proteins (ion pumps) in the plasma membrane of root hair cells use energy (ATP) from mitochondria to transport mineral ions against their concentration gradient from the soil into the cell. The cell surface area of root hair cells is greatly increased by the elongated hair-like projections, maximising the surface for ion uptake.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::2(a)": "B — 3 correct statements. The three correct statements are: deforestation contributes to global warming (trees absorb CO₂; removing them reduces CO₂ absorption and releases stored carbon); global warming leads to melting of sea ice (confirmed by satellite data); and global warming may lead to changes in rainfall patterns. Global warming is caused by an INCREASE in greenhouse gases (not a reduction). Global warming is affecting all regions, not just the ice caps.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::2(b)(i)": "Plant protein sources require fewer trophic levels in the food chain than animal protein sources — there is no intermediate animal trophic level. Energy and biomass are lost at every trophic level (through respiration, excretion, movement), so using plants directly as food avoids this energy loss. Livestock also produce greenhouse gases directly through methane from enteric fermentation (in ruminants like cattle) and nitrous oxide from manure. Growing feed crops for animals requires large areas of agricultural land and energy inputs. Plant protein production, with no animal intermediary, therefore has a lower greenhouse gas footprint.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::2(b)(ii)": "Rainforest trees are large carbon stores — they absorb CO₂ through photosynthesis and lock it in their biomass. When the forest is cleared (by burning or felling), this stored carbon is released as CO₂ into the atmosphere (burning produces CO₂ directly; decomposition of remaining material also releases CO₂). Natural pasture also stores carbon in the soil and plant roots; deforested land has far less vegetation and soil carbon. Additionally, cleared land may dry out and its organic matter decompose faster, releasing CO₂ through decomposer respiration. Cattle on natural pasture live within an established, carbon-stable ecosystem; cattle on deforested land represent a net increase in atmospheric CO₂.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::3(a)(i)": "Radiocarbon dating (carbon-14 dating) can determine the age of organic material in peat layers by measuring the proportion of ¹⁴C remaining. Pollen analysis can also date peat layers by identifying characteristic pollen from known plant species at each depth.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::3(a)(ii)": "Peat bog conditions prevent decomposition in three main ways: (1) Anaerobic (waterlogged) conditions — the bog is saturated with water, which excludes oxygen. Decomposers (bacteria, fungi) that break down organic matter aerobically cannot survive without oxygen, so decomposition is severely limited. (2) Acidic conditions (low pH) — sphagnum moss produces organic acids, lowering the pH to around 3–4. Most decomposer enzymes have an optimum near neutral pH; the extreme acidity denatures them or inhibits their activity, preventing decomposition. (3) Low temperature — cold bog conditions slow metabolic reactions in any surviving decomposers.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::3(b)": "To measure the rate of recovery of the peat bog: (1) Establish permanent quadrat plots at regular intervals along transects across the bog. (2) Record baseline measurements at the start: percentage cover of sphagnum moss, water table depth, soil pH, and species present. (3) Repeat measurements at regular time intervals (e.g. monthly or seasonally). (4) Record the increase in percentage cover of sphagnum moss and the depth of peat (using a peat probe) over time as indicators of recovery. (5) Compare species composition to an undisturbed reference peat bog. (6) Use standardised methodology (same quadrat size, same time of year for each survey) to ensure results are comparable. Rate of recovery = change in sphagnum cover ÷ time.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::4(a)": "Two structures indicating Plasmodium is eukaryotic: (1) Nucleus — a membrane-bound nucleus containing linear chromosomes is a defining feature of eukaryotes; prokaryotes have no nuclear membrane. (2) Mitochondria — Plasmodium has mitochondria for aerobic respiration; prokaryotes lack membrane-bound organelles. (Accept also: endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi apparatus, 80S ribosomes.)",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::4(b)(i)": "The primary structure of a protein is the specific sequence of amino acids joined by peptide bonds in the polypeptide chain. The order of amino acids is determined by the sequence of codons in the mRNA (and ultimately the DNA base sequence of the gene). The primary structure dictates how the polypeptide folds into its secondary, tertiary, and quaternary structures.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::4(b)(ii)": "After translation at ribosomes (on the rough ER), the polypeptide enters the lumen of the rough endoplasmic reticulum (rER) where it is folded into its tertiary structure (with assistance from molecular chaperones) and may undergo initial modifications. Vesicles bud off from the rER and transport the protein to the Golgi apparatus. The Golgi apparatus processes and modifies the protein (e.g. glycosylation — adding carbohydrate groups to form a glycoprotein; or phosphorylation; or cleavage of signal sequences). The protein is then sorted and packaged into vesicles for secretion (exocytosis) or for delivery to specific cellular compartments (e.g. lysosomes).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::5(a)": "A mutation in the gene coding for the haem-synthesis enzyme changes the DNA base sequence. During transcription, this altered sequence produces a different mRNA codon sequence. At translation, the different codons cause a different amino acid to be incorporated into the polypeptide (altered primary structure). This changes the R-group interactions (hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bridges) within the protein, altering its tertiary structure and therefore the shape of the active site. If the active site is the wrong shape, it can no longer form a complementary enzyme–substrate complex with its substrate (the molecule in the haem synthesis pathway). The enzyme cannot catalyse the reaction, so haem production is prevented.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::5(b)(i)": "Person C is unaffected. Since porphyria is caused by a dominant allele and person C is unaffected, they must be homozygous recessive (genotype: pp). Phenotype: unaffected / does not have porphyria.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::5(b)(ii)": "B — 0.25. For porphyria (dominant allele P): if person A has porphyria (genotype Pp) and person B is unaffected (pp), their offspring have a 50% chance of having porphyria (Pp). Probability of being male = 50%. So probability of male WITH porphyria = 0.5 × 0.5 = 0.25.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::5(c)": "siRNA could be used to prevent porphyria by targeting the mRNA transcribed from the dominant mutant allele. siRNA molecules complementary to the mutant mRNA are introduced into cells (e.g. via a viral vector or nanoparticle). The siRNA binds to the mutant mRNA by complementary base pairing, forming a double-stranded complex. This triggers the RISC (RNA-induced silencing complex) to degrade the mutant mRNA. With the mutant mRNA destroyed, translation of the faulty enzyme cannot occur, preventing accumulation of the toxic intermediate in the haem synthesis pathway, and thus preventing development of porphyria.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::6(a)": "A mitochondrion (labelled diagram) should show: outer membrane (smooth, continuous); inner membrane (folded into cristae, increasing surface area); matrix (fluid-filled interior, site of Krebs cycle); intermembrane space (narrow space between inner and outer membranes); ribosomes (70S, present in matrix); and circular DNA (present in matrix).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::6(b)": "Mitochondria are located in the cytoplasm of cells. At fertilisation, the sperm contributes only its haploid nucleus (and centrosome) to the egg — the sperm's cytoplasm (including its mitochondria) is shed and does not enter the egg. The egg (oocyte) contributes its haploid nucleus plus all of its cytoplasm, including all maternal mitochondria. Therefore, the fertilised egg (zygote) inherits mitochondria exclusively from the mother.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::6(c)(i)": "In the mitochondrial replacement therapy (MRT) described: the nucleus removed from the donor egg contains the donor's nuclear DNA but no mutant mitochondrial DNA. The nucleus from the mother's fertilised egg (which contains the mother's nuclear DNA but also mutant mitochondrial DNA in the cytoplasm) is transferred into the enucleated donor egg. The resulting embryo now has the mother's and father's nuclear DNA (in the transferred nucleus) but the donor's healthy mitochondria (in the donor egg's cytoplasm). Because the mitochondria come from the donor (who has normal mitochondrial DNA), the embryo does not develop mitochondrial disease.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::6(c)(ii)": "The offspring produced by this technique inherits most of its characteristics from its two biological parents: the vast majority of DNA — all 23 pairs of nuclear chromosomes encoding approximately 20 000 genes — comes from the mother and father. However, the offspring also inherits a small amount of genetic material from the donor: mitochondrial DNA (~37 genes encoding components of the mitochondrial electron transport chain). The offspring therefore has genetic contributions from three people ('three-parent baby'), but the vast majority of heritable characteristics (eye colour, facial features, disease susceptibilities determined by nuclear genes) come from the mother and father, not the donor.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::7(a)(i)": "Percentage change = [(135 − 1855) ÷ 1855] × 100 = (−1720 ÷ 1855) × 100 = −92.7%. (A decrease of 92.7% to 3 significant figures.)",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::7(a)(ii)": "When vaccination rates increase, not all children are vaccinated immediately — some were already past the vaccination window; others may not have received all doses. Newly vaccinated children take time to develop full immunity — the B cells and T cells must proliferate and differentiate into memory cells and plasma cells (a primary response takes days to weeks). Additionally, children vaccinated shortly before a measles outbreak may not yet have developed full immunity. Herd immunity requires a high enough percentage of the population to be immune before disease transmission is effectively broken. It therefore takes time after an increase in vaccination rate for sufficient individuals to be fully immunised and for herd immunity to reduce transmission to the point where case numbers fall significantly.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::7(b)(i)": "B — B cells are activated to become plasma cells. Plasma cells are the effector B cells that secrete large quantities of specific antibodies. Memory B cells remain dormant until re-exposure to the antigen.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::7(b)(ii)": "A — active artificial. The vaccine stimulates the immune system to mount its own immune response (active immunity), producing memory cells and antibodies. It is artificial because it uses a vaccine (an antigen introduced deliberately by a healthcare professional) rather than resulting from natural infection.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::7(b)(iii)": "The measles virus destroys some B memory cells and existing antibodies. If the measles infection destroys memory B cells from previous vaccinations (e.g. for polio, mumps, rubella), the child may lose their immunological memory for those diseases. Without memory cells, the child would no longer have rapid (secondary) immune response protection against those pathogens — their immunity could revert toward the weaker, slower primary response. Repeating the other vaccinations restimulates the immune system to produce new memory B cells and antibodies for those diseases, restoring their protection.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::8(a)": "B — non-specific response and bacteria are destroyed by phagocytes. The initial (innate) immune response to pathogens is non-specific: phagocytes (neutrophils and macrophages) rapidly engulf any foreign material by phagocytosis, regardless of the specific antigen present. Antibodies are part of the specific (adaptive) immune response, which develops later.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::8(b)": "Dormant M. tuberculosis bacteria reside inside tubercles (granulomas) — isolated lesions in the lung surrounded by a wall of macrophages and other immune cells. Inside these tubercles, the bacteria are not actively replicating and express fewer surface antigens. Importantly, the bacteria survive inside macrophages by preventing lysosomal fusion (their thick, waxy cell wall resists digestion). Without active antigen presentation to T helper cells, the specific immune response (antibody production, T cytotoxic cell activation) is not stimulated against the dormant bacteria. They effectively hide from immune surveillance and are not recognised or destroyed.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::8(c)(i)": "HIV-positive TB patients = 15% × 9.4 million = 1.41 million. Percentage of HIV-positive TB patients who died = (521 700 ÷ 1 410 000) × 100 ≈ 37%.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::8(c)(ii)": "HIV co-infection dramatically increases the number of deaths from TB. HIV-positive TB patients have a much higher death rate (~37%) than HIV-negative TB patients (fewer proportional deaths from the ~8 million HIV-negative TB cases). The table/graph data show that HIV status is a major risk factor for TB mortality — HIV-infected individuals are far more likely to die from TB than those without HIV.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p1::8(c)(iii)": "HIV infects and destroys T helper cells (CD4+ T cells). T helper cells are essential for coordinating both the humoral (B cell/antibody) and cell-mediated (T cytotoxic cell) immune responses to M. tuberculosis. With HIV, T helper cell counts decline, progressively impairing the ability of the immune system to control TB bacteria, increasing TB incidence. Anti-viral drugs (antiretrovirals) reduce HIV replication, slowing the rate of T helper cell destruction. As T helper cell counts recover or are maintained, the immune system regains its ability to control M. tuberculosis. More effective immune responses mean fewer new TB infections and better control of latent TB, reducing the proportion of HIV-positive individuals who progress to active TB. Fewer active TB cases means fewer TB deaths — particularly in HIV-positive individuals, who suffer the highest TB mortality. The evidence (graph of TB incidence vs T helper cell count) shows that higher T helper cell counts correlate with lower TB incidence, confirming that maintaining T helper cell numbers reduces TB disease.",

// ─── Edexcel A 2022 P2 — comprehensive answers ───────────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p2::1(b)": "During intense exercise, insufficient oxygen reaches muscle cells to maintain aerobic respiration at the rate needed. Under these anaerobic conditions, pyruvate (from glycolysis) is converted to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase. This reaction re-oxidises reduced NAD (NADH + H⁺ → NAD), which is essential for glycolysis to continue accepting hydrogen. Glycolysis can therefore continue producing ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation even without oxygen. The lactate is transported to the liver, where it is later converted back to glucose (Cori cycle) when oxygen is available.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p2::2(a)": "The cardiac cycle involves electrical and mechanical events coordinated by the conducting system. The SAN (pacemaker) in the right atrium generates a spontaneous action potential that spreads across both atria, causing atrial systole (atrial contraction). The impulse reaches the AVN, which delays transmission by ~0.1 s (allowing ventricles to fill). The impulse passes via the bundle of His and Purkyne fibres to the ventricles, triggering ventricular systole from the apex upward, pumping blood into the aorta and pulmonary artery. Ventricular diastole follows — ventricular muscle relaxes, semilunar valves close (preventing backflow), and the atrioventricular valves open as blood refills the ventricles. The P wave on the ECG = atrial depolarisation; QRS complex = ventricular depolarisation (systole); T wave = ventricular repolarisation (diastole).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p2::3(a)": "Muscle contraction requires calcium ions (Ca²⁺), ATP, myosin, and actin. Nervous impulse reaches the neuromuscular junction → acetylcholine (ACh) is released and binds to nicotinic receptors on the motor end plate → action potential spreads across the sarcolemma and T-tubules → sarcoplasmic reticulum releases Ca²⁺ → Ca²⁺ binds to troponin on the thin (actin) filament → troponin changes shape, moving tropomyosin off the myosin-binding sites on actin → myosin heads (energised by ATP hydrolysis) bind to actin forming cross-bridges → power stroke (myosin head pivots, pulling actin toward the M line) → ADP + Pᵢ released → new ATP binds myosin, detaching the cross-bridge → cycle repeats → sarcomere shortens → muscle contracts.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p2::4(a)": "The resting potential (approximately −70 mV) is maintained by the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump, which actively transports 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in per ATP hydrolysed, maintaining high intracellular [K⁺] and high extracellular [Na⁺]. The membrane is more permeable to K⁺ than Na⁺ at rest (leak channels), so K⁺ diffuses out, making the inside more negative. When a stimulus reaches threshold, voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open; Na⁺ rushes in, depolarising the membrane to ~+40 mV (action potential). Na⁺ channels inactivate; voltage-gated K⁺ channels open; K⁺ rushes out, repolarising the membrane. During the refractory period (hyperpolarisation), no new action potential can be generated — this ensures unidirectional propagation and limits the frequency of impulses.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p2::5(a)": "Systolic blood pressure (mmHg) during exercise: during moderate exercise, stroke volume and heart rate both increase, raising cardiac output and systolic blood pressure. As exercise intensity increases, sympathetic nervous stimulation increases heart rate (positive chronotropy) and stroke volume (positive inotropy) — CO = HR × SV rises, so systolic pressure increases. Diastolic pressure may stay the same or decrease slightly due to vasodilation of skeletal muscle arterioles (reducing peripheral resistance). Pulse pressure (systolic − diastolic) increases.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p2::6(a)": "In the light, the phytochrome system controls flowering. In long-day (short-night) conditions, red light converts Pr to Pfr (active form) during the long photoperiod. During the short night, insufficient time elapses for Pfr to revert to Pr (dark reversion). High Pfr levels accumulate, which stimulates flowering genes (florigen production) in long-day plants. In short-day (long-night) plants, the converse applies: a long enough night allows Pfr to revert to Pr; low Pfr (or high Pr) triggers flowering. Interrupting the dark period with a brief flash of red light converts Pr to Pfr, preventing short-day plant flowering.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p2::7(a)": "Plant hormones IAA (auxin) and ABA (abscisic acid) interact to control dormancy. IAA promotes cell elongation and growth; ABA promotes dormancy and inhibits germination. In drought conditions, ABA accumulates — it causes stomatal closure (by triggering K⁺ efflux from guard cells, reducing turgor) and promotes seed dormancy. IAA produced in apical buds inhibits lateral bud growth (apical dominance). Cytokinins counteract IAA, promoting lateral growth. The balance between these hormones determines the growth pattern of the plant.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p2::8(a)": "Insulin is released by β cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas in response to rising blood glucose. Insulin is a peptide hormone (protein-based) that binds to receptor proteins on the surface of target cells (e.g. liver hepatocytes, muscle cells, adipocytes). Binding activates intracellular signalling cascades that: (1) increase the number of glucose transporter proteins (GLUT4) in the cell membrane, increasing glucose uptake; (2) activate glycogen synthase — glucose is converted to glycogen (glycogenesis) and stored; (3) promote fat synthesis (lipogenesis) in adipose tissue. Blood glucose falls back to the set point — negative feedback inhibits further insulin secretion.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p2::9(a)": "In an investigation comparing effects of two drugs on heart rate: control variables — age, sex, baseline heart rate, time of day, caffeine intake. Independent variable — drug type (A, B, or placebo). Dependent variable — change in heart rate (beats per minute). Statistical analysis — calculate mean and standard deviation; use a t-test or ANOVA to compare groups; results are significant if p < 0.05. A placebo group controls for the placebo effect; double blinding prevents bias from patient and researcher expectation.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p2::10(a)": "Evaluating the evidence for lifestyle factors and cardiovascular disease: cohort studies follow large populations over time, measuring exposures and outcomes, but cannot prove causation (confounding variables). Randomised controlled trials (RCTs) provide stronger evidence by randomly assigning participants to interventions and controls. Relative risk is used to quantify the association: a relative risk of 2.0 means the exposed group has twice the risk. Meta-analyses combine data from multiple studies to increase statistical power. Lifestyle factors with robust evidence include: smoking (relative risk of CHD ~2–4), physical inactivity, diet high in saturated fats/trans fats (raise LDL cholesterol → atherosclerosis), obesity (BMI > 30), and hypertension. These risk factors promote endothelial damage, inflammation, and plaque formation — the pathological basis of CHD.",

// ─── Edexcel A 2022 P3 — comprehensive answers ───────────────────────────────
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p3::1(b)": "Mineral ions are absorbed by root hair cells via active transport. Because mineral ion concentrations in soil water are lower than inside vacuoles of root hair cells, diffusion would move ions in the wrong direction. Instead, carrier proteins (ion pumps) in the plasma membrane use ATP energy to transport specific ions (e.g. NO₃⁻, Mg²⁺, PO₄³⁻) against their concentration gradient from soil solution into the root hair cell cytoplasm. The large surface area of root hairs maximises the membrane available for ion uptake.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p3::2(a)": "Colonisation of new habitats follows a predictable succession: pioneer species (e.g. lichens, mosses) colonise bare substrate, modifying the environment (adding organic matter to soil, increasing water retention). Over time, the environment becomes more favourable for subsequent species — grasses, then shrubs, then trees. Each community alters the abiotic environment, enabling the next community (seral stage) to establish. The final stable community is the climax community. Species diversity increases through succession as the environment becomes more complex and varied, supporting more niches.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p3::3(a)": "Sampling biodiversity: use random quadrats to avoid bias — generate random coordinates using a random number table. Within each quadrat, record the species present (species richness) and the number of individuals of each species (abundance). Use the Simpson's diversity index D = 1 − [Σn(n−1) ÷ N(N−1)] to quantify biodiversity. To compare two areas, test whether the difference in D values is statistically significant using an appropriate statistical test. Transects can be used if studying a gradient (e.g. from urban to rural).",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p3::4(a)": "Ecological efficiency at each trophic level: typically only 10% of energy at one trophic level is transferred to the next. Energy is lost through: (1) respiration — the majority of energy intake is used for metabolic processes and heat; (2) excretion — energy in urine and faeces; (3) egestion — undigested material. Efficiency = (production at level n+1 ÷ production at level n) × 100. Low trophic efficiency means fewer organisms can be supported at higher trophic levels, which is why food chains are rarely longer than 4–5 links.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p3::5(a)": "The Hill reaction demonstrates that isolated chloroplasts can reduce an artificial electron acceptor (DCPIP — which turns from blue to colourless when reduced) in the light. This proves that the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis produce reducing power (reduced NADP/electron donors) and can transfer electrons to an acceptor. In the absence of CO₂ (no Calvin cycle), DCPIP substitutes as the electron acceptor, and oxygen is released. This supports the chemiosmotic theory of photophosphorylation.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p3::6(a)": "Enzyme immobilisation: enzymes are attached to inert support materials (e.g. sodium alginate beads, ion exchange resins, cellulose). Advantages: (1) the enzyme can be recovered and reused, reducing cost; (2) the product is not contaminated with the enzyme (important for food/pharmaceutical applications); (3) immobilised enzymes can be more thermostable. Applications include glucose isomerase (converting glucose to fructose in high-fructose corn syrup) and penicillinase (penicillin manufacturing). The enzyme is immobilised by physical adsorption, covalent bonding, entrapment within a gel matrix (e.g. alginate), or cross-linking.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p3::7": "Evaluating the use of gene therapy for genetic diseases: gene therapy can potentially cure genetic diseases by correcting the faulty allele in somatic cells (somatic gene therapy) or germline cells (germline gene therapy). Somatic therapy is temporary (repeated treatment may be needed) and does not affect offspring. Germline therapy is permanent and heritable but raises ethical concerns about designer babies and unintended consequences. Vectors (viral or non-viral) deliver the therapeutic gene. Risks include immune response to the viral vector, insertional mutagenesis (the gene inserts near an oncogene → cancer), and off-target effects. Benefits: potential cure for currently incurable conditions (e.g. SCID, cystic fibrosis). Ethical issues: consent of future generations; defining 'disease' vs 'enhancement'. Current successes: approved gene therapies for SCID, haemophilia B, and spinal muscular atrophy.",
"bio-edxa-9bn0-2022-p3::8(a)": "Analysis of the scientific article: the article's key biological claims should be evaluated using the data provided. Strong claims are those supported by peer-reviewed data from large sample sizes, replicated across multiple studies. Weaker claims use limited sample sizes, no controls, or correlational rather than causal evidence. Consider: is the research published in a peer-reviewed journal? Is the sample size adequate? Are controls included? Are potential confounding variables addressed? Is the effect size biologically meaningful, not just statistically significant?",

// ─── Edexcel B (Advancing Biology) 9BI0 — 2024, 2023, 2022 ─────────────────
// 2024 Paper 1 — Advanced Biochemistry, Microbiology and Genetics
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p1::1(a)": "DNA is a double-stranded polynucleotide. Each nucleotide consists of a deoxyribose sugar, a phosphate group, and one of four nitrogenous bases (adenine, thymine, cytosine, or guanine). Adjacent nucleotides within a strand are joined by phosphodiester bonds (between the 3' carbon of one deoxyribose and the 5' carbon of the next via the phosphate group). The two antiparallel strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs: adenine–thymine (two hydrogen bonds) and cytosine–guanine (three hydrogen bonds). The double helix is stabilised by base stacking interactions between adjacent base pairs.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p1::2(a)": "Gene expression involves transcription and translation. In transcription, RNA polymerase binds to the promoter region of a gene on the template strand and unwinds the DNA. Free RNA nucleotides base-pair with the template strand (A–U, T–A, G–C), and RNA polymerase joins them by phosphodiester bonds, forming a pre-mRNA strand. In eukaryotes, introns are removed by splicing (by spliceosomes) and exons are joined to form mature mRNA. The mRNA leaves the nucleus through nuclear pores. In translation, mRNA binds to ribosomes; tRNA molecules with complementary anticodons carry specific amino acids and bind to codons on the mRNA; peptide bonds form between adjacent amino acids as the ribosome moves along the mRNA, elongating the polypeptide chain.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p1::3(a)": "Enzymes are biological catalysts that lower the activation energy of reactions by forming enzyme–substrate complexes. The active site has a specific 3D shape complementary to the substrate. In the induced fit model, the active site changes shape slightly as the substrate binds, improving complementarity and straining bonds in the substrate, facilitating the reaction. Enzyme activity is affected by temperature (increases rate up to the optimum, then denatures the enzyme above it) and pH (alters ionisation of R groups in the active site; extreme pH denatures enzymes).",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p1::2(a)": "Enzyme active sites have a specific 3D shape complementary to their substrate. In the induced fit model, the active site changes shape slightly when the substrate binds, improving complementarity and straining bonds in the substrate. Enzymes lower activation energy by stabilising the transition state. Inhibitors reduce enzyme activity: competitive inhibitors bind reversibly at the active site (overcome by excess substrate); non-competitive inhibitors bind allosteric sites, altering the active site shape permanently; irreversible inhibitors form covalent bonds with the active site, permanently inactivating the enzyme.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p1::3(a)": "Microbial growth phases: lag phase — bacteria adapting to the new medium, synthesising enzymes; log/exponential phase — rapid binary fission, population doubles each generation time; stationary phase — growth rate equals death rate as nutrients deplete and waste accumulates; death/decline phase — death rate exceeds growth rate as toxic wastes accumulate and nutrients are exhausted. Generation time = time ÷ number of doublings. Growth curves are produced by serial dilution and colony counting (viable count) or by turbidimetry (optical density — measures total cell number, living and dead).",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p1::4(a)": "Genetic engineering (recombinant DNA technology) to produce insulin: identify the human insulin gene. Isolate using restriction endonucleases that cut at specific palindromic sequences, producing complementary sticky ends. Cut bacterial plasmid with same enzyme. Join gene into plasmid using DNA ligase (complementary sticky ends anneal; ligase seals phosphodiester bonds). Transform bacteria (e.g. E. coli) with the recombinant plasmid by heat shock or electroporation. Screen for successful transformants using selectable markers (antibiotic resistance or reporter genes such as GFP). Grow selected bacteria in fermenters — they express the human insulin gene, producing insulin. Purify insulin from the culture medium.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p1::5(a)": "Gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments by size. DNA (negatively charged due to phosphate groups) is loaded into wells in an agarose gel submerged in buffer. A voltage is applied — DNA migrates toward the positive electrode (anode). Smaller fragments experience less resistance from the gel matrix and travel further. After running, the gel is stained (e.g. ethidium bromide or SYBR Green) and visualised under UV light. Fragment sizes are determined by comparing band positions with a DNA ladder (size standard). Applications: DNA profiling, restriction mapping, checking PCR products.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p2::1(a)": "Osmoregulation in mammals involves the kidneys and ADH. Blood water potential is monitored by osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus. When blood osmolarity rises (water potential falls), the posterior pituitary releases more ADH. ADH binds to V2 receptors on collecting duct cells, triggering insertion of aquaporins into the luminal membrane. Water moves by osmosis from the collecting duct filtrate into the hypertonic medullary interstitium and then the blood, producing a small volume of concentrated urine. When blood osmolarity falls, less ADH is released, fewer aquaporins are inserted, less water is reabsorbed, and more dilute urine is produced. This negative feedback maintains blood water potential near the set point.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p2::2(a)": "The kidney regulates blood water potential by varying the permeability of the collecting duct to water. When blood osmolarity rises, osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus trigger secretion of ADH from the posterior pituitary. ADH binds to V2 receptors on collecting duct cells, activating a cAMP cascade that causes aquaporin-2 channels to insert into the apical membrane. Water reabsorption increases, producing concentrated urine. When blood osmolarity falls (e.g. after drinking), less ADH is secreted, fewer aquaporins are inserted, less water is reabsorbed, and more dilute urine is produced — negative feedback restores the set point.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p2::3(a)": "Thermoregulation maintains core body temperature at ~37 °C. Thermoreceptors in the hypothalamus (central) and skin (peripheral) detect temperature deviations. When too hot: vasodilation of skin arterioles increases blood flow and heat loss by radiation; sweat glands increase sweat production — evaporation removes latent heat. When too cold: vasoconstriction reduces skin blood flow; shivering (rapid muscle contractions) generates metabolic heat; piloerection traps air for insulation. All responses are coordinated by the hypothalamic thermoregulatory centre via the autonomic nervous system. Negative feedback brings temperature back to the set point.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p2::4(a)": "Synaptic transmission: action potential reaches the presynaptic terminal → voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open → Ca²⁺ enters → synaptic vesicles fuse with the presynaptic membrane (exocytosis) → neurotransmitter (e.g. acetylcholine, noradrenaline, serotonin, dopamine) diffuses across the synaptic cleft → binds to specific receptors on the postsynaptic membrane → opens ion channels (e.g. Na⁺ channels for excitatory; Cl⁻ channels for inhibitory) → generates excitatory or inhibitory postsynaptic potential. Neurotransmitter is then degraded by enzymes (e.g. acetylcholinesterase) or taken back up by reuptake transporters. Drugs can act at synapses: e.g. SSRIs block serotonin reuptake; curare blocks ACh receptors; organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p3::1(a)": "Planning an investigation: identify the independent variable (e.g. enzyme concentration), dependent variable (rate of product formation), and controlled variables (temperature, pH, substrate concentration, buffer). Use replicates (≥3) to assess variability. Include a negative control (no enzyme or boiled enzyme). Measure at regular intervals; use a colorimeter for objective readings. Plot results with error bars and use an appropriate statistical test (e.g. t-test or ANOVA) to determine significance.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p3::2(a)": "ELISA (enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay) detects specific proteins (antigens or antibodies) in a sample. Direct ELISA: coat a plate with the antibody; add sample; any antigen binds; add an enzyme-linked secondary antibody; add substrate — enzyme converts substrate to a coloured product (visible or measured spectrophotometrically). Indirect ELISA: coat the plate with antigen; add sample (test for antibodies); add enzyme-linked anti-antibody; add substrate — colour change indicates antibody presence. Used in HIV testing, pregnancy tests, food safety, and clinical diagnostics.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2024-p3::3(a)": "Bioinformatics tools allow comparison of large DNA or protein sequence datasets. BLAST (Basic Local Alignment Search Tool) aligns a query sequence against a database to find similar sequences. Sequence alignment identifies conserved regions (functionally important) and divergent regions. Phylogenetic trees are constructed from sequence data by calculating the number of base or amino acid differences between species — more differences indicate a longer time since divergence. This allows molecular clocks to estimate when species last shared a common ancestor.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p1::1(a)": "The cell cycle consists of interphase (G1 — cell growth and protein synthesis; S — DNA replication; G2 — organelle replication and further growth) followed by mitosis (prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase) and cytokinesis. During S phase, each chromosome is replicated semiconservatively: the double helix unwinds (helicase), each strand acts as a template, and DNA polymerase adds complementary nucleotides in the 5'→3' direction, producing two identical sister chromatids joined at the centromere.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p1::2(a)": "Restriction enzymes (restriction endonucleases) are bacterial enzymes that cut double-stranded DNA at specific palindromic recognition sequences (4–8 bp). Some produce blunt ends; others produce staggered cuts giving single-stranded 'sticky ends' that can base-pair with complementary sequences from another DNA molecule cut with the same enzyme. Different restriction enzymes recognise different sequences (e.g. EcoRI recognises GAATTC). Restriction mapping uses multiple enzymes to determine the positions of recognition sites along a DNA molecule.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p1::3(a)": "Cell signalling: a hormone/neurotransmitter (first messenger) binds to a receptor on the target cell surface. G-protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) activate adenylate cyclase → converts ATP to cAMP (second messenger) → activates protein kinase A → phosphorylates target proteins → cellular response (e.g. glycogen breakdown in liver). Receptor tyrosine kinases (RTKs) are activated by insulin-like growth factors → autophosphorylation → MAP kinase cascade → gene expression changes. Signal transduction amplifies the signal: one hormone molecule can activate thousands of effector molecules.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p1::4(a)": "Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium (HWE) predicts allele and genotype frequencies in a non-evolving population: p² + 2pq + q² = 1 (genotype frequencies) and p + q = 1 (allele frequencies). HWE requires: no mutation; no natural selection; large population size (no genetic drift); no gene flow (no migration); random mating. Deviations from HWE indicate evolution is occurring. To apply: if the frequency of homozygous recessive (aa) is q², then q = √q², p = 1 − q, and the frequency of heterozygotes = 2pq.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p1::5(a)": "Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplifies specific DNA sequences in vitro using heat cycling: (1) Denaturation (94–98 °C) — double-stranded DNA is separated into single strands by breaking hydrogen bonds. (2) Annealing (50–65 °C) — short single-stranded DNA primers complementary to the flanking sequences bind to each template strand. (3) Extension (72 °C) — heat-stable DNA polymerase (Taq polymerase) extends from each primer in the 5'→3' direction, synthesising the new strand. Each cycle doubles the amount of target DNA; 30–40 cycles produce millions of copies. PCR is used in forensics, medical diagnosis, genetic engineering, and the study of ancient DNA.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p2::1(a)": "The nervous system transmits information as electrical impulses. A resting neurone has a resting potential of approximately −70 mV (inside negative), maintained by the sodium–potassium pump (3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in per ATP). When stimulated above threshold, voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open — Na⁺ rushes in (depolarisation) — reversing the membrane potential to approximately +40 mV. Voltage-gated K⁺ channels then open — K⁺ leaves — repolarising and briefly hyperpolarising the membrane (refractory period). The action potential propagates along the axon (in myelinated neurones, by saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier).",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p2::2(a)": "Haemoglobin's oxygen dissociation curve is sigmoidal because of cooperative binding. Each haemoglobin molecule has four subunits; binding of the first O₂ causes conformational changes that increase affinity for subsequent O₂ molecules (cooperative binding). At high pO₂ (lungs) haemoglobin is fully saturated; at low pO₂ (respiring tissues) O₂ is released. The Bohr effect: increased CO₂ (and lower pH) shifts the curve right (reduced affinity at any pO₂), enhancing O₂ unloading in active tissues. Fetal haemoglobin (HbF) has higher O₂ affinity than adult Hb — its curve is shifted left — allowing fetal blood to extract O₂ from maternal blood across the placenta.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p2::3(a)": "The mammalian kidney has three main regions: cortex, medulla, and pelvis. Each nephron consists of: Bowman's capsule (glomerular filtration — high pressure forces fluid from glomerular capillaries into the capsule, filtering small molecules: water, glucose, amino acids, urea, ions); proximal convoluted tubule (PCT — selective reabsorption of all glucose, amino acids, most water and salts by active transport and osmosis — many microvilli and mitochondria); loop of Henle (establishes the medullary osmotic gradient — descending limb permeable to water; ascending limb impermeable to water but actively transports NaCl out — creates countercurrent multiplier); distal convoluted tubule (DCT — fine-tuning of pH and ion balance); collecting duct (permeability regulated by ADH — water reabsorption varies with body water status).",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p2::4(a)": "Diabetes mellitus: Type 1 — autoimmune destruction of β cells in islets of Langerhans → absolute insulin deficiency → uncontrolled hyperglycaemia → ketoacidosis. Treated with exogenous insulin (injections or pump). Type 2 — insulin resistance (cells do not respond to insulin) and relative insulin deficiency → hyperglycaemia. Risk factors: obesity, inactivity, age. Managed by diet, exercise, metformin (reduces hepatic glucose output), GLP-1 agonists, SGLT2 inhibitors, or insulin. Consequences of uncontrolled hyperglycaemia: damage to blood vessels → retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, cardiovascular disease.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p3::1(a)": "Gel electrophoresis of DNA: pour agarose gel into a tray with wells; allow to set. Load DNA samples mixed with loading buffer (contains dye and glycerol) into wells. Load a DNA ladder in adjacent well. Run electrophoresis at constant voltage in TAE buffer. DNA migrates toward positive electrode — smaller fragments travel further. Stain with ethidium bromide or SYBR Green; visualise under UV. Compare band positions to ladder to determine fragment sizes.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p3::2(a)": "Experimental design for a valid investigation: identify the research question and variables. Independent variable (IV) — what you change; dependent variable (DV) — what you measure; controlled variables — everything else kept constant. Controls: negative control (no treatment — baseline); positive control (treatment with known outcome — confirms method works). Replication: repeat measurements to assess variability; calculate mean and standard deviation. Statistical tests: choose based on data type and distribution — t-test (compare two means, normally distributed), Mann-Whitney U (non-parametric, two groups), ANOVA (three or more groups), chi-squared (categorical data). State the null hypothesis; results are significant if p < 0.05 (less than 5% probability of result by chance).",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2023-p3::3(a)": "Conservation biology: in situ conservation (in natural habitat) — national parks, nature reserves, SSSI (Sites of Special Scientific Interest), marine protected areas. Ex situ conservation (outside natural habitat) — zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks, cryopreservation of gametes and embryos, tissue culture. Both approaches are needed: in situ maintains wild populations and ecosystems; ex situ provides a safety net for critically endangered species and material for reintroduction programmes. Biodiversity hotspots (areas with exceptional species richness and high endemism under threat) are prioritised for conservation investment. International agreements (CITES, Convention on Biological Diversity) coordinate global conservation efforts.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2022-p1::1(a)": "Meiosis produces genetically diverse haploid gametes. Sources of variation: (1) independent assortment — during meiosis I, homologous chromosome pairs align randomly at the metaphase plate; each cell receives a random mix of maternal and paternal chromosomes, producing 2²³ possible combinations in humans; (2) crossing over — during prophase I, non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes exchange segments at chiasmata, creating new combinations of alleles on each chromosome (recombinant chromosomes); (3) random fertilisation — any sperm can fertilise any egg, creating further variation.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2022-p1::2(a)": "DNA profiling (genetic fingerprinting): (1) Extract DNA from the sample. (2) Amplify variable number tandem repeat (VNTR) regions or short tandem repeats (STRs) using PCR with specific primers. (3) Separate fragments by gel electrophoresis. (4) Compare the banding pattern between individuals — each person has a unique pattern (except identical twins). Applications: forensic science (linking suspects to crime scenes), paternity testing, identification of disaster victims, population genetics studies. The probability of two unrelated individuals having the same profile is extremely small (1 in billions), making DNA profiling highly reliable.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2022-p1::3(a)": "Transgenic organisms contain foreign DNA (from another species) stably integrated into their genome. They are produced by inserting a gene of interest into a fertilised egg or embryo using microinjection, electroporation, or viral vectors. Examples: GM crops (herbicide-resistant soy, insect-resistant Bt cotton, golden rice with β-carotene); transgenic mice (for biomedical research — oncogene models, knockout mice); sheep producing human α1-antitrypsin in their milk. Benefits: increased yield, reduced pesticide use, pharmaceutical production. Risks: unintended effects on non-target organisms, horizontal gene transfer to wild relatives, ethical concerns about 'playing God', potential allergenicity of novel proteins.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2022-p1::4(a)": "Genetic diseases can result from point mutations (single base substitution), frameshift mutations (insertion or deletion of bases), chromosomal mutations (changes in chromosome number or structure), or triplet repeat expansions (e.g. Huntington's disease). Genetically inherited diseases follow predictable inheritance patterns. Autosomal dominant: one copy of the mutant allele causes the disease (e.g. Huntington's, Marfan syndrome). Autosomal recessive: two copies needed (e.g. cystic fibrosis, phenylketonuria). X-linked recessive: gene on X chromosome — males (XY) are affected with one copy; females need two copies (e.g. haemophilia, Duchenne muscular dystrophy). Genetic screening, counselling, and therapy options are available for many conditions.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2022-p2::1(a)": "The immune response has two arms. The innate immune response is non-specific and immediate: phagocytes (neutrophils, macrophages) engulf pathogens by phagocytosis — lysosomes fuse with phagosomes, hydrolyzing the pathogen. Macrophages act as antigen-presenting cells (APCs), displaying antigens on MHC class II molecules. The adaptive immune response is specific and slower: T helper cells (with complementary T cell receptors) bind to APC-presented antigens and become activated; they release cytokines stimulating B cell activation and T cytotoxic cell proliferation. B cells differentiate into plasma cells (producing specific antibodies) and memory B cells. Antibodies neutralise pathogens, agglutinate them, or opsonise them for phagocytosis.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2022-p2::2(a)": "The immune system responds to pathogens through innate and adaptive immunity. Innate: immediate, non-specific — physical barriers (skin, mucus), phagocytes, complement, inflammation. Adaptive: specific, slower — T lymphocytes (T helper, cytotoxic T cells) and B lymphocytes (plasma cells → antibodies, memory cells). Antibodies have a specific antigen-binding site (Fab region) complementary to the antigen — they neutralise pathogens, opsonise them for phagocytosis, or activate complement. Vaccination stimulates the adaptive immune response without causing disease, creating immunological memory. Passive immunity: pre-formed antibodies given directly (e.g. anti-venom, maternal antibodies via placenta/breast milk) — immediate but temporary.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2022-p2::3(a)": "Aerobic respiration: glycolysis (cytoplasm) — glucose → 2 pyruvate + net 2 ATP + 2 NADH. Link reaction (mitochondrial matrix) — pyruvate → acetyl-CoA + CO₂ + NADH. Krebs cycle (matrix) — acetyl-CoA + oxaloacetate → 4 CO₂ + 3 NADH + 1 FADH₂ + 1 ATP (per acetyl-CoA, so × 2 per glucose). Oxidative phosphorylation (inner mitochondrial membrane) — NADH and FADH₂ donate electrons to the electron transport chain; protons are pumped into the intermembrane space; electrochemical gradient drives protons back through ATP synthase (chemiosmosis) → ~32–34 ATP; O₂ is the final electron acceptor, combining with H⁺ and electrons to form H₂O. Total yield: ~36–38 ATP per glucose.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2022-p2::4(a)": "The electron transport chain (ETC) on the inner mitochondrial membrane: NADH donates 2 electrons to Complex I (NADH dehydrogenase); FADH₂ donates to Complex II (succinate dehydrogenase). Electrons pass sequentially through Complexes I → III → IV (and II → III → IV via ubiquinone/coenzyme Q). At each step, electron carriers are alternately reduced and oxidised, releasing free energy. This energy is used by Complexes I, III, and IV to pump protons (H⁺) from the matrix into the intermembrane space, creating an electrochemical gradient (proton motive force). Protons flow back through ATP synthase (Complex V) — this drives rotation of the rotor, powering ATP synthesis (chemiosmosis). O₂ accepts electrons and H⁺ at Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase) to form water.",
"bio-edxb-9bi0-2022-p3::1(a)": "Evaluating experimental data: consider sample size (larger = more reliable); whether a control was included; identify potential sources of error (systematic error, random error); consider whether results are repeatable and reproducible. Apply an appropriate statistical test to determine whether differences are significant (P < 0.05 means less than 5% probability the difference is due to chance). Consider whether the method measures what it claims to (validity) and whether results can be generalised (external validity).",

// ─── OCR A Biology H420 — 2024, 2023, 2022 ────────────────────────────────

// 2024 Paper 1 — Biological processes
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p1::1(a)": "The phospholipid bilayer is the basic structure of the cell-surface membrane. Each phospholipid has a hydrophilic phosphate head (facing the aqueous environments inside and outside the cell) and two hydrophobic fatty acid tails (facing inward, away from water). This arrangement creates a selectively permeable barrier. Cholesterol molecules interspersed between phospholipids reduce fluidity and permeability. Proteins embedded in the bilayer act as channels, carriers, receptors, and enzymes. Glycoproteins and glycolipids on the outer surface act as cell-surface receptors and recognition sites (e.g. for cell signalling, the immune system, and cell adhesion).",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p1::1(b)": "The cell cycle is regulated by cyclins and cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs). Cyclins accumulate at specific phases; binding of a cyclin to its CDK activates the kinase, which phosphorylates target proteins to advance the cycle. Key checkpoints: G1/S checkpoint (checks DNA integrity and cell size before committing to replication); G2/M checkpoint (checks for complete DNA replication before mitosis); spindle assembly checkpoint (all chromosomes must attach to spindle fibres before anaphase begins). Tumour suppressor genes (p53, Rb) halt the cycle if damage is detected; proto-oncogenes promote progression. Mutations in these regulators can lead to uncontrolled cell division (cancer).",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p1::1(c)": "Transport across membranes: simple diffusion — small, non-polar molecules (O₂, CO₂, ethanol, steroids) dissolve in the phospholipid bilayer and diffuse down their concentration gradient; no protein required. Facilitated diffusion — large polar molecules or ions (glucose, amino acids, ions) cross through specific channel proteins (ion channels — gated or ungated) or carrier proteins (conformational change to transport molecule); still down the concentration gradient, no energy required. Active transport — molecules moved against their concentration gradient using ATP energy; requires carrier proteins (pumps) such as Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase. Co-transport (secondary active transport) — uses the Na⁺ gradient established by the pump to drive co-transport of another molecule (e.g. glucose–Na⁺ co-transporter in gut epithelium).",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p1::2(a)": "Enzymes lower activation energy by forming enzyme-substrate complexes at the active site. The complementary shape of the active site to the substrate (lock and key) or the conformational change upon substrate binding (induced fit) positions reactive groups precisely, straining bonds and facilitating the reaction. Competitive inhibitors have a similar shape to the substrate and compete for the active site — increasing substrate concentration can overcome inhibition. Non-competitive inhibitors bind to an allosteric site, changing the shape of the active site so the substrate can no longer bind — inhibition cannot be overcome by increasing substrate concentration.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p1::3(a)": "Gas exchange in mammals: O₂ diffuses from alveolar air into alveolar epithelial cells, then into blood plasma and red blood cells where it binds to haemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin. CO₂ diffuses from red blood cells (where it is produced in the Krebs cycle) into the alveoli. The large surface area of alveoli, thin walls (one cell thick), moist surface, and rich capillary network maintain steep concentration gradients for efficient gas exchange. Ventilation constantly replenishes the air in the alveoli (tidal volume + dead space), maintaining the gradient.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p1::2(b)": "Respiration: glycolysis in the cytoplasm produces pyruvate; the link reaction in the mitochondrial matrix converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA releasing CO₂ and NADH; the Krebs cycle produces ATP, NADH, FADH₂, and CO₂; oxidative phosphorylation on the inner mitochondrial membrane uses the electron transport chain and ATP synthase (chemiosmosis) to generate ~34 ATP. In anaerobic conditions, pyruvate is converted to lactate (animals) or ethanol and CO₂ (yeast) to regenerate NAD for glycolysis. Photosynthesis: light-dependent reactions on thylakoid membranes produce ATP and NADPH and release O₂ (from photolysis of water); light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) in the stroma use ATP and NADPH to fix CO₂ into GP and then G3P (triose phosphate), which is used to make glucose.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p1::3(b)": "Haemoglobin and oxygen transport: oxyhaemoglobin forms in the lungs where pO₂ is high (100 mmHg); oxyhaemoglobin dissociates in tissues where pO₂ is low (40 mmHg). The sigmoidal oxygen dissociation curve reflects cooperative binding — binding of the first O₂ increases affinity for subsequent molecules. The Bohr effect: raised CO₂ (from active tissues) → raised [H⁺] → haemoglobin has lower O₂ affinity → more O₂ released in tissues. Myoglobin has a hyperbolic dissociation curve with higher O₂ affinity than haemoglobin — acts as an O₂ store in muscle, releasing O₂ only at very low pO₂ during intense exercise. CO₂ is transported as bicarbonate ions (HCO₃⁻) in plasma (70%), as carbaminohaemoglobin (23%), and dissolved in plasma (7%).",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p2::1(a)": "Natural selection: individuals in a population show variation (due to mutations and sexual reproduction). In a given environment, individuals with alleles that improve survival and reproduction are more likely to survive long enough to reproduce and pass on those alleles (differential reproductive success). Over generations, the frequency of advantageous alleles increases in the population. Speciation occurs when populations become reproductively isolated — allele frequencies diverge sufficiently that they can no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p2::1(b)": "Variation and evolution: within a population, genetic variation arises from mutations (random base changes), independent assortment (random alignment of bivalents in meiosis I producing 2²³ combinations in humans), crossing over (exchange of segments between non-sister chromatids at chiasmata during prophase I), and random fertilisation. Environmental factors add phenotypic variation (not heritable). Natural selection acts on phenotypic variation — individuals better suited to their environment survive longer and reproduce more. Directional selection shifts the mean phenotype; stabilising selection reduces variation; disruptive selection favours extreme phenotypes. Genetic drift (random allele frequency changes) is significant in small populations.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p2::2(a)": "Classification and taxonomy: living organisms are classified using a hierarchical system — Domain, Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species (mnemonic: Dear King Philip Came Over For Good Soup). The biological species concept defines a species as a group of organisms capable of interbreeding to produce fertile offspring. Phylogenetics uses evolutionary relationships (based on DNA/protein sequence homology, shared derived characters — synapomorphies) to construct phylogenetic trees. Molecular phylogenetics compares gene sequences; BLAST searches find homologous sequences in databases. Three-domain system (Woese): Bacteria, Archaea (both prokaryotes), Eukarya — based on ribosomal RNA sequence analysis.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p2::3(a)": "Ecosystems and energy flow: energy enters ecosystems via photosynthesis (primary production). GPP = gross primary production (total energy fixed); NPP = GPP − R (energy available to consumers). Only ~10% of energy passes between trophic levels. The rest is lost in: respiration (heat); egestion (faeces); excretion (urine). Ecological efficiency = (production at level n+1 ÷ production at level n) × 100%. Nutrient cycles (nitrogen, carbon) involve decomposers returning nutrients from dead organic matter to the soil/atmosphere. Nitrogen cycle: nitrogen fixation (N₂ → NH₃, by Rhizobium in root nodules or free-living Azotobacter); nitrification (NH₃ → NO₂⁻ → NO₃⁻, by nitrifying bacteria); assimilation (plants absorb NO₃⁻); ammonification (decomposers convert organic N → NH₃); denitrification (NO₃⁻ → N₂, by denitrifying bacteria in anaerobic soils).",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p3::1(a)": "Experimental design for an enzyme investigation: independent variable — substrate concentration (or pH, temperature). Dependent variable — rate of reaction (e.g. time for colour change, or gas volume collected). Control variables — enzyme concentration, temperature, pH, volume of reactants. Controls — a blank (no enzyme, or boiled enzyme) to account for spontaneous reaction. Replicates — at least three per condition. Statistical analysis — use a t-test or Mann-Whitney U test to compare means; state the null hypothesis (no significant difference between conditions).",

// Paper 2 — Biological diversity
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p3::2(a)": "Evaluating experimental evidence: consider sample size (larger → more reliable); appropriate controls (positive and negative); whether results are repeatable (same researcher, same conditions) and reproducible (different researchers, different conditions); sources of systematic error (procedural bias, instrument calibration) and random error (natural variability); appropriate statistical analysis; peer review; potential confounding variables. Correlation ≠ causation — a correlational study identifies associations but cannot establish cause; only experimental studies with randomised controls establish causation.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2024-p3::3(a)": "Gene expression can be controlled at transcriptional level (transcription factors bind enhancers or silencers to promote or inhibit RNA polymerase binding); post-transcriptional (alternative splicing of pre-mRNA); translational (mRNA stability, miRNA degradation of mRNA); post-translational (protein modification — glycosylation, phosphorylation; ubiquitination → proteasomal degradation). Epigenetic control: DNA methylation (adds methyl groups to cytosine in CpG islands — silences gene expression); histone modification (acetylation of histones relaxes chromatin → increases expression; deacetylation condenses chromatin → reduces expression). These are heritable changes in gene expression without changes to DNA sequence.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2023-p1::1(a)": "Mitosis produces two genetically identical daughter cells from one parent cell. During prophase, chromosomes condense and the nuclear envelope breaks down; during metaphase, chromosomes align at the metaphase plate; during anaphase, centromeres split and sister chromatids are pulled to opposite poles by spindle fibres; during telophase, nuclear envelopes reform around each set of chromosomes. Cytokinesis then divides the cytoplasm. Mitosis is used for growth, repair, and asexual reproduction.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2023-p1::2(a)": "DNA replication is semi-conservative: each strand serves as a template. Key enzymes: helicase unwinds the double helix at the replication fork; primase synthesises a short RNA primer to provide a 3'-OH for DNA polymerase; DNA polymerase III (prokaryotes) / DNA polymerase δ and ε (eukaryotes) synthesises the new strand in the 5'→3' direction; DNA polymerase I removes RNA primers and fills the gaps; DNA ligase seals the nicks (joins Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand). The leading strand is synthesised continuously; the lagging strand is synthesised discontinuously as Okazaki fragments (in the 5'→3' direction opposite to the fork movement). Meselson and Stahl experiment (1958) using ¹⁵N/¹⁴N confirmed semi-conservative replication by density-gradient centrifugation.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2023-p1::3(a)": "Proteins have four levels of structure: primary — sequence of amino acids joined by peptide bonds (determined by gene). Secondary — regular local folding: α-helix (H bonds between C=O and N–H every 4 residues) or β-pleated sheet (H bonds between parallel or antiparallel strands). Tertiary — overall 3D folding of the polypeptide, stabilised by: hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, disulfide bridges (between cysteine residues), and hydrophobic interactions between non-polar R groups. Quaternary — association of two or more polypeptide subunits (e.g. haemoglobin: 4 subunits; collagen: 3 polypeptide chains). The unique tertiary structure of an enzyme's active site determines its specificity for its substrate.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2023-p2::1(a)": "Biodiversity can be measured at three levels: genetic diversity (variation in allele frequencies within a species), species diversity (number and relative abundance of species in an area — measured by indices such as Simpson's), and ecosystem diversity (variety of habitats and communities). Biodiversity is maintained by protecting habitats (nature reserves, marine protected areas), sustainable management, captive breeding and reintroduction programmes, seed banks, and international conservation agreements (e.g. the Convention on Biological Diversity). Human activities that threaten biodiversity include habitat destruction, pollution, invasive species, overexploitation, and climate change.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2023-p2::2(a)": "Genetic diversity and conservation: genetic diversity is the variety of alleles in a population. Measured by: number of polymorphic loci (loci with more than one allele), heterozygosity index, allele richness. Low genetic diversity makes a population vulnerable to: novel pathogens (no resistant individuals), environmental change (no adaptive variants), and inbreeding depression (expression of deleterious recessive alleles). Conservation measures to maintain genetic diversity: captive breeding programmes (managed pedigrees to avoid inbreeding); reintroduction programmes; connecting fragmented habitats (wildlife corridors) to enable gene flow; seed banks preserving genetic diversity of crop species and wild plants.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2023-p2::3(a)": "Succession is the sequential change in species composition of a community over time. Primary succession begins on bare substrate (e.g. bare rock after volcanic eruption): pioneer species (e.g. lichens) colonise and modify the substrate, making it habitable for subsequent species. Each seral stage changes abiotic conditions (adding organic matter, increasing soil depth, altering pH) enabling the next community. Secondary succession begins on land where soil already exists (e.g. after fire or flooding): faster than primary because soil and seed bank remain. Climax community is the final stable state for a given climate — determined by regional climate (temperate woodland in the UK). Species diversity increases during succession as habitat complexity increases, providing more niches.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2023-p3::1(a)": "In a synoptic question linking topics: homeostasis maintains internal conditions within narrow limits via negative feedback. The hypothalamus monitors blood temperature and osmolarity; effectors (sweat glands, muscles, blood vessels) correct deviations. The endocrine system (hormones) coordinates longer-term responses while the nervous system provides rapid, localised responses. Both systems converge on target cells with specific receptors.",

// Paper 3 — Unified biology
"bio-ocra-h420-2023-p3::2(a)": "Practical skills — microscopy and cell biology: light microscopy can resolve structures down to ~200 nm; electron microscopy (TEM and SEM) resolves to ~0.2 nm. To observe mitosis: prepare a root tip squash — cut root tip, place in HCl (60 °C, 10 min) to soften, stain with toluidine blue or aceto-orcein to stain chromosomes, squash between two slides, observe at ×400. To calculate the mitotic index = (number of cells in mitosis ÷ total cells counted) × 100%. Electron micrographs allow identification of ultrastructure (mitochondria cristae, rough ER, nuclear pores). Graticule and stage micrometer are used to measure actual sizes: actual size = measured size ÷ magnification.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2023-p3::3(a)": "Homeostasis: maintaining a constant internal environment via negative feedback. Blood glucose regulation: after a meal (glucose rises) — pancreatic β cells secrete insulin → liver hepatocytes convert glucose to glycogen (glycogenesis) and increase glucose uptake → blood glucose falls. When blood glucose falls → α cells secrete glucagon → liver converts glycogen back to glucose (glycogenolysis) and makes glucose from non-carbohydrate sources (gluconeogenesis) → blood glucose rises. In Type 1 diabetes, β cells are destroyed (autoimmune) — insulin cannot be produced → blood glucose cannot be controlled — treated with insulin injections. Blood calcium: parathyroid hormone (PTH) released when Ca²⁺ falls → osteoclasts resorb bone, kidney reabsorbs more Ca²⁺, activates vitamin D → Ca²⁺ rises. Calcitonin (from thyroid) has opposite effect.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2022-p1::1(a)": "In eukaryotic cells, transcription occurs in the nucleus. RNA polymerase binds to the promoter, unwinds the DNA, and synthesises a pre-mRNA strand complementary to the template strand. The pre-mRNA undergoes processing: a 5' cap and poly-A tail are added (protecting the mRNA from degradation and facilitating ribosome binding), and introns are removed by spliceosomes (leaving exons joined to form mature mRNA). The mature mRNA is exported to the cytoplasm for translation at ribosomes.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2022-p1::2(a)": "Immune system overview: Physical and chemical barriers (skin, stomach acid, mucus, lysozyme) form the first line of defence. Non-specific innate immune response: inflammation (vasodilation, increased permeability, phagocyte recruitment), phagocytosis (neutrophils, macrophages), complement system, natural killer cells. Specific adaptive response: T helper cells (coordinate response via cytokines), cytotoxic T cells (kill infected cells presenting antigen on MHC class I), B cells (plasma cells → antibodies; memory B cells). Antibody structure: Y-shaped glycoprotein, 4 polypeptides (2 heavy + 2 light), linked by disulfide bonds. Fab region — variable domains — antigen-binding site; Fc region — constant domain — complement binding/Fc receptor binding. Monoclonal antibodies: produced by fusing B cells with myeloma cells (hybridomas); used in pregnancy tests, HIV tests, cancer targeting (Herceptin).",
"bio-ocra-h420-2022-p1::3(a)": "Nervous coordination: the central nervous system (CNS) consists of the brain and spinal cord. The brain regions: cerebrum (conscious thought, memory, language, voluntary movement); cerebellum (balance, coordination, fine motor control); medulla oblongata (breathing, heart rate, blood pressure — autonomic control); hypothalamus (thermoregulation, osmoregulation, circadian rhythms, pituitary control). Reflex arcs: stimulus → receptor → sensory neurone → relay neurone (spinal cord) → motor neurone → effector → response. Reflexes are involuntary, faster than conscious response, protective. Spinal reflexes (withdrawal, knee jerk) bypass the brain for speed.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2022-p2::1(a)": "Ecological sampling techniques: transects (line or belt) used to study changes in species distribution across an environmental gradient (e.g. from sea to land); quadrats used to estimate abundance and frequency within an area — placed randomly using random number tables; mark-release-recapture for mobile animals. Data collected include species present, percentage cover or ACFOR scale, and numbers. Abiotic factors (light intensity, pH, soil moisture) are measured alongside to explain distribution patterns.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2022-p2::2(a)": "Population ecology: a population is all individuals of a species in a given area at a given time. Population size is estimated by mark-release-recapture (Lincoln-Petersen method for mobile animals): N = (M × S) ÷ R, where M = number marked and released, S = total in second sample, R = recaptured and marked. Assumptions: marks do not affect survival; marks are not lost; population is closed (no births, deaths, emigration, immigration); marked individuals mix randomly. For plants/immobile animals: random quadrats with appropriate size — estimate of percentage cover, frequency, or density. Population growth is limited by density-dependent factors (competition, disease, predation) and density-independent factors (temperature, drought, floods).",
"bio-ocra-h420-2022-p2::3(a)": "Plant hormones (plant growth substances): auxin (IAA) — promotes cell elongation in shoots (phototropism, gravitropism), inhibits lateral bud growth (apical dominance), promotes root initiation. Gibberellins — stimulate stem elongation, seed germination (mobilise food reserves by activating α-amylase in aleurone cells), fruit development. Cytokinins — promote cell division, delay senescence; antagonise auxin in lateral buds. Abscisic acid (ABA) — promotes dormancy, stomatal closure (drought response). Ethene — promotes fruit ripening, leaf abscission, senescence. Applied uses: rooting powder (auxin to promote roots on cuttings); gibberellin to increase fruit size in seedless grapes; ethene to coordinate fruit ripening for transport.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2022-p3::1(a)": "Photosynthesis and respiration are linked through ATP and reduced coenzymes. In the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis, light energy is used to produce ATP and NADPH; these drive the Calvin cycle (light-independent) to fix CO₂ into triose phosphate and glucose. In aerobic respiration, glucose is oxidised through glycolysis, the link reaction, the Krebs cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation to produce ATP, CO₂, and water. The CO₂ from respiration can be fixed again in photosynthesis, linking the two processes in the carbon cycle.",

// ─── OCR A 2022 P3 extra questions ──────────────────────────────────────────
"bio-ocra-h420-2022-p3::2(a)": "Investigating enzyme-controlled reactions: measure product formation or substrate consumption over time (e.g. O₂ produced by catalase, starch remaining after amylase). Control variables: temperature (water bath), pH (buffer), enzyme concentration, substrate concentration. Determine Vmax and Km from a rate vs [substrate] curve using Lineweaver-Burk double reciprocal plots; Km = substrate concentration at half Vmax (measure of enzyme-substrate affinity). Competitive inhibition: Vmax unchanged, Km increases. Non-competitive inhibition: Vmax decreases, Km unchanged. These can be distinguished by the effect on the rate vs [substrate] curve.",
"bio-ocra-h420-2022-p3::3(a)": "Biotechnology applications: fermentation — microorganisms (bacteria, yeast, fungi) grown in fermenters to produce useful products (insulin, antibiotics, enzymes, ethanol, citric acid). Conditions optimised: temperature (30–37 °C for most microbes), pH, O₂ supply, nutrient concentration, sterility. Continuous culture: nutrients continuously added and product removed — maintains log-phase growth. Batch culture: fixed volume of medium; organism grows through all phases; harvested at end. Immobilised enzymes: enzyme attached to inert support — recoverable, thermostable, product-free of enzyme contamination. Used in: lactose-free milk production (lactase), glucose biosensors (glucose oxidase), high-fructose corn syrup (glucose isomerase).",

// ─── OCR B (Advancing Biology) H422 — 2024, 2023, 2022 ─────────────────

// 2024 papers
"bio-ocrb-h422-2024-p1::1(a)": "Cells are the basic structural and functional unit of life. All cells are surrounded by a cell-surface membrane (phospholipid bilayer), contain DNA (as the genetic material), and use ribosomes for protein synthesis. Eukaryotic cells (animals, plants, fungi) have a membrane-bound nucleus containing linear chromosomes, and membrane-bound organelles (mitochondria, ER, Golgi, lysosomes). Prokaryotic cells (bacteria) have no nucleus — DNA is circular and free in the cytoplasm; they have 70S ribosomes, a cell wall of peptidoglycan, and may have plasmids, a capsule, and flagella.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2024-p1::2(a)": "The structure of DNA: deoxyribonucleic acid is a double-stranded polynucleotide. Each nucleotide consists of a deoxyribose sugar, a phosphate group, and one of four bases (A, T, C, G). Adjacent nucleotides are joined by phosphodiester bonds (3'→5') to form the backbone. The two antiparallel strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs: A–T (2 H bonds), G–C (3 H bonds). The double helix is right-handed with a major and minor groove. Chargaff's rules: [A] = [T] and [G] = [C] in any double-stranded DNA. The ratio of A+T to G+C varies between species and is used as a measure of GC content.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2024-p1::3(a)": "Meiosis produces haploid gametes with genetic variation. Two divisions: Meiosis I (reductive) — homologous chromosomes separate; prophase I — chromosomes condense, bivalents form, crossing over occurs at chiasmata; metaphase I — bivalents align at metaphase plate (random orientation = independent assortment); anaphase I — homologous chromosomes separate; telophase I — two haploid cells form (each with replicated chromosomes). Meiosis II (equational) — similar to mitosis; sister chromatids separate → 4 haploid cells. Sources of genetic variation: crossing over (new allele combinations on chromosomes); independent assortment (random arrangement of bivalents → 2²³ possible combinations in humans); random fertilisation.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2024-p2::1(a)": "Evolution by natural selection: within a population there is genetic variation (due to mutation and sexual reproduction). In a particular environment, individuals with alleles that improve survival and/or reproduction have greater reproductive fitness. They are more likely to survive, reproduce, and pass their alleles to offspring. Over generations, the frequency of advantageous alleles increases. Accumulated genetic changes can lead to speciation if populations become reproductively isolated.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2024-p2::2(a)": "Speciation: allopatric speciation — populations become geographically isolated (by mountain ranges, oceans, rivers). Separate populations accumulate different mutations and experience different selection pressures. Over time, allele frequencies diverge (genetic drift + natural selection). When populations come back into contact, they can no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring — reproductive isolation is complete, two species formed. Sympatric speciation — occurs without geographic isolation (e.g. polyploidy in plants — chromosome duplication events produce individuals that can only breed with others of the same ploidy level; can occur rapidly). Reproductive isolating mechanisms: pre-zygotic (habitat isolation, temporal isolation, behavioural isolation, mechanical isolation, gametic incompatibility) and post-zygotic (hybrid inviability, hybrid sterility, hybrid breakdown).",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2024-p2::3(a)": "Population genetics and Hardy-Weinberg: in an idealised population with no evolution, allele and genotype frequencies remain constant. Conditions: no mutation; no natural selection; large population size; no gene flow; random mating. Genotype frequencies: p² (AA) + 2pq (Aa) + q² (aa) = 1, where p = frequency of dominant allele, q = frequency of recessive allele, p + q = 1. If q² is known (homozygous recessive individuals), q = √q² and p = 1 − q; heterozygote frequency = 2pq. Deviations from HWE indicate natural selection, non-random mating, genetic drift, or gene flow — all indicators that evolution is occurring.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2024-p3::1(a)": "Genetic engineering (recombinant DNA technology): a gene of interest is isolated using restriction endonucleases that cut at specific palindromic sequences, producing complementary sticky ends. The same enzyme cuts a vector (e.g. a bacterial plasmid). The gene is inserted into the vector by complementary base pairing of sticky ends and sealed by DNA ligase. The recombinant plasmid is introduced into a host cell (e.g. bacteria by transformation); cells that have taken up the vector are identified using selectable markers (antibiotic resistance or reporter genes). The host cells express the human gene, producing the protein of interest (e.g. insulin).",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2024-p3::2(a)": "Writing up a scientific investigation: introduction should place the investigation in context, citing relevant literature and stating a clear hypothesis. Methods: write in the past tense and passive voice; give enough detail for another scientist to replicate (exact concentrations, temperatures, volumes, timing). Results: present data in labelled tables, graphs with error bars; report means and standard deviations. Discussion: interpret results in context of the hypothesis and existing literature; discuss sources of error; suggest improvements and extensions. Conclusion: state whether the hypothesis is supported; use statistical analysis to justify (p-value, confidence intervals). Evaluation: comment on reliability (replication), validity (measuring what you intend to measure), and accuracy of measurements.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2024-p3::3(a)": "CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing: a guide RNA (gRNA) complementary to the target DNA sequence is synthesised and bound to Cas9 endonuclease. The gRNA-Cas9 complex binds to the target DNA and Cas9 makes a double-strand break at the precise target location. The cell repairs the break by: (1) non-homologous end joining (NHEJ) — error-prone, often introduces insertions/deletions (indels) that disrupt the gene (gene knockout); or (2) homology-directed repair (HDR) — if a repair template is provided, the target sequence can be replaced with the desired sequence (gene correction or insertion). Applications: correcting disease-causing mutations, producing disease-resistant crops, gene drive technology for controlling insect populations.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2023-p1::1(a)": "Protein synthesis requires transcription and translation. The gene's template strand is transcribed by RNA polymerase (nucleotides added in 5'→3' direction, complementary to the template). The mRNA carries codons (three-base sequences); each codon is recognised by a tRNA anticodon carrying a specific amino acid. Peptide bonds form between adjacent amino acids as the ribosome moves along the mRNA from the start codon (AUG) to the stop codon.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2023-p1::2(a)": "Membrane fluidity and function: the fluid mosaic model (Singer and Nicolson, 1972) describes the plasma membrane as a phospholipid bilayer (fluid) with proteins embedded in it (mosaic). Cholesterol molecules are interspersed among the phospholipids — they reduce fluidity at high temperatures (by restricting phospholipid movement) and prevent solidification at low temperatures (by maintaining gaps between phospholipid tails). Temperature increases membrane fluidity — above a certain threshold, the membrane becomes too fluid, losing integrity. Unsaturated fatty acid tails (with cis double bonds causing 'kinks') increase fluidity by preventing tight packing; saturated tails pack closely together (lower fluidity).",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2023-p1::3(a)": "Transcription and translation: transcription — RNA polymerase binds to the promoter on the template strand; nucleotides assemble by complementary base pairing (U instead of T); phosphodiester bonds form; pre-mRNA is processed (5' cap, poly-A tail, splicing out of introns by spliceosomes) → mature mRNA leaves the nucleus. Translation — mRNA binds to the small ribosomal subunit; tRNA with anticodon complementary to the start codon (AUG) enters the P site carrying methionine; elongation: new tRNA enters the A site; peptidyl transferase forms a peptide bond; ribosome translocates; aminoacyl-tRNA cycle continues until a stop codon (UAA, UAG, UGA) is reached; polypeptide is released. Multiple ribosomes can translate one mRNA simultaneously (polysome).",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2023-p2::1(a)": "Photosynthesis occurs in chloroplasts. The light-dependent reactions on thylakoid membranes produce ATP and NADPH (from photolysis of water and the electron transport chain). These products drive the Calvin cycle in the stroma: CO₂ is fixed by Rubisco onto RuBP, producing GP; GP is reduced (using ATP and NADPH) to triose phosphate; TP is used to make glucose and to regenerate RuBP.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2023-p2::2(a)": "Photosynthesis: light-dependent reactions on the thylakoid membranes of chloroplasts. Photosystem II (P680) absorbs light → photoionisation — excited electrons leave chlorophyll → photolysis of water releases O₂, H⁺, and electrons to replace lost electrons. Electrons pass along electron carriers (plastoquinone → cytochrome b6f complex → plastocyanin), releasing energy used to pump H⁺ into the thylakoid lumen — proton gradient drives ATP synthase (photophosphorylation). Photosystem I (P700) absorbs light → reduces NADP to NADPH (via ferredoxin and NADP reductase). Light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle): CO₂ + RuBP → GP (catalysed by Rubisco) → GP reduced to G3P using ATP and NADPH → G3P used to make glucose and to regenerate RuBP.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2023-p2::3(a)": "Factors affecting enzyme activity: temperature — increases reaction rate (more kinetic energy, more frequent collisions) up to optimum; above optimum, high temperature denatures enzyme (breaks H bonds and ionic bonds in tertiary structure, distorting the active site). pH — each enzyme has an optimum pH; extremes of pH protonate or deprotonate R groups, disrupting ionic bonds and H bonds → denaturation. Substrate concentration — rate increases with [S] until all active sites are occupied (Vmax — zero-order kinetics); at low [S], rate is first-order (proportional to [S]). Enzyme concentration — proportional increase in rate if substrate is in excess. Inhibitors: competitive (reversible, increase apparent Km); non-competitive (reduce Vmax, unchanged Km).",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2023-p3::1(a)": "Respiration produces ATP for cellular work. Glycolysis (cytoplasm): glucose → 2 pyruvate + 2 ATP + 2 NADH. Link reaction (mitochondrial matrix): pyruvate → acetyl CoA + CO₂ + NADH. Krebs cycle (matrix): acetyl CoA → CO₂ + NADH + FADH₂ + 1 ATP. Oxidative phosphorylation (inner mitochondrial membrane): NADH and FADH₂ donate electrons to the ETC; protons pumped into intermembrane space create a gradient; ATP synthase uses the gradient (chemiosmosis) to produce ATP; O₂ is the final electron acceptor, forming water.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2023-p3::2(a)": "Mathematical skills in biology: magnification = image size ÷ actual size; actual size = image size ÷ magnification. Units: convert between µm (10⁻⁶ m) and mm (10⁻³ m) — 1 mm = 1000 µm. Surface area to volume ratio: as organisms increase in size, SA:V decreases → diffusion alone insufficient; need specialised exchange surfaces and transport systems. Percentage change = [(new − old) ÷ old] × 100%. Standard deviation (SD) measures spread of data — 68% of values fall within 1 SD of the mean; 95% within 2 SD. t-test formula: t = (mean₁ − mean₂) ÷ (SE of difference); if t > critical value at p = 0.05 → statistically significant difference. Standard error (SE) = SD ÷ √n — measures precision of the mean; shown as error bars on graphs.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2023-p3::3(a)": "Ecology practical techniques: estimating population density of plants — use random quadrats (random number table gives coordinates); record species present, abundance (number), percentage cover, or ACFOR scale; count for at least 10 quadrats in each area. For mobile animals — mark-release-recapture or kick sampling for aquatic invertebrates. Measuring abiotic factors: light intensity (light meter), soil pH (pH probe), soil moisture (soil probe), temperature (thermometer). Interpreting results: compare means using a t-test if two groups; use a Spearman's rank correlation if testing the relationship between two variables (e.g. light intensity and plant abundance); r_s = 1 − (6Σd²) ÷ (n(n²−1)).",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2022-p1::1(a)": "The cell cycle is divided into interphase (G1, S, G2) and the mitotic phase (M phase). G1: cell grows, proteins synthesised. S: DNA replicates semiconservatively. G2: organelles replicate, cell checks for DNA damage. M phase: mitosis (PMAT) followed by cytokinesis. Checkpoints (G1/S, G2/M, spindle checkpoint) ensure the cycle only proceeds if conditions are met. Cyclins and cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs) regulate progression through checkpoints. Uncontrolled passage through checkpoints leads to cancer.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2022-p1::2(a)": "Carbohydrates: monosaccharides (hexoses: glucose, galactose, fructose; pentoses: ribose, deoxyribose) are the monomers. Disaccharides form by condensation reactions (glycosidic bonds): maltose (glucose+glucose, α-1,4), sucrose (glucose+fructose), lactose (glucose+galactose). Polysaccharides: starch (amylose — unbranched, α-1,4 links; amylopectin — branched, α-1,4 and α-1,6 links) — energy storage in plants; glycogen (highly branched, α-1,4 and α-1,6) — energy storage in animals/fungi; cellulose (β-1,4 links, straight chains, H-bonded to adjacent chains forming microfibrils) — structural in plant cell walls. Reducing sugars (all monosaccharides, maltose, lactose) give a positive result with Benedict's reagent (brick-red precipitate). Non-reducing sugars (sucrose) must first be hydrolysed with HCl before testing.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2022-p1::3(a)": "Lipids: triglycerides — one glycerol + three fatty acids joined by ester bonds (condensation reactions releasing water). Saturated fatty acids have no C=C double bonds (straight chain, pack closely together, solid at room temperature — e.g. butter, lard); unsaturated have one or more cis C=C double bonds (kinked chain, cannot pack closely, liquid at room temperature — e.g. vegetable oil). Trans fats (hydrogenated unsaturated fats with trans double bonds) behave similarly to saturated fats; linked to CVD. Phospholipids: glycerol + two fatty acids + phosphate group; hydrophilic head, hydrophobic tails → self-assemble into bilayers in aqueous environments. Role in cell membranes. Cholesterol: important component of mammalian cell membranes (regulates fluidity), precursor of steroid hormones and bile salts.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2022-p2::1(a)": "Variation within a population is essential for natural selection. Genetic variation arises from mutations (random changes in DNA base sequence), independent assortment and crossing over during meiosis, and random fertilisation. Environmental factors can also cause phenotypic variation (not heritable). Selection acts on phenotype — individuals whose phenotype is best suited to the current environment are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing on their alleles. Directional selection shifts the mean phenotype; stabilising selection reduces variation around the mean; disruptive selection favours extreme phenotypes.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2022-p2::2(a)": "Water properties and their biological importance: (1) Dipole structure (δ+ H, δ− O) → hydrogen bonding between water molecules. (2) Cohesion — hydrogen bonds between water molecules allow water columns to be pulled up xylem (transpiration stream). (3) Adhesion — water adheres to hydrophilic xylem walls. (4) High specific heat capacity — water absorbs a lot of energy before its temperature rises → buffers against temperature changes in cells and aquatic environments. (5) High latent heat of vaporisation — evaporation of water (sweat) removes a large amount of heat → efficient cooling. (6) Solvent properties — polar water dissolves ionic and polar substances (glucose, amino acids, ions) for transport in blood and cytoplasm. (7) Ice less dense than water — ice floats, insulating aquatic habitats in winter. (8) Ionisation — water self-ionises slightly (H₂O ⇌ H⁺ + OH⁻), providing the H⁺ and OH⁻ ions involved in many biochemical reactions.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2022-p2::3(a)": "Animal behaviour and reproduction: the nervous system controls rapid responses; the endocrine system coordinates slower, longer-lasting responses. Innate (instinctive) behaviour is genetically determined and does not require learning — e.g. newly hatched turtles crawl toward the sea; reflexes. Learned behaviour results from experience — habituation (decreased response to repeated unrewarded stimulus), classical conditioning (Pavlov's dogs), operant conditioning (trial-and-error learning), insight learning (problem-solving). Sexual reproduction involves meiosis to produce haploid gametes and fertilisation to restore the diploid number. Advantages of sexual reproduction: genetic variation (via crossing over, independent assortment, random fertilisation) — allows adaptation to changing environments. Asexual reproduction (mitosis): rapid, efficient, all offspring genetically identical to parent — useful in stable environments.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2022-p3::2(a)": "Cloning and gene technology in agriculture: recombinant DNA technology involves isolating a gene of interest (using restriction enzymes), inserting it into a vector (plasmid or virus), transforming host cells (e.g. Agrobacterium tumefaciens for plants), and selecting transformed organisms. Transgenic crops (GM crops) can be engineered for herbicide resistance (e.g. Roundup Ready soya), pest resistance (Bt crops producing insecticidal protein), drought tolerance, or enhanced nutritional content (Golden Rice, vitamin A). Micropropagation (tissue culture) allows rapid, identical cloning of elite plant varieties from explants on sterile agar with growth hormones. Somatic cell nuclear transfer (SCNT) is used in animal cloning. Ethical considerations: biodiversity loss, gene flow to wild relatives, consumer safety, intellectual property.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2022-p3::3(a)": "Immunity and disease: natural immunity — innate (non-specific, immediate) includes skin, mucus, phagocytes, inflammation, fever. Adaptive (specific) immunity: (a) Humoral — B lymphocytes differentiate into plasma cells secreting specific antibodies; memory B cells provide long-term immunity. (b) Cell-mediated — T helper cells activate B cells and cytotoxic T cells; T cytotoxic cells destroy infected cells via perforins; T regulatory cells suppress immune response after infection cleared. Antigens are molecules (usually proteins/glycoproteins) that trigger an immune response; antibodies are complementary Y-shaped glycoproteins (immunoglobulins) with specific antigen-binding sites. Vaccination introduces antigens (attenuated pathogen, killed pathogen, toxoid, or antigen alone) to stimulate primary immune response and generate memory cells → faster, stronger secondary response on re-exposure. Herd immunity: if enough of the population is immune, transmission chains are broken, protecting non-immune individuals.",
"bio-ocrb-h422-2022-p3::1(a)": "Ecology investigates relationships between organisms and their environment. A habitat is the physical location where an organism lives; a niche includes all biotic and abiotic interactions. Food chains and food webs describe energy transfer between trophic levels (producer → primary consumer → secondary consumer). Energy is lost at each level (respiration, excretion, faeces), so typically only 10% transfers between trophic levels. Ecosystems are maintained by nutrient cycling (nitrogen cycle, carbon cycle) and energy flow from the sun.",

};
